window.GATE_BANK=window.GATE_BANK||{};window.GATE_BANK["databases"]=[{"id": "DB-U1-Q01", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In an entity–relationship diagram, a weak entity set is depicted by", "options": ["a single rectangle.", "a double rectangle.", "a single diamond.", "a double diamond."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> ER notation. A weak entity set has no key attribute of its own; its existence depends on an owner entity set, so it is drawn as a <b>double rectangle</b>. Its identifying relationship is drawn as a double diamond, its partial key is underlined with a dashed line, and its total participation in the identifying relationship is shown by a double line.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A weak entity set cannot be identified by its own attributes, only by (owner key + partial key).</li><li>The standard Chen notation distinguishes it with the double outline: double rectangle for the set, double diamond for the identifying relationship.</li><li>Hence the correct symbol for the weak entity set itself is the double rectangle.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — a double rectangle.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A single rectangle denotes a strong (regular) entity set.</li><li>(C) A diamond denotes a relationship set, not an entity set.</li><li>(D) A double diamond denotes the identifying relationship of a weak entity set.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the double rectangle (weak entity set) with the double diamond (identifying relationship). They are different symbols for different things and a question may ask for either.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> If the option mentions a diamond at all, it is describing a relationship, not an entity set.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fix the four ER symbols once: rectangle = entity set, double rectangle = weak entity set, diamond = relationship, double diamond = identifying relationship.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q02", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Entity set A has a many-to-one relationship R with entity set B. The participation of A in R is total and the participation of B in R is partial. Which one of the following is necessarily TRUE?", "options": ["Every entity of B is related to at least one entity of A.", "Every entity of A is related to exactly one entity of B.", "Every entity of B is related to exactly one entity of A.", "R must be merged into B because B's participation is partial."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Cardinality ratio plus participation constraint. “Many-to-one from A to B” means each entity of A is related to at most one entity of B, while one entity of B may be related to many entities of A. Total participation of A adds “at least one”, and the two bounds together give “exactly one”.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Many-to-one A → B: an A-entity can be linked to at most one B-entity.</li><li>Total participation of A: every A-entity is linked to at least one B-entity.</li><li>At most one and at least one give exactly one B-entity for every A-entity.</li><li>Partial participation of B only says some B-entities may have no A-entity at all; it constrains nothing about A.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — every entity of A is related to exactly one entity of B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) B's participation is partial, so a B-entity need not appear in R at all.</li><li>(C) Many-to-one allows many A-entities to share one B-entity, so B is not related to a unique A.</li><li>(D) A relationship is merged into an entity set only for a one-to-one or one-to-many case without loss, and partial participation of B does not force it.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students read “partial participation of B” as if it weakened the constraint on A. Participation constrains only the entity set it is attached to.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Total participation on the “many” side of a many-to-one turns “at most one” into “exactly one”.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Separate the two facts: the cardinality ratio bounds the count from above, participation bounds it from below. Combine them for the answer.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q03", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In a Chen-notation ER diagram, which of the following are drawn with a <b>double</b> outline or a double diamond? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A weak entity set.", "The identifying relationship of a weak entity set.", "A multivalued attribute.", "A derived attribute."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> ER notation distinguishes four special constructs. Double rectangle = weak entity set, double diamond = identifying relationship, double ellipse = multivalued attribute, dashed ellipse = derived attribute.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) A weak entity set is drawn as a double rectangle — true.</li><li>(B) The identifying relationship is drawn as a double diamond — true.</li><li>(C) A multivalued attribute uses a <b>double ellipse</b>, not a double outline on a set or a relationship.</li><li>(D) A derived attribute uses a <b>dashed ellipse</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) uses a double ellipse, which is a different shape.</li><li>(D) uses a dashed ellipse.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The double ellipse for a multivalued attribute is confused with the double rectangle for a weak entity set. Both are “double”, but the shape differs and so does the meaning.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Remember the pairing shape-to-meaning: double rectangle / double diamond belong together as the weak-entity pair; double ellipse is an attribute-level symbol.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q04", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Entity set E owns weak entity set W through an identifying relationship R. E has 4 entities, R permits at most 3 W-entities per E-entity, and every E-entity participates totally in R. What is the maximum possible number of entities in W?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A weak entity set's cardinality is bounded by the owner's cardinality times the per-owner limit. Total participation of the owner gives a lower bound, the per-owner limit gives the upper bound.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every E-entity must own at least one W-entity (total participation), so |W| ≥ 4.</li><li>Each E-entity may own at most 3 W-entities, so |W| ≤ 4 × 3 = 12.</li><li>The upper bound 12 is attained when all 4 E-entities own the full 3 W-entities each.</li><li>So the maximum is 12.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the two numbers instead of multiplying, or forgetting that a weak entity is owned by exactly one owner — which is what makes the count a product rather than a sum.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For an identifying relationship the owner side is one-to-many, so multiply the owner count by the per-owner limit; the owner's participation decides the minimum, the limit decides the maximum.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q05", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In ER modelling, aggregation is used to", "options": ["combine several attributes of one entity set into a single composite attribute.", "treat a relationship set together with its participating entity sets as one higher-level entity set that can then take part in another relationship.", "replace a weak entity set by a strong entity set.", "convert a ternary relationship into three binary relationships."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Aggregation is the ER construct that removes a limitation of plain ER diagrams: a relationship set cannot participate in another relationship. Aggregating a relationship plus its entity sets yields a single abstract entity that can.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Plain ER forbids a relationship set from being an endpoint of another relationship.</li><li>Example: a project is done by employees using machines. “Uses” relates the (employee, project) pair to a machine, not an employee to a machine directly.</li><li>Aggregation groups Employee–Works_On–Project into one entity set and lets that set participate in Uses.</li><li>So aggregation abstracts a relationship together with its participants into a higher-level entity set.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a composite attribute, drawn as an ellipse with sub-ellipses.</li><li>(C) is not an ER operation at all; a weak entity set stays weak unless the schema is redesigned.</li><li>(D) is a modelling decision about arity, not aggregation.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Aggregation is confused with generalization (which factors common attributes of entity sets into a superclass) and with the composite-attribute notation. All three “combine” things, but at different levels.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> If the sentence mentions “a relationship participating in another relationship”, the answer is aggregation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Aggregation = relationship-as-entity. Generalization = entity-as-subclass. Composite attribute = attribute-of-attribute.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q06", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A multivalued attribute in an ER diagram is represented by", "options": ["a single ellipse.", "a double ellipse.", "a dashed ellipse.", "a rectangle."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Attribute notation in Chen's model. A multivalued attribute can hold a set of values for one entity, and is drawn as a double ellipse. A derived attribute is drawn with a dashed ellipse; a key attribute has its name underlined.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Multivalued means several values per entity, e.g. phone numbers of a person.</li><li>The extra value needs an extra outline, so the symbol is the double ellipse.</li><li>A derived attribute (computed from others, e.g. age from date of birth) uses a dashed ellipse instead.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — a double ellipse.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A single ellipse is a simple, single-valued attribute.</li><li>(C) A dashed ellipse is a derived attribute.</li><li>(D) A rectangle is an entity set.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The double ellipse is confused with the double rectangle of a weak entity set. Note that the doubling pattern is applied to the base shape: rectangle for sets, ellipse for attributes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Learn the four attribute shapes together: single ellipse (simple), double ellipse (multivalued), dashed ellipse (derived), underlined name (key).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q07", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</span> has primary key <span class=\"mm\">(A, B)</span> and a foreign key <span class=\"mm\">C</span> that references <span class=\"mm\">S(C)</span>. Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> cannot be NULL, but <span class=\"mm\">C</span> may be NULL.", "<span class=\"mm\">C</span> cannot be NULL, but <span class=\"mm\">A</span> may be NULL.", "Any of <span class=\"mm\">A, B, C</span> may be NULL.", "<span class=\"mm\">A</span> alone must be unique."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Entity integrity says no attribute of a primary key may be NULL; referential integrity says a foreign key must either be NULL or match an existing referenced value. The two rules apply to different attributes here, so both can hold at once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Entity integrity: every attribute of the primary key (A, B) is NOT NULL. So A and B cannot be NULL.</li><li>Referential integrity constrains C: each C-value must be NULL or equal some S.C value.</li><li>Nothing forbids C from being NULL, because C is not part of R's primary key.</li><li>Hence (A, B) are mandatory, C is optional.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses the two rules.</li><li>(C) contradicts entity integrity.</li><li>(D) A is only part of the key; A alone may repeat.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students assume a foreign key can never be NULL. It can, provided the FK attributes are not part of the primary key — that is exactly how “optional” relationships are modelled.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Check whether the FK overlaps the primary key. No overlap → NULL is allowed. Overlap → entity integrity forbids NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q08", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</span> satisfy the functional dependencies <span class=\"mm\">{AB → C, C → D, D → A}</span>. How many candidate keys does <span class=\"mm\">R</span> have?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A candidate key is a minimal set of attributes whose closure is the whole relation. Compute closures of each attribute alone, then of the promising pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A⁺ = A; B⁺ = B; C⁺ = C D A = ACD (using C → D and D → A); D⁺ = D A = AD. None is the whole relation.</li><li>AB⁺ = A B C D = ABCD. AB is a key, and neither A nor B alone is, so AB is a candidate key.</li><li>BC⁺ = B C D A = ABCD. BC is minimal (B⁺, C⁺ both fail), so BC is a candidate key.</li><li>BD⁺ = B D A = ABD, and AB → C applies, so BD⁺ = ABCD. BD is minimal, so BD is a candidate key.</li><li>AC⁺ = ACD, AD⁺ = AD, CD⁺ = ACD — none is the whole relation. The answer is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 3 candidate keys (AB, BC, BD).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) misses BC and BD.</li><li>(B) misses BD.</li><li>(D) over-counts; AC, AD and CD are not keys.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at AB, or counting AB and BA as different keys. Also easy to miss BD, which needs the two-step closure D → A followed by AB → C.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Here C and D never appear in a closure that contains B, so B is in every key. That forces you to test AB, BC and BD — exactly three.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write every single-attribute closure first. Any attribute absent from all of them must appear in every candidate key; then test the minimal pairs containing it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q09", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about keys of a relation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every relation has at least one candidate key.", "A superkey that has no proper subset which is also a superkey is a candidate key.", "A relation can have at most one candidate key.", "A primary key attribute may contain NULL values."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Keys are defined through functional determination on the whole set of attributes. The set of all attributes of a relation is always a superkey, so minimal superkeys always exist.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The full attribute set functionally determines itself, so it is a superkey; shrinking it until it is minimal yields a candidate key. Hence at least one candidate key always exists.</li><li>A candidate key is precisely a minimal superkey — that is the definition given in (B).</li><li>A relation may have several candidate keys; one of them is chosen as the primary key.</li><li>Entity integrity forbids NULL in any primary key attribute.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is false — a relation may have several candidate keys, and exactly one is picked as primary.</li><li>(D) is false — entity integrity requires primary key attributes to be NOT NULL.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The word “candidate” is read as “optional”, suggesting there might be none. Every relation has at least one; what varies is how many.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two facts to keep: the full attribute set is always a superkey (so keys exist), and minimality is what separates candidate keys from superkeys.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q10", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</span> satisfies the functional dependency <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> and no other non-trivial dependency. How many superkeys does <span class=\"mm\">R</span> have?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey is any attribute set whose closure is all of R. With only A → B, the closure of a set S is S ∪ {B} if A ∈ S, and S otherwise; a superkey must therefore contain A and C.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The candidate key: A⁺ = AB, so A alone is not a key; AC⁺ = ABC. Hence AC is the only candidate key.</li><li>Every superkey must contain the candidate key AC.</li><li>The only attributes available to add are the remaining ones, here just B, giving the supersets AC and ABC.</li><li>So R has exactly 2 superkeys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only AC and forgetting that a superkey may contain extra attributes, or counting A and C separately as keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Find the candidate key first; every superkey is then a superset of it, so the count is 2 raised to the number of leftover attributes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q11", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D, E)</span> satisfy the functional dependencies <span class=\"mm\">{A → BC, CD → E, B → D, E → A}</span>. How many candidate keys does <span class=\"mm\">R</span> have?", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Candidate keys are minimal sets with full closure. Because the dependencies form a cycle (A → B → D → E → A together with C on one edge), the relation has several minimal keys and every attribute belongs to at least one of them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A⁺: A → BC gives B, C; B → D gives D; CD → E gives E. So A⁺ = ABCDE. A is a candidate key.</li><li>E⁺: E → A, then A → BC, then B → D, then CD → E. So E⁺ = ABCDE. E is a candidate key.</li><li>BC⁺: B → D, CD → E, E → A, A → BC. So BC⁺ = ABCDE, and B⁺ = BD, C⁺ = C — BC is minimal.</li><li>CD⁺: CD → E, E → A, A → BC, B → D. So CD⁺ = ABCDE, and C⁺ = C, D⁺ = D — CD is minimal.</li><li>B⁺ = BD, C⁺ = C, D⁺ = D, BD⁺ = BD — none is the whole relation, and every other set contains one of A, E, BC, CD. So there are exactly 4 candidate keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 4 candidate keys (A, E, BC, CD).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) misses BC and CD.</li><li>(B) misses one of E, BC, CD.</li><li>(D) over-counts: BD⁺ = BD is not a key.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Missing E or CD because the cycle makes closure look “too easy”. The dependency cycle A → B → D → E → A means closure from any member reaches all the others, so several attributes become keys in their own right.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Any attribute whose single-attribute closure is the whole relation is a key by itself; here that is true of A and E, and the C-bearing pairs add two more.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> When FDs form a cycle, compute closure from each single attribute first — every attribute that closes to ABCDE is already a key and needs no partner.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q12", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B)</span> has <span class=\"mm\">A</span> as its primary key and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> as an ordinary nullable attribute. Which one of the following tuples, if inserted into <span class=\"mm\">R</span>, violates a constraint implied by this schema?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">(NULL, 5)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">(1, NULL)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">(1, 5)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">(2, NULL)</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Entity integrity: no component of a primary key may be NULL. B is not part of the key, so NULL is allowed there and only uniqueness would ever reject it — and NULLs do not clash with each other under this rule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A is the primary key, so every tuple must supply a non-NULL value for A.</li><li>(NULL, 5) supplies no key value, so it violates entity integrity.</li><li>(1, NULL) and (2, NULL) are fine: the key values 1 and 2 are present, B is nullable.</li><li>(1, 5) is fine as long as 1 is not already present.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — (NULL, 5).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) and (D) have a valid key value and a NULL in a nullable column.</li><li>(C) has a valid key value and no NULL.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students reject (1, NULL) thinking no attribute may be NULL. Nullability is a schema property; only primary-key attributes are forced to be non-NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Look at where NULL sits. Inside the primary key → rejected. Outside it → allowed unless the column is declared NOT NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q13", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In relational algebra, the division operator <span class=\"mm\">R ÷ S</span> is used to express", "options": ["universal quantification — the “for all” condition.", "aggregation of a numeric attribute over groups.", "an outer join that preserves unmatched tuples.", "elimination of duplicates from a bag."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Division is the relational-algebra counterpart of universal quantification. R ÷ S keeps those A-values of R(A, B) that are paired in R with <b>every</b> B-value appearing in S.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write R(A, B) and S(B). R ÷ S = { a | for every b ∈ S, (a, b) ∈ R }.</li><li>The “for every b ∈ S” is exactly a ∀ condition, so division expresses queries such as “students who took all courses”.</li><li>It is not primitive: R ÷ S = π_A(R) − π_A( (π_A(R) × S) − R ).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — universal quantification.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Aggregation is not an operator of the basic algebra at all; it is added as γ in extended algebra.</li><li>(C) Outer joins are extensions of ⋈, written ⟕ / ⟖ / ⟗.</li><li>(D) Duplicate elimination is implicit in every algebra operator, not the job of ÷.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Division is mistaken for ordinary arithmetic division or for a join. It answers “for all”, not “how many”.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read division as a nested quantifier: keep a only if nothing in S is missing from a's row.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q14", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B) = {(1, 10), (2, 20), (3, 10)}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S(B, C) = {(10, p), (20, q), (30, r)}</span>. How many tuples are there in <span class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</span> (natural join)?", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A natural join equates the common attribute B and concatenates matching pairs. Its size is Σ_b r_b · s_b over the common values, where r_b and s_b are the multiplicities of b in R and S.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Common attribute is B.</li><li>B = 10 appears once in R and once in S → 1 × 1 = 1 tuple: (1, 10, p).</li><li>B = 20 appears once in R and once in S → 1 tuple: (2, 20, q).</li><li>B = 30 appears only in S → contributes nothing; B = 10 also occurs twice in R, so (3, 10, p) is a second tuple for B = 10.</li><li>Total = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 3 tuples.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) counts distinct B values instead of tuples.</li><li>(C) and (D) invent matches for B = 30, which is absent from R.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students assume one output tuple per distinct value of B (giving 2), forgetting that repeated B values in R each match. The join is a cross product restricted to equal B.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count multiplicities: for each common value b, multiply its count in R by its count in S and sum. Here 1·1 + 1·1 = 2 for B = 10 and B = 20, plus the second B = 10 row, giving 3.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q15", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about relational algebra operators are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Selection <span class=\"mm\">σ</span> filters tuples and projection <span class=\"mm\">π</span> filters attributes.", "Projection <span class=\"mm\">π</span> removes duplicate tuples from its result.", "<span class=\"mm\">R − S</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S − R</span> are always equal.", "<span class=\"mm\">R ∪ S</span> is defined only when <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S</span> are union-compatible."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Relational algebra is set-based, so every operator produces a relation, i.e. a set with no duplicate tuples, and the binary set operators require matching schemas.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) σ keeps rows satisfying a predicate, π keeps a chosen list of columns — true.</li><li>(B) Because the result is a relation (a set), π collapses duplicates — true.</li><li>(C) Set difference is not symmetric: R − S keeps R's tuples absent from S, which is a different set from S − R.</li><li>(D) Union requires the same number of attributes with compatible domains in the same order — true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) fails whenever the two relations are not identical: {1} − {2} = {1} but {2} − {1} = {2}.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The duplicate elimination of π is the usual surprise: SQL's SELECT does not remove duplicates, but relational algebra's π does. Mixing the two semantics is a standard error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Remember: algebra = sets, SQL = bags. Every algebra operator, including π, returns a set.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q16", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B) = {(a₁, b₁), (a₁, b₂), (a₂, b₁), (a₃, b₁), (a₃, b₂), (a₄, b₃)}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S(B) = {b₁, b₂}</span>. How many tuples does <span class=\"mm\">R ÷ S</span> contain?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> R ÷ S keeps an A-value only if it is paired with every B-value of S in R. Check each A-value against the full set {b₁, b₂}.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>a₁ is paired with b₁ and with b₂ → qualifies.</li><li>a₂ is paired only with b₁ → does not qualify.</li><li>a₃ is paired with b₁ and with b₂ → qualifies.</li><li>a₄ is paired only with b₃, which is not even in S → does not qualify.</li><li>So R ÷ S = {a₁, a₃}, two tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Requiring only “at least one” match, which would keep a₂ and a₄ as well. Division demands every value of S, not any one of them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Group R by A and check set-equality against S. Here a₁ and a₃ both map to exactly {b₁, b₂}.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q17", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Consider the schema <span class=\"mm\">Doctor(did, dname, dage)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">Patient(pid, rdid, pname, page)</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">rdid</span> is a foreign key referencing <span class=\"mm\">did</span>. Assume every doctor has at least one patient. Consider the relational algebra query<br/><span class=\"mm\">π_did(Doctor) − π_did( Doctor ⋈<sub>(did = rdid) ∧ (dage ≤ page)</sub> Patient )</span><br/>The query evaluates to the set of dids of doctors whose age is greater than that of", "options": ["some patient.", "all patients.", "some of his/her patients.", "all of his/her patients."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Read the query as a set difference: start from all doctors, then remove the doctors that have a patient whose age is at least the doctor's. What survives is exactly the doctors for whom no such patient exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Doctor ⋈<sub>(did = rdid) ∧ (dage ≤ page)</sub> Patient keeps pairs where the doctor's age is ≤ some patient's age, i.e. the doctor is not older than that patient.</li><li>Projecting on did gives the doctors having at least one patient who is at least as old as they are.</li><li>Subtracting this from π_did(Doctor) leaves the doctors who have no such patient.</li><li>“No patient is at least as old as the doctor” means the doctor is strictly older than every patient — all of his/her patients.</li><li>The assumption that every doctor has at least one patient matters: without it, a doctor with no patients would also survive, and the query would not mean “older than all”.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — all of his/her patients.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (C) describe the set that is <b>removed</b>, not the set that survives.</li><li>(B) would require a ∀ condition expressed with division, not a single join.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering “some patient” by stopping at the join instead of completing the difference, or reading the join's ≤ as if it removed patients rather than doctors.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Any “−” over a join that encodes “exists a violating tuple” is the standard pattern for a universal statement: removing the violators leaves the ones that satisfy “for all”.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Translate each operator in turn: ⋈ with a condition = “there exists”, π = “the did of”, − = “but not”. Composing them gives “doctors for whom there is no patient at least as old”, which is “older than all”.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q18", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "The relational algebra operator that returns a relation containing only a chosen subset of the attributes of its input is", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">σ</span> (select)", "<span class=\"mm\">π</span> (projection)", "<span class=\"mm\">ρ</span> (rename)", "<span class=\"mm\">⋈</span> (join)"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The six primitive operators act on different aspects of a relation: σ on rows, π on columns, ρ on names, and ×, ∪, − on whole relations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>σ<sub>condition</sub>(R) keeps the rows that satisfy the condition, leaving the schema unchanged.</li><li>π<sub>attr list</sub>(R) keeps the listed columns and discards the rest, leaving the rows filtered by duplicate elimination.</li><li>ρ renames a relation or its attributes; ⋈ combines two relations on a condition.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — π (projection).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) selects rows, not columns.</li><li>(C) only changes names.</li><li>(D) combines two relations.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> σ and π are swapped. A quick check: σ for <b>s</b>elect rows (horizontal cut), π for <b>p</b>roject columns (vertical cut).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Picture the table: a horizontal cut removes rows (σ), a vertical cut removes columns (π).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q19", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S(A, B)</span> be union-compatible relations. Which of the following are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">R ∪ S</span> contains every tuple that appears in <span class=\"mm\">R</span> or in <span class=\"mm\">S</span> or in both, with duplicates removed.", "<span class=\"mm\">R ∩ S</span> can be written as <span class=\"mm\">R − (R − S)</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">R − S = S − R</span> for all <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">R × S</span> contains <span class=\"mm\">|R| + |S|</span> tuples."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Set semantics. Union, intersection and difference behave as ordinary set operations once the schemas match; the Cartesian product multiplies cardinalities.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition of set union; since the result is a relation, duplicates collapse.</li><li>(B) R − S removes from R everything shared with S; removing those again from R leaves exactly the common part, so R − (R − S) = R ∩ S.</li><li>(C) fails for any unequal pair, e.g. R = {t₁}, S = {t₂}.</li><li>(D) is wrong: |R × S| = |R| · |S|.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) Difference is directional.</li><li>(D) The Cartesian product multiplies, it does not add.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Intersection is thought to need its own operator. It does not: ∩ is derivable from − (and from ⋈). Similarly ∪ is derivable from − and ×, which is why only six operators are primitive.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Memorise the derivations: R ∩ S = R − (R − S), and |R × S| = |R| × |S|.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q20", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</span> has 10 tuples and relation <span class=\"mm\">S(C, D)</span> has 8 tuples, with <span class=\"mm\">C</span> the only common attribute. The tuples of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> use exactly 4 distinct values of <span class=\"mm\">C</span>, and the tuples of <span class=\"mm\">S</span> use exactly 5 distinct values of <span class=\"mm\">C</span>. What is the maximum possible number of tuples in <span class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</span>?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "31", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The size of a natural join is Σ<sub>c</sub> r_c · s_c over the common attribute, where r_c and s_c are the multiplicities of c in R and S. Under fixed totals the sum of products is largest when the two distributions are aligned and as concentrated as the distinct-value counts allow.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let r_c ≥ 1 for 4 values of C with Σ r_c = 10, and s_c ≥ 1 for 5 values of C with Σ s_c = 8. Then |R ⋈ S| = Σ_c r_c · s_c.</li><li>Because there are 4 and 5 distinct values, at least 1 tuple must be spent on each of them. That leaves 10 − 4 = 6 extra R-tuples and 8 − 5 = 3 extra S-tuples to concentrate on one shared value c₀.</li><li>Put all the extras on c₀: r = (7, 1, 1, 1) and s = (4, 1, 1, 1, 1).</li><li>Matching the largest counts together gives 7 × 4 = 28 on c₀, and the three remaining shared values contribute 1 × 1 each: 28 + 3 = 31.</li><li>No other distribution does better — by the rearrangement inequality the best alignment pairs the largest r_c with the largest s_c, and concentrating mass maximises the largest product.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 31.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying the totals, 10 × 8 = 80, or the distinct counts, 4 × 5 = 20. Both ignore the hard constraint that each of the distinct values must carry at least one tuple.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Subtract the compulsory one tuple per distinct value first; only the leftovers can be concentrated. Here 6 and 3 leftovers give 7 × 4 = 28 plus 3 forced unit products.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q21", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "The tuple relational calculus query <span class=\"mm\">{ t | t ∈ Student ∧ t[age] &gt; 20 }</span> is equivalent to", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">π_age(σ_age&gt;20(Student))</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">σ_age&gt;20(Student)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">σ_age&gt;20(π_age(Student))</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">Student ⋈ σ_age&gt;20(Student)</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In tuple calculus the free tuple variable ranges over all tuples of the relation named in the membership clause; a conjunctive condition on its attributes corresponds exactly to selection, and no projection is involved because t carries all attributes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>t ∈ Student fixes the range of t to the tuples of Student.</li><li>t[age] &gt; 20 is the selection predicate.</li><li>The result keeps whole tuples, so the algebra translation is σ_age&gt;20(Student) with the same schema.</li><li>Projection would be needed only if the query returned t[some attributes], not t itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — σ_age&gt;20(Student).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) changes the schema to a single column, which the query does not ask for.</li><li>(C) projects before selecting, which may drop attributes the predicate needs.</li><li>(D) joins Student with itself; the calculus query has one variable.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students add a projection because the predicate mentions age. The predicate filters; it does not change the output schema. Only an explicit attribute list on t changes the schema.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Membership clause → which relation. Conditions on attributes → σ. t[list] on the left of the bar → π of that list.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q22", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In tuple relational calculus, the expression <span class=\"mm\">{ t | ¬(t ∈ R) }</span> is rejected as unsafe because", "options": ["it is syntactically invalid.", "it can denote an infinite relation, since t may take values outside the active domain.", "negation is not permitted in relational calculus.", "t is a free variable."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A calculus expression is safe when every value it can produce comes from the active domain — the set of values actually occurring in the database. { t | ¬(t ∈ R) } ranges over the infinite universe of all possible tuples, so it is not a finite relation and cannot be evaluated.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The comprehension { t | ¬(t ∈ R) } admits every tuple not in R, over every possible attribute domain.</li><li>That set is infinite (or at least unbounded), so it is not a relation in the database sense.</li><li>Safety requires each answer tuple to be drawn from dom(R) extended by constants in the query.</li><li>Negation itself is perfectly legal — only its unrestricted use is the problem.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — it can denote an infinite relation.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The syntax is well formed.</li><li>(C) Negation is allowed; unsafe negation is not.</li><li>(D) t is free by design — the free variable names the answer tuples.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Negation is blamed. The calculus allows ¬ freely; what it forbids is a query whose result is not confined to the active domain. { t | t ∈ R ∧ ¬P(t) } is safe, { t | ¬(t ∈ R) } is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask of every calculus query: which relation bounds the free variable? If nothing does, the query is unsafe.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q23", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about relational calculus are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Tuple relational calculus and domain relational calculus have the same expressive power.", "Every relational algebra expression can be written as a safe relational calculus expression.", "Relational calculus is procedural: it states how the answer is to be computed.", "The safety condition confines a query's values to the active domain of the database."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Relational algebra and the two calculi are all <b>relationally complete</b>: the algebra is procedural, the calculi are declarative, and all three express exactly the same class of queries once safety is imposed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) TRC and DRC are inter-translatable, so neither is more expressive — true.</li><li>(B) Codd's theorem gives translations both ways between algebra and safe calculus — true.</li><li>(C) Calculus says <b>what</b> the answer is (a set of tuples satisfying a formula); the algebra says <b>how</b>. So calculus is declarative, not procedural.</li><li>(D) Safety restricts quantifiers and free variables to the active domain, which is what makes the expression finite — true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) reverses the definitions — calculus is declarative, not procedural.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calculus is assumed to be the “stronger” formalism. In fact it has exactly the same power as the algebra; what it lacks is a fixed evaluation order, which is why SQL compilers translate to algebra.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two things to keep straight: procedural = algebra, declarative = calculus, equal in power; and safety is the condition that makes calculus finite.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q24", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B) = {(1, 5), (2, 5), (2, 7), (3, 5), (4, 9)}</span>. The domain relational calculus query<br/><span class=\"mm\">{ ⟨a⟩ | ∃b (⟨a, b⟩ ∈ R ∧ b = 5) }</span><br/>returns how many distinct values of <span class=\"mm\">a</span>?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A domain-calculus query projects the free variables of its formula. The existential quantifier on b makes b a bound (projected-away) variable, so the answer is the set of a-values that co-occur with b = 5.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The formula requires a pair (a, b) in R with b = 5.</li><li>Pairs with b = 5 are (1, 5), (2, 5) and (3, 5).</li><li>Their a-values are 1, 2, 3 — three distinct values.</li><li>(2, 7) and (4, 9) are excluded because their b is not 5.</li><li>In algebra this is π_A(σ_B=5(R)), which has 3 tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting tuples rather than distinct a-values, or being distracted by the repeated a = 2. The result of a calculus query is a set, so a = 2 contributes once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Free variables = output columns. Here only a is free, so translate to π_A(σ_B=5(R)) and count.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q25", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which one of the following queries <b>cannot</b> be expressed in relational algebra, nor in safe relational calculus?", "options": ["Names of employees who earn more than their manager.", "Names of all employees who are, directly or indirectly, subordinates of a given employee.", "Names of employees who work on every project.", "Names of employees who work on no project."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Relational algebra and safe calculus are relationally complete but not Turing complete: they can express any finite first-order query over the stored relations, and nothing that needs an unbounded number of iteration steps. Transitive closure is the standard query outside that class.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is a self-join of Employee with itself on manager = eid plus a comparison — first-order, expressible.</li><li>(C) is division: π_eid(Works) ÷ π_pid(Project) — expressible.</li><li>(D) is a difference: π_eid(Employee) − π_eid(Works) — expressible.</li><li>(B) asks for the transitive closure of the “manager of” relation. Its length is unbounded, so no fixed finite expression of the algebra or of first-order calculus can capture it.</li><li>This is the classic reason SQL needed recursion (WITH RECURSIVE) to compute closure.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) needs one join and one comparison.</li><li>(C) is a textbook division query.</li><li>(D) is a set difference.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> “Indirectly” is read as “one more join”. One extra join only reaches depth two; no finite number of joins reaches every depth, which is precisely why closure is not first-order expressible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Whenever a query says “directly or indirectly”, “any number of levels”, or “shortest path”, suspect transitive closure — outside relational algebra.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Distinguish the quantifier patterns. “For all projects” is a bounded ∀ and division handles it; “any number of steps” is an unbounded iteration and needs recursion.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q26", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In Chen notation, a relationship set of <b>degree three</b> is drawn as", "options": ["a single diamond joined by three lines, one to each of the three participating entity sets.", "three diamonds, each joined to two entity sets.", "a double diamond joined to three entity sets.", "a rectangle joined to three diamonds."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The <i>degree</i> of a relationship set is the number of entity sets that take part in it. Chen notation draws one relationship as one diamond, with one line per participating entity set — so degree three is one diamond with three lines.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Count the entity sets that take part: three.</li><li>Draw one diamond — the relationship itself is a single object, not one object per pair.</li><li>Draw one connecting line from the diamond to each of the three entity sets.</li><li>Label each line with that entity set's cardinality contribution if the diagram shows it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — one diamond with three lines.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading a ternary relationship as three binary diamonds. Three binary relationships record three independent pairwise facts and cannot express the single triple fact that a ternary relationship records.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q27", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Entity sets <span class=\"mm\">A</span> (<span class=\"mm\">m</span> entities) and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> (<span class=\"mm\">n</span> entities), with <span class=\"mm\">m, n ≥ 1</span>, take part in a many-to-many relationship <span class=\"mm\">R</span>, and <b>both</b> participations are total. The number of tuples in the relation that represents <span class=\"mm\">R</span> lies", "options": ["exactly at <span class=\"mm\">m · n</span>.", "between <span class=\"mm\">max(m, n)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">m · n</span>, inclusive.", "between <span class=\"mm\">m + n</span> and <span class=\"mm\">m · n</span>, inclusive.", "exactly at <span class=\"mm\">m + n</span>."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two independent constraints bound a many-to-many relationship: the ratio gives the upper bound and total participation gives the lower bound.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Upper bound: each of the <span class=\"mm\">m</span> A-entities may pair with each of the <span class=\"mm\">n</span> B-entities, so at most <span class=\"mm\">m · n</span> distinct pairs.</li><li>Lower bound from A's side: total participation means every A-entity appears in at least one pair, so at least <span class=\"mm\">m</span> pairs.</li><li>Lower bound from B's side: every B-entity appears in at least one pair, so at least <span class=\"mm\">n</span> pairs.</li><li>Pairs are distinct tuples, so the count is at least the larger of the two bounds, i.e. at least <span class=\"mm\">max(m, n)</span>. Both bounds are attainable.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">max(m, n) ≤ |R| ≤ m · n</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the cardinalities (<span class=\"mm\">m + n</span>) or assuming the maximum is forced. The maximum <span class=\"mm\">m · n</span> needs every A-entity to pair with <i>every</i> B-entity, which the m:n ratio permits but does not require.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q28", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<span class=\"mm\">W</span> is a weak entity set owned by entity set <span class=\"mm\">E</span> through the identifying relationship <span class=\"mm\">R</span>. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">W</span> has a partial key (discriminator) that distinguishes W-entities owned by the <i>same</i> E-entity.", "The participation of <span class=\"mm\">W</span> in <span class=\"mm\">R</span> is total.", "The primary key of the relation built from <span class=\"mm\">W</span> is the primary key of <span class=\"mm\">E</span> together with <span class=\"mm\">W</span>'s partial key.", "A W-entity may exist without being related to any E-entity."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A weak entity set has no key of its own. It is identified by its owner, and its existence is dependent on that owner — existence dependency is exactly what 'weak' means.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Because <span class=\"mm\">W</span> has no key of its own, it carries a partial key (drawn with a dashed underline) that only has to be unique <i>within</i> one owner's set of W-entities — so (A) is true.</li><li>A weak entity cannot be identified without its owner, so it must always take part in the identifying relationship — so (B) is true.</li><li>The identifying relationship is one-to-many from <span class=\"mm\">E</span> to <span class=\"mm\">W</span>, so the owner's key together with the partial key is unique over all of <span class=\"mm\">W</span> — so (C) is true.</li><li>(D) contradicts existence dependency: an owner-less weak entity has no identity at all — so (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the partial key alone identifies a weak entity. It does not: two different owners may each own a W-entity with the same partial-key value, which is why the owner's key must be prefixed.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q29", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "An ER schema contains entity sets <span class=\"mm\">A, B, C, D</span>; a weak entity set <span class=\"mm\">W</span> owned by <span class=\"mm\">A</span>; a multivalued attribute <span class=\"mm\">phone</span> on <span class=\"mm\">B</span>; a 1:N relationship from <span class=\"mm\">A</span> to <span class=\"mm\">C</span>; a many-to-many relationship between <span class=\"mm\">B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">D</span>; and a ternary relationship among <span class=\"mm\">A, B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">C</span>.<br/>Applying the standard ER-to-relational mapping — one relation per strong or weak entity set, a separate relation per multivalued attribute, a 1:N relationship absorbed into the relation on its many side, and a separate relation per m:n or n-ary relationship — how many relations does the schema produce?", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The ER-to-relational mapping is a fixed recipe; the question is which constructs cost a relation and which are absorbed into an existing one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Strong entity sets <span class=\"mm\">A, B, C, D</span> → 4 relations.</li><li>Weak entity set <span class=\"mm\">W</span> → 1 more relation (it becomes a relation whose key is A's key plus W's partial key) → 5.</li><li>Multivalued attribute <span class=\"mm\">phone</span> → 1 more relation (owner key + phone value) → 6.</li><li>1:N relationship <span class=\"mm\">A → C</span> → 0 new relations; it is absorbed as a foreign key in <span class=\"mm\">C</span> → still 6.</li><li>m:n relationship <span class=\"mm\">B–D</span> → 1 more relation → 7.</li><li>Ternary relationship among <span class=\"mm\">A, B, C</span> → 1 more relation → 8.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging a relation for the 1:N relationship, or forgetting that a weak entity set still gets its own relation. The 1:N side is the free one; the weak entity set is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Only relationships whose cardinality cannot be carried by a foreign key — m:n and n-ary — need a relation of their own.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q30", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which one of the following statements about a ternary relationship <span class=\"mm\">R</span> among entity sets <span class=\"mm\">A, B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">C</span> is TRUE?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">R</span> is always equivalent to three binary relationships among <span class=\"mm\">A, B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">C</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">R</span> can record a combination of an A-entity, a B-entity and a C-entity that no collection of binary relationships can record.", "<span class=\"mm\">R</span> must be many-to-many in all three directions.", "<span class=\"mm\">R</span> requires every entity to take part exactly once."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A relationship of degree three is an association among three entities <i>simultaneously</i>, not three associations among pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A ternary relationship instance is a triple <span class=\"mm\">(a, b, c)</span>.</li><li>Three binary relationships would instead store <span class=\"mm\">(a, b)</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(b, c)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(a, c)</span> separately. From those three pairs you cannot tell which triples actually occurred: the pairs <span class=\"mm\">(a1, b1), (b1, c1), (a1, c1)</span> are consistent with both <span class=\"mm\">(a1, b1, c1)</span> and with <span class=\"mm\">(a1, b1, c2), (a1, b2, c1), …</span></li><li>So the ternary relationship carries information the binary decomposition loses — (B) is true and (A) is false.</li><li>Nothing forces the ratio to be m:n in every direction, and nothing forces single participation — (C) and (D) are false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a ternary relationship is just 'three binary ones glued together'. It is not, and that is precisely why the ER-to-relational mapping gives an n-ary relationship its own relation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q31", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Entity set <span class=\"mm\">A</span> has a one-to-many relationship <span class=\"mm\">R</span> with entity set <span class=\"mm\">B</span> — that is, one A-entity is related to many B-entities. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["Each B-entity is related to at most one A-entity.", "Each A-entity is related to at most one B-entity.", "Each A-entity must be related to at least one B-entity.", "Each B-entity must be related to at least one A-entity."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A cardinality ratio constrains <i>how many</i> entities on one side may be associated with an entity on the other. It says nothing about whether an entity must be associated at all — that is participation, a separate constraint.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>'One A to many B' means the function runs from B to A: each B-entity picks at most one A-entity.</li><li>So (A) is true.</li><li>The same ratio allows an A-entity to have any number of B-entities, including zero, so (B) is false.</li><li>(C) and (D) assert total participation, which the question never states. Both are false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 'one-to-many from A to B' as 'one B per A'. The 'one' side is the side that is unique in the other direction: one A per B.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q32", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</span> has exactly one candidate key, <span class=\"mm\">{A}</span>. The number of superkeys of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> is", "options": ["4", "8", "15", "16"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey is any attribute set that contains a candidate key. With a single candidate key <span class=\"mm\">{A}</span>, a set is a superkey exactly when it contains <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Any superkey must contain <span class=\"mm\">A</span> — otherwise it cannot determine the whole tuple.</li><li>Each of the remaining three attributes <span class=\"mm\">B, C, D</span> is then free to be in or out.</li><li>That gives <span class=\"mm\">2³ = 8</span> superkeys.</li><li>Check: <span class=\"mm\">{A}, {AB}, {AC}, {AD}, {ABC}, {ABD}, {ACD}, {ABCD}</span> — eight.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 8.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <span class=\"mm\">2⁴ = 16</span> by letting <span class=\"mm\">A</span> vary too. <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is compulsory in every superkey, so only the other three attributes are free.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With one candidate key of size <span class=\"mm\">k</span> in a relation of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> attributes, the superkey count is <span class=\"mm\">2^(n − k)</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q33", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D, E)</span> satisfies the functional dependencies <span class=\"mm\">F = {A → B, BC → D, D → E, E → C}</span>. The number of candidate keys of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> is", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A candidate key is a minimal attribute set whose closure is all five attributes. Enumerate closures, then drop any set that contains a smaller key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">A⁺ = {A, B}</span> — <span class=\"mm\">BC → D</span> needs <span class=\"mm\">C</span>, so nothing further fires. <span class=\"mm\">A</span> alone is not a key.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, C}⁺</span>: <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> adds B; <span class=\"mm\">BC → D</span> adds D; <span class=\"mm\">D → E</span> adds E. Closure is all five — <span class=\"mm\">{A, C}</span> is a key.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, D}⁺</span>: <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span>; <span class=\"mm\">D → E</span>; <span class=\"mm\">E → C</span>; then <span class=\"mm\">BC → D</span>. All five — <span class=\"mm\">{A, D}</span> is a key.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, E}⁺</span>: <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span>; <span class=\"mm\">E → C</span>; <span class=\"mm\">BC → D</span>; <span class=\"mm\">D → E</span>. All five — <span class=\"mm\">{A, E}</span> is a key.</li><li>No set without <span class=\"mm\">A</span> closes: from <span class=\"mm\">{B, C, D, E}</span> the rules <span class=\"mm\">BC → D, D → E, E → C</span> never produce <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.</li><li>So the candidate keys are <span class=\"mm\">{A, C}, {A, D}, {A, E}</span> — three, all minimal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first key found, or counting <span class=\"mm\">{A, C, D}</span> as a fourth key. A superset of a key is a superkey, never a candidate key.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q34", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about functional dependencies are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> is trivial exactly when <span class=\"mm\">B ⊆ A</span>.", "If <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B → C</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">A → C</span>.", "If <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">AC → BC</span> for every attribute set <span class=\"mm\">C</span>.", "If <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">C → B</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">A → C</span>."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Armstrong's axioms: reflexivity, augmentation and transitivity generate every dependency implied by a set, and nothing else does.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Reflexivity: <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> holds automatically when <span class=\"mm\">B ⊆ A</span>, because a set always determines its own subsets. Such a dependency is called trivial — (A) is true.</li><li>Transitivity: <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B → C</span> give <span class=\"mm\">A → C</span> — (B) is true.</li><li>Augmentation: adding the same attributes to both sides preserves the dependency, so <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> yields <span class=\"mm\">AC → BC</span> — (C) is true.</li><li>(D) is the converse of transitivity and is not a valid rule. Two different sets may each determine <span class=\"mm\">B</span> without determining each other — (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <span class=\"mm\">A → B, C → B ⊢ A → C</span> as transitivity. Transitivity needs the same set on the right of one dependency and the left of the next; here the shared attribute is on the right of both.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q35", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</span> has exactly one candidate key, <span class=\"mm\">{A, B}</span>. The number of superkeys of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> is", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every superkey must contain the whole candidate key; the attributes outside it are free.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A superkey must contain <span class=\"mm\">{A, B}</span>.</li><li>The two remaining attributes <span class=\"mm\">C, D</span> are each free to be included or excluded.</li><li>That gives <span class=\"mm\">2² = 4</span> superkeys: <span class=\"mm\">{AB}, {ABC}, {ABD}, {ABCD}</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 8 by treating the key as size 1, or 16 by letting <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> vary independently. Neither can be dropped.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q36", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</span> has exactly two candidate keys, <span class=\"mm\">{A}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{B}</span>. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">R</span> has exactly 8 superkeys.", "<span class=\"mm\">{A, B}</span> is a superkey but not a candidate key.", "Every attribute of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> is prime.", "<span class=\"mm\">R</span> has exactly two superkeys."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Candidate keys are the <i>minimal</i> superkeys. Any larger set containing a key is still a superkey, but minimality is what a candidate key adds.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, B}</span> determines every attribute (it contains the key <span class=\"mm\">{A}</span>), so it is a superkey.</li><li>It is not minimal, because the proper subset <span class=\"mm\">{A}</span> is already a superkey. So it is not a candidate key — (B) is true.</li><li>Superkeys are the sets containing <span class=\"mm\">A</span> or containing <span class=\"mm\">B</span>: <span class=\"mm\">{A}, {B}, {AB}, {AC}, {BC}, {ABC}</span> — six, so (A) and (D) are both false.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">C</span> belongs to no candidate key, so it is non-prime — (C) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding 4 + 4 = 8 superkeys by counting the supersets of each key separately. The set <span class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</span> is counted twice that way; inclusion–exclusion gives 4 + 4 − 2 = 6.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q37", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which one of the following violates <b>entity integrity</b>?", "options": ["A tuple whose primary-key attribute is NULL.", "A tuple whose foreign key is NULL.", "A tuple whose non-key attribute is NULL.", "Two tuples that agree on a non-key attribute."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Entity integrity: no component of a primary key may be NULL, because the key is what identifies the tuple. Referential integrity, by contrast, is about foreign keys and explicitly allows NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A primary key identifies a tuple; a NULL is 'unknown', and an unknown value cannot identify anything — so (A) violates entity integrity.</li><li>A NULL foreign key means 'this tuple refers to nothing', which referential integrity permits — (B) is legal.</li><li>Non-key attributes may be NULL unless a NOT NULL constraint says otherwise — (C) is legal.</li><li>Only key attributes must be unique; duplicates in a non-key attribute are ordinary data — (D) is legal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing entity integrity with referential integrity, and so rejecting a NULL foreign key. That is exactly the case referential integrity allows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q38", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation <span class=\"mm\">R</span> has 4 tuples and 3 attributes; relation <span class=\"mm\">S</span> has 5 tuples and 2 attributes. They share exactly one attribute <span class=\"mm\">B</span>, which is a key of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and a foreign key in <span class=\"mm\">S</span> that is never NULL. Every tuple of <span class=\"mm\">S</span> matches exactly one tuple of <span class=\"mm\">R</span>. The number of tuples in <span class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</span> is", "options": ["4", "5", "9", "20"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A natural join pairs tuples that agree on the common attribute. When the common attribute is a key on one side, each tuple on the other side has at most one partner.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each of the 5 tuples of <span class=\"mm\">S</span> has a <span class=\"mm\">B</span>-value that is a key value of <span class=\"mm\">R</span>, so it matches exactly one <span class=\"mm\">R</span>-tuple.</li><li>That produces exactly one joined tuple per <span class=\"mm\">S</span>-tuple: 5.</li><li>Some <span class=\"mm\">R</span>-tuples may match nothing and simply do not appear — the join is not an outer join, so <span class=\"mm\">R</span>'s 4 tuples do not add to the count.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying 4 × 5 = 20, which is the size of the Cartesian product and only an upper bound for a join. Because <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is a key of <span class=\"mm\">R</span>, no <span class=\"mm\">S</span>-tuple can have two partners.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q39", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "For union-compatible relations <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S</span>, which expression is equivalent to <span class=\"mm\">R ∩ S</span>?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">R − (R − S)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">(R − S) − S</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">(R ∪ S) − R</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">π<sub>all</sub>(R) − π<sub>all</sub>(S)</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Intersection is not primitive in the core algebra; it is defined from set difference.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">R − S</span> keeps the tuples of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> that are not in <span class=\"mm\">S</span>.</li><li>Removing those from <span class=\"mm\">R</span> leaves exactly the tuples of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> that <i>are</i> in <span class=\"mm\">S</span>: <span class=\"mm\">R − (R − S) = R ∩ S</span>.</li><li>(B) simplifies to <span class=\"mm\">R − S</span>; (C) is <span class=\"mm\">S − R</span>; (D) is just <span class=\"mm\">R − S</span> again.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing <span class=\"mm\">(R ∪ S) − R</span>, which looks like it 'removes the overlap' but actually returns <span class=\"mm\">S − R</span> — the opposite set.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q40", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Consider <span class=\"mm\">Works(eid, pid)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">Projects(pid)</span>. Which expressions return exactly the <span class=\"mm\">eid</span>s of employees who work on <b>every</b> project? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">π<sub>eid</sub>(Works) ÷ π<sub>pid</sub>(Projects)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">π<sub>eid</sub>(Works) − π<sub>eid</sub>((π<sub>eid</sub>(Works) × π<sub>pid</sub>(Projects)) − Works)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">π<sub>eid</sub>(Works) − π<sub>eid</sub>(π<sub>eid</sub>(Works) × π<sub>pid</sub>(Projects))</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">π<sub>eid</sub>(Works ⋈ Projects)</span>"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 'For all' is division. Equivalently, it is 'difference of difference': take everyone, subtract those who miss at least one project.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the division operator applied directly: it returns the <span class=\"mm\">eid</span>s whose set of projects contains the whole of <span class=\"mm\">π<sub>pid</sub>(Projects)</span> — true.</li><li>(B) builds the set of <i>missing</i> pairs — every (eid, pid) combination that is not in <span class=\"mm\">Works</span> — projects it to <span class=\"mm\">eid</span>, and subtracts it from all employees. What survives misses no project — true.</li><li>(C) subtracts all employees who work on anything at all, which returns only employees with no project — false.</li><li>(D) returns every employee who works on at least one project — that is 'exists', not 'for all' — false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading (D) as the answer. A join with the projects relation answers 'works on <i>some</i> project'; the universal quantifier needs division or the double difference.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q41", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">R(A) = {1, 2, 3}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S(A) = {2, 3, 4}</span>. The number of tuples in <span class=\"mm\">R ⋈<sub>R.A &lt; S.A</sub> S</span> is", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A theta join pairs every tuple of one relation with every tuple of the other that satisfies the condition — here a strict inequality, not an equality on a common attribute.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>List the pairs <span class=\"mm\">(r, s)</span> with <span class=\"mm\">r ∈ R</span>, <span class=\"mm\">s ∈ S</span> and <span class=\"mm\">r &lt; s</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">r = 1</span>: <span class=\"mm\">s = 2, 3, 4</span> → 3 pairs.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">r = 2</span>: <span class=\"mm\">s = 3, 4</span> → 2 pairs.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">r = 3</span>: <span class=\"mm\">s = 4</span> → 1 pair.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">3 + 2 + 1 = 6</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Joining on equality of <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, which would give only the pairs <span class=\"mm\">(2, 2)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(3, 3)</span>. The condition is <span class=\"mm\">&lt;</span>, and both relations are kept whole — the attribute is not merged.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q42", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">S(B)</span> be a relation with <b>zero</b> tuples. For a relation <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B)</span>, the division <span class=\"mm\">R ÷ S</span> is", "options": ["the empty relation.", "<span class=\"mm\">π<sub>A</sub>(R)</span>.", "undefined, because division needs a non-empty divisor.", "<span class=\"mm\">R</span>."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Division asks: which <span class=\"mm\">A</span>-values are paired with <i>every</i> <span class=\"mm\">B</span>-value in <span class=\"mm\">S</span>? With an empty <span class=\"mm\">S</span> the requirement is vacuously satisfied by every value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>By definition, <span class=\"mm\">R ÷ S = { a | for every b ∈ S, (a, b) ∈ R }</span>.</li><li>When <span class=\"mm\">S = ∅</span> there is no <span class=\"mm\">b</span> to check, so the condition holds for every <span class=\"mm\">a</span> that appears in <span class=\"mm\">R</span>.</li><li>The values of <span class=\"mm\">a</span> that appear in <span class=\"mm\">R</span> are exactly <span class=\"mm\">π<sub>A</sub>(R)</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">R ÷ ∅ = π<sub>A</sub>(R)</span>.</li><li>Confirm with the derived formula: <span class=\"mm\">π<sub>A</sub>(R) − π<sub>A</sub>((π<sub>A</sub>(R) × ∅) − R) = π<sub>A</sub>(R) − π<sub>A</sub>(∅) = π<sub>A</sub>(R)</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 'the empty relation' by pattern-matching 'empty input, empty output'. Universally quantified conditions are vacuously true over an empty range, so the result is the <i>largest</i> possible set, not the smallest.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> This vacuous-truth case is the same reason a universal statement over an empty domain is true, and it is a favourite GATE trap.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q43", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following is <b>not</b> a unary relational algebra operator?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">σ</span> (selection)", "<span class=\"mm\">π</span> (projection)", "<span class=\"mm\">⋈</span> (natural join)", "<span class=\"mm\">ρ</span> (rename)"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Arity of an operator = number of relations it consumes. Selection, projection and rename each transform one relation; a join combines two.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">σ<sub>cond</sub>(R)</span> filters the tuples of a single relation — unary.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">π<sub>attrs</sub>(R)</span> keeps a subset of the attributes of a single relation — unary.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">ρ</span> renames a relation or its attributes — unary.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</span> needs both <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S</span> — it is binary, so it is the answer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Classifying the join as unary because 'it acts on the attributes in common'. It consumes two relations, which is what arity measures.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q44", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S(B, C)</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is a key of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and a foreign key in <span class=\"mm\">S</span> that is never NULL. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</span> has exactly as many tuples as <span class=\"mm\">S</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</span> has attributes <span class=\"mm\">A, B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">C</span>.", "<span class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</span> can contain more tuples than <span class=\"mm\">|R| × |S|</span>.", "The natural join is associative."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A natural join equates the common attributes and keeps one copy of each. When the common attribute is a key on one side, the join is a lookup, not a product.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every tuple of <span class=\"mm\">S</span> has a non-NULL <span class=\"mm\">B</span>-value, and referential integrity guarantees a matching <span class=\"mm\">R</span>-tuple. Since <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is a key of <span class=\"mm\">R</span>, that partner is unique — one joined tuple per <span class=\"mm\">S</span>-tuple, so the counts are equal — (A) is true.</li><li>The common attribute appears once, and the remaining attributes of both sides are retained — (B) is true.</li><li>A join can never produce more than the Cartesian product <span class=\"mm\">|R| × |S|</span>; it selects a subset of those pairs — (C) is false.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">(R ⋈ S) ⋈ T = R ⋈ (S ⋈ T)</span> for natural joins, and it is commutative too — (D) is true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a natural join can blow up the row count. Duplication on the common attribute is what multiplies rows, and a key on one side makes that impossible.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q45", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relations <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S(A, B)</span> are union-compatible, with <span class=\"mm\">|R| = 12</span>, <span class=\"mm\">|S| = 9</span> and <span class=\"mm\">|R − S| = 5</span>. The number of tuples in <span class=\"mm\">R ∩ S</span> is", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">R</span> splits into two disjoint parts: the tuples it shares with <span class=\"mm\">S</span>, and the tuples it does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">R = (R − S) ∪ (R ∩ S)</span>, and the two parts are disjoint.</li><li>So <span class=\"mm\">|R| = |R − S| + |R ∩ S|</span>.</li><li>Substituting: <span class=\"mm\">12 = 5 + |R ∩ S|</span>, hence <span class=\"mm\">|R ∩ S| = 7</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <span class=\"mm\">|R ∪ S|</span> instead: that would give <span class=\"mm\">12 + 9 − 7 = 14</span>, a different quantity. The stated set is the difference, not the union.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q46", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In the tuple relational calculus formula <span class=\"mm\">{ t | ∃s ∈ R (t[A] = s[A]) }</span>, the variable <span class=\"mm\">t</span> is", "options": ["bound by the existential quantifier.", "free.", "quantified over the domain of <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.", "unsafe."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A variable is free when it is not in the scope of a quantifier. The free variables of a formula are exactly the columns of the result.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">s</span> is introduced by <span class=\"mm\">∃s ∈ R</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">s</span> is bound.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">t</span> is never introduced by a quantifier — it appears on the left of the bar and inside the formula as a free variable.</li><li>Free variables supply the output columns, which is why the result is a set of <span class=\"mm\">t</span> tuples — so (B) is true.</li><li>Quantification over the domain of <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is domain calculus syntax; here the range is a relation, so (C) is false. Nothing about safety is violated, so (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the variable on the left of the bar is bound because it 'belongs to' the query. Being on the left of the bar is precisely what makes it free.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q47", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about the domain relational calculus are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A query has the form <span class=\"mm\">{ ⟨x₁, …, xₙ⟩ | P(x₁, …, xₙ) }</span>.", "Its variables range over domain values rather than over whole tuples.", "Safety confines the result to values drawn from the active domain.", "It is strictly more expressive than relational algebra."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Domain calculus is the value-based counterpart of tuple calculus: the variables are single values, and the result is a set of value-tuples.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The result is a set of value-tuples <span class=\"mm\">⟨x₁, …, xₙ⟩</span> satisfying <span class=\"mm\">P</span> — (A) is true.</li><li>Each <span class=\"mm\">xᵢ</span> ranges over the domain of an attribute, not over tuples — that is the distinction from tuple calculus — (B) is true.</li><li>Safety restricts every variable to the active domain — the set of values actually occurring in the database — which is what makes the result finite — (C) is true.</li><li>Domain calculus, tuple calculus and relational algebra are all equivalent in expressive power (Codd's theorem), so (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing one calculus is stronger than the other. Both are exactly as strong as relational algebra — and all three fall short of transitive closure.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q48", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B) = {(1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 1), (4, 2), (5, 3)}</span>. The number of tuples in <span class=\"mm\">{ ⟨a₁, a₂⟩ | ∃b (R(a₁, b) ∧ R(a₂, b) ∧ a₁ ≠ a₂) }</span> is", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The shared bound variable <span class=\"mm\">b</span> links two occurrences of the same relation — a self-join expressed in calculus. The condition <span class=\"mm\">a₁ ≠ a₂</span> removes the diagonal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Group the tuples by <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">b = 2</span>: <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ {1, 4}</span> → the ordered pairs <span class=\"mm\">(1, 4)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(4, 1)</span> → 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">b = 3</span>: <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ {2, 5}</span> → <span class=\"mm\">(2, 5)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(5, 2)</span> → 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">b = 1</span>: <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ {3}</span> only → no pair with <span class=\"mm\">a₁ ≠ a₂</span> → 0.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">2 + 2 + 0 = 4</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that the answer is ordered. <span class=\"mm\">⟨a₁, a₂⟩</span> and <span class=\"mm\">⟨a₂, a₁⟩</span> are different tuples, so <span class=\"mm\">{1, 4}</span> contributes two, not one. Dropping the <span class=\"mm\">a₁ ≠ a₂</span> condition would add the three diagonal tuples.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q49", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "For union-compatible <span class=\"mm\">R(A, B)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S(A, B)</span>, which tuple calculus expression is equivalent to <span class=\"mm\">R − S</span>?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">{ t | t ∈ R ∧ ¬(t ∈ S) }</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{ t | ¬(t ∈ R) ∧ t ∈ S }</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{ t | t ∈ R ∨ t ∈ S }</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{ t | ¬(t ∈ R ∧ t ∈ S) }</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Translation is compositional: <span class=\"mm\">∧</span> for 'and', <span class=\"mm\">∨</span> for 'or', <span class=\"mm\">¬</span> for 'not', and a membership clause for each relation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>'In <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and not in <span class=\"mm\">S</span>' translates to <span class=\"mm\">t ∈ R ∧ ¬(t ∈ S)</span> — (A).</li><li>(B) is <span class=\"mm\">S − R</span>, the other difference.</li><li>(C) is <span class=\"mm\">R ∪ S</span>.</li><li>(D) is the complement of the intersection — everything except the shared tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the operands: <span class=\"mm\">t ∈ R ∧ ¬(t ∈ S)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">¬(t ∈ R) ∧ t ∈ S</span> look symmetric but are different sets. Order matters in difference.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q50", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which one of the following tuple calculus expressions is <b>safe</b>?", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">{ t | ¬(t ∈ R) }</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{ t | t ∈ R ∨ t[A] &gt; 5 }</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{ t | t ∈ S ∧ ¬(t ∈ R) }</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">{ t | t[A] = 3 }</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Safety is a syntactic test: every free variable must be confined by a positive membership clause in a relation, so the result is drawn from the finite active domain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) has a free <span class=\"mm\">t</span> under a negation with no relation bounding it — it admits every tuple not in <span class=\"mm\">R</span>, an infinite set. Unsafe.</li><li>(B) is a disjunction whose second branch, <span class=\"mm\">t[A] &gt; 5</span>, is unbounded — a <span class=\"mm\">∨</span> is safe only if both branches are. Unsafe.</li><li>(C) bounds <span class=\"mm\">t</span> by the positive clause <span class=\"mm\">t ∈ S</span>; the negation then only filters within that finite set. Safe.</li><li>(D) constrains one attribute and leaves the rest unconstrained — infinitely many tuples satisfy it. Unsafe.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any formula with a negation is unsafe, or that a <span class=\"mm\">∨</span> is safe as long as one branch is bounded. A negated or disjunctive subformula is safe only when something positive in the formula still confines the variable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The practical rule: a variable is confined if some positive membership <span class=\"mm\">t ∈ R</span> appears in the same conjunction. Filtering inside a confined range is always safe.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q01", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following sets contains <b>only</b> Data Definition Language (DDL) statements?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">MERGE</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">CREATE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">ALTER</code>, <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code>, <code class=\"mm\">TRUNCATE</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">GRANT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">REVOKE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">DENY</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">COMMIT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">ROLLBACK</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> SQL statements are grouped by what they act on. <b>DDL</b> defines and changes the <i>schema</i> (<code class=\"mm\">CREATE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">ALTER</code>, <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code>, <code class=\"mm\">TRUNCATE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">RENAME</code>). <b>DML</b> changes the <i>data</i> inside the objects (<code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">MERGE</code>). <b>DCL</b> controls privileges (<code class=\"mm\">GRANT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">REVOKE</code>). <b>TCL</b> controls transactions (<code class=\"mm\">COMMIT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">ROLLBACK</code>, <code class=\"mm\">SAVEPOINT</code>).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Check each word against the four groups: definition, manipulation, control, transaction.</li><li>Option (B) lists four schema-changing statements only — all DDL.</li><li>Option (A) is the DML family; (C) is DCL; (D) mixes a DML query with two transaction-control statements.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE are all DDL.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE and MERGE act on rows, so they are DML.</li><li>(C) GRANT and REVOKE hand out or take back privileges, so they are DCL (DENY is the same family).</li><li>(D) SELECT reads rows (DML/DQL), while COMMIT and ROLLBACK end or undo a transaction (TCL).</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> <code class=\"mm\">TRUNCATE</code> looks like a row operation, so it is often filed under DML. In the SQL standard it is a DDL statement: it drops and re-creates the table's storage rather than deleting rows one at a time, which is why it is far faster than an unqualified <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DDL = definition, DML = data, DCL = privileges, TCL = transactions.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q02", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Table <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> holds 8 rows. Compare <code class=\"mm\">DELETE FROM enroll;</code> with <code class=\"mm\">DROP TABLE enroll;</code>. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> removes the table definition but leaves the rows; <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code> removes the rows.", "Both statements remove the rows and the table definition.", "<code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> removes every row but keeps the table definition; <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code> removes the rows together with the table definition.", "<code class=\"mm\">DROP</code> removes only the rows and leaves the definition; <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> removes the definition."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The row set and the schema object are separate things. <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> is DML: it removes rows from an existing table, and the table keeps its columns, constraints, indexes and privileges. <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code> is DDL: it removes the table object itself from the catalogue, so the rows go with it and any later reference to the name fails.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">DELETE FROM enroll;</code> with no <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> deletes all 8 rows; the table still exists and accepts new inserts.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">DROP TABLE enroll;</code> deletes the definition; the 8 rows disappear because the object that held them is gone.</li><li>A re-created table has no rows and no old constraints — nothing is restored.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is wrong because <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> never touches the definition.</li><li>(A) and (D) invert the two statements.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code> as \"delete the rows\". The distinction is testable: after <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">SELECT * FROM enroll</code> returns an empty result set; after <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code>, the same query raises a \"no such table\" error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DELETE empties, DROP erases.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q03", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In the schema below, <code class=\"mm\">enroll.roll</code> is declared <code class=\"mm\">REFERENCES student(roll) ON DELETE SET NULL</code>. Student 4 has two rows in <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code>. After <code class=\"mm\">DELETE FROM student WHERE roll = 4;</code> what happens to those two rows?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span>", "options": ["Both <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> rows are deleted with the student.", "One row is deleted and the other is set to NULL.", "The delete is rejected, because two rows still reference student 4.", "Both <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> rows remain, with their <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> value set to NULL."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A foreign key with a referential action decides what happens to <i>referencing</i> rows when the <i>referenced</i> row is deleted. The four standard actions are <code class=\"mm\">CASCADE</code> (delete the children), <code class=\"mm\">SET NULL</code> (keep the children, null out the key), <code class=\"mm\">SET DEFAULT</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">NO ACTION</code>/<code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> (reject the delete).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">ON DELETE SET NULL</code> keeps every referencing row and overwrites only the foreign-key column.</li><li>Both of student 4's enrollment rows are referencing rows, so both are kept.</li><li>Their <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> becomes NULL; the <code class=\"mm\">cid</code> and <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> values are untouched.</li><li>The student row itself is removed, so the two orphaned enrollments now have no owner.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes <code class=\"mm\">ON DELETE CASCADE</code>.</li><li>(C) describes <code class=\"mm\">NO ACTION</code> or <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code>, the default when no action is written.</li><li>(B) is not an action any standard system performs.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing \"the delete is refused\" with \"the children are removed\". A foreign key by itself does not prevent a delete; it only prevents leaving a <i>dangling</i> reference. <code class=\"mm\">SET NULL</code> avoids the dangling reference by erasing the value, so the delete is allowed and the child rows survive. Note the column must be nullable for this action to be legal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Foreign key actions: CASCADE deletes children, SET NULL orphans them, RESTRICT refuses the parent delete.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q04", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<code class=\"mm\">student</code> holds 6 rows and <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> holds 8 rows, as below. Two DML statements are then executed, in this order:<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">DELETE FROM enroll WHERE marks IS NULL;\nDELETE FROM student WHERE roll NOT IN (SELECT roll FROM enroll);</span>How many rows remain in <code class=\"mm\">student</code>?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is a two-step DML trace. The first statement is a filtered delete on the child table; the second deletes parent rows that no longer have any child. Because <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> is the primary key of <code class=\"mm\">student</code> it can never be NULL, so the <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> subquery contains no NULL and behaves like an ordinary anti-join.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Step 1 removes the two ungraded enrollments: (2,CS101,NULL) and (5,EC201,NULL). Six enrollment rows remain.</li><li>The rolls still present in <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> are 1, 2, 3 and 4 — note that roll 2 survives through its CS102 row, and roll 5 has now lost its only row.</li><li>Step 2 deletes student rows whose roll is not in {1, 2, 3, 4}: that removes rolls 5 and 6.</li><li>6 − 2 = 4 students remain: rolls 1, 2, 3 and 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 students remain (rolls 1, 2, 3, 4).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Deleting roll 5 in step 1 by mistake, because its enrollment was ungraded. The first statement deletes <i>enrollment</i> rows, not students; roll 5 is removed only in step 2, and only because it has no enrollment left. Also note that a NULL in the <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> subquery would have made step 2 delete nothing at all — it is safe here only because <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> is a key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Trace each statement on the table it names, then re-evaluate the next statement against the new state.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q05", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about SQL constraints and privileges are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> constraint rejects a row whose checked expression evaluates to UNKNOWN.", "<code class=\"mm\">GRANT SELECT ON student TO analyst WITH GRANT OPTION;</code> lets <code class=\"mm\">analyst</code> pass that same privilege on to other users.", "In standard SQL a <code class=\"mm\">UNIQUE</code> constraint permits several rows that have NULL in the unique column.", "A column declared <code class=\"mm\">PRIMARY KEY</code> may contain NULL provided the table also has a <code class=\"mm\">UNIQUE</code> constraint on the same column."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three separate rules are being tested: how <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> treats UNKNOWN, what <code class=\"mm\">WITH GRANT OPTION</code> adds, and how the standard treats NULL inside <code class=\"mm\">UNIQUE</code> and <code class=\"mm\">PRIMARY KEY</code>. A constraint is violated only when its predicate is <i>false</i>; UNKNOWN (from a NULL operand) satisfies it. NULL is never equal to NULL, so two NULLs cannot duplicate each other in a unique column. A primary key, by definition, may not be NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) A <code class=\"mm\">CHECK (marks &gt;= 0)</code> on a NULL <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> evaluates to UNKNOWN, which is accepted — so the row is inserted. The statement is false.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">WITH GRANT OPTION</code> is exactly the right to re-grant. True.</li><li>(C) The unique constraint is violated only when two rows agree on every constrained column with non-NULL values; NULLs are distinct, so many NULLs may coexist. True (SQL Server is the well-known deviation, allowing only one).</li><li>(D) Entity integrity forbids NULL in any primary-key column, whatever other constraints exist. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B) and (C) are true.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading a <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> as \"must be true\". SQL uses three-valued logic, so a constraint fires only on FALSE; UNKNOWN passes. This is why <code class=\"mm\">CHECK (marks IN (0,1))</code> does not stop a NULL from being inserted, and why a \"not equal\" check such as <code class=\"mm\">CHECK (a &lt;&gt; b)</code> still admits rows where either side is NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Constraints reject FALSE, not UNKNOWN; NULLs are distinct in UNIQUE; a primary key is never NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q06", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the schema below, which query returns exactly the students who have <b>not</b> enrolled in any course?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">SELECT roll FROM student s WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = s.roll);</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">SELECT roll FROM student s WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = s.roll);</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">SELECT roll FROM student WHERE roll NOT IN (SELECT roll FROM enroll WHERE marks IS NOT NULL);</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">SELECT roll FROM student WHERE roll &lt;&gt; ALL (SELECT roll FROM enroll WHERE marks IS NULL);</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is the standard \"for no matching row\" test. The inner query is correlated on <code class=\"mm\">s.roll</code>, so for each student the database asks whether any enrollment row exists; <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> keeps the student only when the answer is \"no row\". Unlike <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> it is safe in the presence of NULLs, because it never compares values — it only tests whether a row was produced.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For roll 1 the inner query returns two rows, so <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is false and roll 1 is dropped.</li><li>For roll 6 the inner query returns no row at all, so <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is true and roll 6 is kept.</li><li>Applying this to every student leaves exactly roll 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — only roll 6 (Farhan) has no enrollment.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the complement: it returns the five students who <i>are</i> enrolled.</li><li>(C) filters the subquery to graded rows, so the enrolled rolls it sees are {1, 2, 3, 4}. Roll 5 is absent from that set even though it <i>is</i> enrolled, so the query wrongly returns rolls 5 and 6.</li><li>(D) asks for rolls that differ from every <i>ungraded</i> enrollment roll ({2, 5}), which returns 1, 3, 4 and 6 — the opposite of the intended set.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> are interchangeable. They agree only when the inner column is declared NOT NULL. When a NULL can appear, <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> silently returns the empty set while <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> still answers the intended question.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For \"has no related row\", prefer NOT EXISTS: it is NULL-safe and reads as a universal test.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q07", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A subquery refers to a column of the table named in the enclosing query. Which statement about such a subquery is TRUE?", "options": ["It must appear in the <code class=\"mm\">FROM</code> clause; a correlated reference is illegal in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code>.", "It is an independent subquery, evaluated exactly once and then reused for every outer row.", "It is a correlated subquery, and conceptually it is evaluated again for each candidate row of the outer query.", "It cannot contain an aggregate function."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A subquery that mentions an outer column is <b>correlated</b>. Its result depends on the current outer row, so the conceptual model is: for each candidate outer row, bind the outer columns, run the inner query, then test the predicate. An <b>uncorrelated</b> subquery mentions only its own tables, so it can be computed once and its result reused — which is why optimisers try to decorrelate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Spot the outer reference: here <code class=\"mm\">s.roll</code> inside the inner query.</li><li>Because the inner result changes with each outer row, it is re-evaluated per row in the conceptual model.</li><li>That is the definition of a correlated subquery.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes an uncorrelated (independent) subquery.</li><li>(A) is false: correlation is legal in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code>, in the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list and in <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code>.</li><li>(D) is false: a correlated aggregate such as <code class=\"mm\">(SELECT AVG(marks) FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = s.roll)</code> is perfectly legal.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a correlated subquery always costs one full scan per row. That is the naive plan; most engines rewrite it into a semi-join or an index lookup, so the practical cost can be close to a single join.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Outer reference present = correlated = per-row semantics; absent = uncorrelated = compute once.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q08", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Suppose the <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> column of <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> is nullable and the subquery below returns at least one NULL.<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT roll FROM student\n WHERE roll NOT IN (SELECT roll FROM enroll);</span>What does the outer query return?", "options": ["Every student roll that is absent from the non-NULL values of the subquery.", "No rows at all.", "Every student roll, including those present in the subquery.", "A syntax error, because NULL cannot appear in an <code class=\"mm\">IN</code> list."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">x NOT IN (a, b, NULL)</code> expands to <code class=\"mm\">x &lt;&gt; a AND x &lt;&gt; b AND x &lt;&gt; NULL</code>. The last conjunct is UNKNOWN for every <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">TRUE AND UNKNOWN = UNKNOWN</code>. A <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause keeps a row only when the predicate is TRUE, so no row qualifies — even a roll that matches nothing in the list.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Rewrite <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> as a chain of <code class=\"mm\">&lt;&gt;</code> comparisons joined by <code class=\"mm\">AND</code>.</li><li>One comparison is against NULL, which yields UNKNOWN.</li><li>UNKNOWN <code class=\"mm\">AND</code> anything is never TRUE, so the whole predicate can never be TRUE.</li><li>The result set is therefore empty.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — the query returns no rows.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is what the query would return if the subquery contained no NULL; the NULL destroys it.</li><li>(C) is the opposite of what happens: UNKNOWN fails, it does not pass.</li><li>(D) is false: NULL is a legal value in a subquery result; the failure is semantic, not syntactic.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> as set difference. Set difference ignores NULLs; <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> propagates them. The fix is either to add <code class=\"mm\">WHERE roll IS NOT NULL</code> to the subquery, or to rewrite the query as <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code>, which never compares values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NOT IN + a NULL in the subquery = empty result. Use NOT EXISTS or filter the NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q09", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about SQL subqueries and quantified comparisons are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">x = ANY (S)</code> returns the same truth value as <code class=\"mm\">x IN (S)</code> for every subquery <code class=\"mm\">S</code> whose result is not NULL-valued.", "<code class=\"mm\">x &gt; ALL (S)</code> is TRUE when the subquery <code class=\"mm\">S</code> returns no rows at all.", "A correlated subquery written in the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list is conceptually evaluated once per row of the result.", "<code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> always return the same rows."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Quantified comparisons are shorthand for a fold: <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code> is an OR over equality (that is <code class=\"mm\">IN</code>), and <code class=\"mm\">&gt; ALL</code> is an AND over the comparisons. An OR over an empty set is FALSE, so <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code> on an empty subquery is false; an AND over an empty set is TRUE, so <code class=\"mm\">&gt; ALL</code> on an empty subquery is vacuously true.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code> ≡ <code class=\"mm\">IN</code>, by definition of the quantifier. True.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">x &gt; ALL (empty)</code> has no counterexample, so it holds vacuously. True — this is the \"every student scored more than the best student of an empty class\" case.</li><li>(C) a <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code>-list subquery correlated on the outer row must be recomputed for each output row. True.</li><li>(D) false: they diverge as soon as the inner column can be NULL, where <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> collapses to the empty set and <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> does not.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C) are true; (D) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">&gt; ALL</code> needs a non-empty subquery. The empty case is exactly where GATE-style questions aim: universal quantification over an empty domain is TRUE, existential quantification over it is FALSE.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> = ANY is IN, <> ALL is NOT IN, > ALL over an empty set is vacuously true.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q10", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the schema and data below, how many students does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s\n WHERE (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enroll e\n         WHERE e.roll = s.roll\n           AND e.marks > (SELECT AVG(marks) FROM enroll e2 WHERE e2.cid = e.cid)) >= 2;</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The query is a doubly nested correlated count. For a given student, the middle query walks that student's enrollments; for each of them the innermost query recomputes the average marks of <i>that course</i>. A NULL <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> never satisfies <code class=\"mm\">&gt;</code>, so ungraded courses simply do not contribute.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Course averages over the non-NULL marks: CS101 = (88 + 95)/2 = 91.5; CS102 = (74 + 65 + 80)/3 = 73; EC201 = 91/1 = 91.</li><li>Roll 1: 88 &gt; 91.5 is false, 74 &gt; 73 is true → count 1.</li><li>Roll 2: CS101 is NULL (not counted), 65 &gt; 73 is false → count 0.</li><li>Roll 3: 91 &gt; 91 is false → count 0.</li><li>Roll 4: 95 &gt; 91.5 is true, 80 &gt; 73 is true → count 2.</li><li>Roll 5: its only mark is NULL → count 0. Roll 6 has no enrollment rows, so the inner count is 0.</li><li>Only roll 4 reaches the threshold of 2, so the outer <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> is 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 student (roll 4, Divya).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps sit here. First, <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code> ignores NULLs rather than treating them as 0, so CS101 averages 91.5 and not 61. Second, the threshold is \"at least 2 courses\", so roll 1 with a single qualifying course must be excluded — a count of 1 is not a pass.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Resolve innermost first: per-course average, then per-student comparison, then the outer count.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q11", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, what does the query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(marks) FROM enroll;</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>", "options": ["8 and 2", "8 and 8", "6 and 6", "8 and 6"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts <i>rows</i> and never looks at column values, so NULLs are included. <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(expr)</code> counts only the rows where <code class=\"mm\">expr</code> is not NULL. Every other aggregate (<code class=\"mm\">SUM</code>, <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code>, <code class=\"mm\">MIN</code>, <code class=\"mm\">MAX</code>) also ignores NULLs; only <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> holds 8 rows, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> = 8.</li><li>Two rows have <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> NULL — (2,CS101) and (5,EC201) — so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(marks)</code> = 8 − 2 = 6.</li><li>The pair is therefore (8, 6).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — 8 and 6.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) would need every row to be graded.</li><li>(C) would be the answer if <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> also skipped NULL rows.</li><li>(A) reports the number of NULL marks instead of the number of non-NULL ones.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(marks)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> are the same. The gap between them is precisely the number of NULLs, which is why <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*) - COUNT(col)</code> is a standard idiom for counting missing values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(col) counts non-NULL values of col.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q12", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which statement about <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> and <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> is TRUE?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> filters individual rows before grouping; <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> filters whole groups after aggregation and may contain aggregate functions.", "<code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> filters individual rows before grouping; <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> filters groups after aggregation.", "Both clauses may contain aggregate functions and are interchangeable.", "<code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> may contain aggregate functions but <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> may not."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> SQL evaluates a grouped query in a fixed order: <code class=\"mm\">FROM</code> → <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> → <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> → <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> → <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> → <code class=\"mm\">ORDER BY</code>. Because <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> runs before the groups exist, it cannot refer to an aggregate; because <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> runs after grouping, it can.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> sees one row at a time, so its predicates use plain columns.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> then collapses rows into groups.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> tests the group, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">SUM(marks)</code> and friends are legal there.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses the order of the two clauses.</li><li>(C) is false: an aggregate in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> is a syntax error in every major system.</li><li>(D) inverts the rule.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Trying to filter on a group with <code class=\"mm\">WHERE COUNT(*) &gt; 2</code>. The engine rejects it; the same condition belongs in <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code>. A useful memory hook: <b>HAVING</b> is to groups what <b>WHERE</b> is to rows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Row filter → WHERE; group filter → HAVING.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q13", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, what does the query return (averages shown to one decimal place)?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT dept, AVG(cgpa) FROM student\n GROUP BY dept\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>", "options": ["Three rows: CSE, ECE and MECH", "Two rows: CSE 8.8 and ECE 7.5", "One row: CSE 8.8", "Two rows: CSE 9.1 and ECE 7.8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The query groups by department, computes each group's average, and then keeps only the groups whose <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> exceeds 1. The <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> test is applied to the group, so a department with a single student disappears even though its average is perfectly well defined.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Group and count: CSE has 3 students, ECE has 2, MECH has 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code> keeps CSE and ECE and drops MECH.</li><li>CSE average = (9.1 + 8.4 + 8.9)/3 = 26.4/3 = 8.8.</li><li>ECE average = (7.8 + 7.2)/2 = 15.0/2 = 7.5.</li><li>Two rows are returned.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — CSE 8.8 and ECE 7.5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) keeps MECH, whose group has only one row and therefore fails the <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> test.</li><li>(C) drops ECE, whose count is 2 and so satisfies <code class=\"mm\">&gt; 1</code>.</li><li>(D) reports the first value of each group instead of the group average.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> filters rows. It filters groups: a department with one student is removed as a group, not the student removed from the average.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> GROUP BY partitions, aggregates summarise, HAVING keeps or drops whole partitions.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q14", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, what single value does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT AVG(marks) FROM enroll WHERE cid = 'CS101';</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>Give the exact value (one decimal place).", "options": [], "answer_display": "91.5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">AVG(expr)</code> is defined as <code class=\"mm\">SUM(expr) / COUNT(expr)</code>. Both aggregates skip NULL, so the denominator is the number of <i>non-NULL</i> values, not the number of rows. That is the whole point of the question: the ungraded row is left out of both the numerator and the denominator.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">cid = 'CS101'</code> selects three rows: (1,CS101,88), (2,CS101,NULL) and (4,CS101,95).</li><li>The NULL row is excluded, so <code class=\"mm\">SUM(marks)</code> = 88 + 95 = 183 and <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(marks)</code> = 2.</li><li>AVG = 183 / 2 = 91.5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 91.5</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing by the row count, 183/3 = 61. That is the value you get if NULL is treated as 0 — SQL never does that for <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code>. If the intent really is \"treat ungraded as zero\", the question must say <code class=\"mm\">AVG(COALESCE(marks,0))</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AVG ignores NULLs in both the sum and the count; the denominator is COUNT(col).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q15", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about grouping and aggregate functions are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">SELECT dept, name FROM student GROUP BY dept;</code> is invalid in standard SQL, because <code class=\"mm\">name</code> is neither grouped nor aggregated.", "A <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause may not contain an aggregate function, but a <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> clause may.", "<code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> ignores rows in which some column is NULL.", "An aggregate query with no <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> returns exactly one row even when the table is empty."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> After <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code>, the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list may contain only (i) grouped columns, (ii) aggregates and (iii) expressions built from those two. Anything else has no single value per group. Separately, an aggregate query with no <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> defines one implicit group covering the whole table — and a group over zero rows still exists, so one row is produced.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">name</code> varies inside a <code class=\"mm\">dept</code> group, so the standard rejects the query. True. (MySQL with <code class=\"mm\">ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY</code> disabled returns an arbitrary name, which is a vendor extension, not the standard.)</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> is evaluated before grouping, so an aggregate there is meaningless; <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> is evaluated after grouping. True.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts rows regardless of NULLs anywhere in them. False.</li><li>(D) the implicit single group exists even for an empty table: <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> = 0 and <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code>/<code class=\"mm\">AVG</code> return NULL, but one row is returned. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D) are true; (C) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting an empty aggregate query to return no rows. It returns one row — the empty-group row — which is why <code class=\"mm\">SELECT MAX(marks) FROM enroll WHERE 1=0</code> yields a single NULL rather than nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Grouped SELECT may only use grouped columns and aggregates; the no-GROUP-BY aggregate always emits one row.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q16", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, how many rows does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT * FROM student s LEFT JOIN enroll e ON s.roll = e.roll;</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>", "options": ["6", "8", "9", "14"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <b>left outer join</b> emits one row for every matching pair, plus one <i>NULL-extended</i> row for each left row that found no match. So the count is (number of matching pairs) + (number of unmatched left rows).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Matching pairs: roll 1 contributes 2, roll 2 contributes 2, roll 3 contributes 1, roll 4 contributes 2, roll 5 contributes 1 — 8 pairs in total, the whole of <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code>.</li><li>Unmatched left rows: roll 6 (Farhan) has no enrollment, so it contributes 1 row with <code class=\"mm\">cid</code> and <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> NULL.</li><li>8 + 1 = 9 rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 9 rows.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the inner-join count: an inner join returns only the 8 matched pairs and silently drops roll 6.</li><li>(A) is the number of students, not the number of joined rows.</li><li>(D) would be the count if the join were many-to-many across a larger table; here the total is bounded by 8 + 6 = 14 only in the degenerate case where every enrollment failed to match.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming an outer join adds one row per left row. It adds one row only for <i>unmatched</i> left rows; matched rows are duplicated exactly as an inner join would duplicate them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> LEFT JOIN row count = matched pairs + unmatched left rows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q17", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, how many rows does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT * FROM student, course;</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>", "options": ["18", "9", "6", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A comma-separated <code class=\"mm\">FROM</code> list with no join condition is a <b>Cartesian product</b>: every row of the first table is paired with every row of the second. The size is the product of the two row counts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">student</code> has 6 rows and <code class=\"mm\">course</code> has 3 rows.</li><li>No <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause removes any pairing.</li><li>6 × 3 = 18 rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 18 rows.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is 6 + 3, the sum of the row counts rather than the product.</li><li>(C) and (D) are the individual table sizes.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the Cartesian product with a natural join. The product has <code class=\"mm\">|R| × |S|</code> rows; a natural join on a common attribute collapses the pairs that agree, and here the two tables share no attribute name at all, so even <code class=\"mm\">NATURAL JOIN</code> would degenerate to the product.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> No join condition = cross product = product of the cardinalities.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q18", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, what does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s\n  LEFT JOIN enroll e ON s.roll = e.roll\n WHERE e.roll IS NULL;</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>", "options": ["8", "0", "6", "1"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is the standard <b>anti-join</b> idiom: left-join, then keep only the NULL-extended rows. After the outer join, <code class=\"mm\">e.roll</code> is NULL exactly for the left rows that found no match, so the <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> predicate isolates the students with no enrollment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The left join produces 9 rows, of which 8 are matched and 1 is the NULL-extended row for roll 6.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">e.roll IS NULL</code> is true only on that single row.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> = 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — 1 (only roll 6).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) would be the answer if every student had at least one enrollment.</li><li>(C) counts all students, ignoring the join.</li><li>(A) is the size of <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing <code class=\"mm\">WHERE e.roll IS NULL</code> inside an <i>inner</i> join and expecting the same answer. With an inner join the NULL-extended rows never exist, so the predicate can never be true and the result is always empty — the outer join is what makes the test meaningful.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Anti-join = LEFT JOIN … WHERE right.key IS NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q19", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about outer joins are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> condition of a left outer join also filters out left rows that fail to match.", "A predicate on a right-table column placed in the <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause of a left outer join can remove the NULL-extended rows and so reduce the result to that of an inner join.", "A <code class=\"mm\">FULL OUTER JOIN</code> returns unmatched rows from both sides, padding the other side with NULLs.", "<code class=\"mm\">A LEFT JOIN B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B RIGHT JOIN A</code> produce the same set of result rows (with the same column pairs) when written with the same join condition."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An outer join preserves one or both sides. <code class=\"mm\">LEFT</code> preserves the left input, <code class=\"mm\">RIGHT</code> preserves the right, <code class=\"mm\">FULL</code> preserves both. Swapping the operands therefore swaps which side is preserved, and the two results describe the same pairs. The <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> condition decides <i>matching</i>; the <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause is applied <i>after</i> the NULL extension, so a WHERE predicate can undo the preservation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(D) swapping the operands of a left join turns it into the mirror-image right join with identical pairs. True.</li><li>(B) if the WHERE clause requires, say, <code class=\"mm\">e.marks &gt; 70</code>, the NULL-extended rows evaluate to UNKNOWN and are discarded, leaving exactly the inner-join rows. True — this is the classic \"outer join that is really an inner join\" bug.</li><li>(C) is the definition of a full outer join. True.</li><li>(A) false: a left row that fails the <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> condition is precisely the row that is kept, padded with NULLs.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (D), (B) and (C) are true; (A) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Putting a right-table predicate in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> and expecting NULL-padded rows to survive. They cannot: a NULL never satisfies a comparison. To filter the <i>matching</i> without losing the unmatched rows, the predicate must move into the <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> clause.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> ON decides matches, WHERE prunes the final rows — and that is how a left join silently becomes an inner join.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q20", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the schema and data below, what does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s\n  JOIN enroll e ON e.roll = s.roll\n  JOIN course c ON c.cid = e.cid\n WHERE s.dept = 'CSE' AND c.credits = 4;</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A three-table equi-join is a chain of matches: the student must match an enrollment, and that enrollment must match a course. <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> then counts <i>rows</i> of the joined result, so a student with two qualifying enrollments contributes two, not one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>CSE students are rolls 1, 2 and 4.</li><li>Their enrollment rows: roll 1 has CS101 and CS102; roll 2 has CS101 and CS102; roll 4 has CS101 and CS102 — six rows in total.</li><li>All six reference CS101 or CS102, and both of those courses have <code class=\"mm\">credits = 4</code>, so the third join and the <code class=\"mm\">credits</code> predicate keep all six.</li><li>No EC201 enrollment belongs to a CSE student, so nothing is filtered further. The answer is 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3 (the number of CSE students) or 2 (the number of courses). The query counts joined rows, so the grain is (student, enrollment, course) — one row per enrollment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> COUNT(*) after a join counts the joined rows; check the grain before you answer.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q21", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which statement about an SQL view is TRUE?", "options": ["A view always stores a private copy of the rows it returns, refreshed on every insert into the base table.", "A view is a named query: it stores no rows of its own (unless it is a materialised view) and its rows are recomputed from the base tables when the view is queried.", "A view must be defined over exactly one base table.", "Dropping a view deletes the rows of the underlying table."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In standard SQL a view is a <i>derived</i> table: the catalogue stores only the defining query. Every reference to the view expands to that query, so the view always reflects the current base tables. A <b>materialised</b> view is the exception — it does keep a stored copy and must be refreshed — but that is an optimisation feature, not what a plain <code class=\"mm\">CREATE VIEW</code> gives you.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The definition is stored as text; no tuples are copied.</li><li>Querying the view runs (or rewrites) the defining query against the base tables.</li><li>Therefore the view is always consistent with the base data, with no refresh step.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a materialised view, and even then the refresh is explicit or scheduled, not automatic on every insert.</li><li>(C) is false: a view may join any number of tables, as the aggregate view in this unit does.</li><li>(D) is false: <code class=\"mm\">DROP VIEW</code> removes only the definition; the base tables and their rows are untouched.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a view is a snapshot. Because it is recomputed, a view over <code class=\"mm\">student</code> immediately shows a newly inserted student — there is nothing to refresh.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A view is stored logic, not stored data.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q22", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which statement about an SQL trigger is TRUE?", "options": ["A trigger fires only for <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> statements.", "A trigger must be called explicitly, for example with <code class=\"mm\">EXECUTE TRIGGER</code>, after the statement it is meant to guard.", "A trigger fires automatically when its specified event (such as an <code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code> or <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>) occurs; it cannot be invoked directly by a statement.", "A table that has triggers may not also have constraints."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A trigger is an <b>event–condition–action</b> rule attached to a table or view. The database fires it as a side effect of the triggering statement; there is no SQL statement that calls a trigger by name. Triggers are declared <code class=\"mm\">BEFORE</code> or <code class=\"mm\">AFTER</code> the event, and <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH ROW</code> or <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH STATEMENT</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The event is a DML change (in standard SQL), optionally restricted with <code class=\"mm\">WHEN</code> to specific columns.</li><li>The action is a block of SQL executed by the engine when the event occurs.</li><li>No user statement names the trigger, so it cannot be called directly.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) there is no such statement in standard SQL.</li><li>(A) triggers are defined on data-changing events; some systems add <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> triggers on views, but a plain <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> does not fire one.</li><li>(D) triggers and constraints are independent mechanisms and routinely coexist; constraints are usually preferred for declarative rules because they are cheaper.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a trigger replaces a foreign key. Triggers can enforce rules that constraints cannot express, but they are procedural, harder to reason about, and a well-modelled schema uses constraints first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Triggers are fired by the engine, never called by the user.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q23", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the schema below, which of the following views is updatable in the standard sense — that is, <code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code> and <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> through the view can be mapped onto the single base table it reads?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span>", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT roll, name, dept FROM student WHERE dept = 'CSE';</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT dept, COUNT(*) AS n FROM student GROUP BY dept;</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT DISTINCT dept FROM student;</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT s.roll, AVG(e.marks) AS avgmarks FROM student s JOIN enroll e ON e.roll = s.roll GROUP BY s.roll;</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A view is updatable when each of its rows corresponds to exactly one row of exactly one base table. Selection and projection preserve that correspondence; aggregation, <code class=\"mm\">DISTINCT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> and joins do not, because one view row can then be derived from many base rows (or from none).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) reads one table, keeps its key column <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> and only filters rows, so every view row maps back to one student row. Updatable.</li><li>(B) produces one row per department; no single student row corresponds to it. Not updatable.</li><li>(C) collapses duplicates, so a view row has no unique source row. Not updatable.</li><li>(D) aggregates over a join; again one row summarises many. Not updatable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B), (C) and (D) are all <b>read-only</b> views under the standard updatability rules. A system may still let you modify them through an <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> trigger, but that is a workaround, not updatability.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a view is read-only whenever it has a <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause. A selection predicate is harmless — in fact, with <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> it becomes an enforced constraint on what may be written through the view.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One view row ↔ one base row is what makes a view updatable; aggregation and DISTINCT break it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q24", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "The view below is created over the data shown. How many rows does <code class=\"mm\">SELECT * FROM v WHERE n &gt; 1;</code> return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">CREATE VIEW v AS\n  SELECT dept, COUNT(*) AS n FROM student GROUP BY dept;</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:\"Consolas\",\"DejaVu Sans Mono\",monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL)</span>", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The view materialises the grouping as an ordinary table of two columns, which can then be filtered like any other table. The <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> on the view is applied <i>after</i> the view's own <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code>, so it filters groups, not students.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The view has three rows: CSE with n = 3, ECE with n = 2 and MECH with n = 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">n &gt; 1</code> keeps CSE and ECE.</li><li>The result has 2 rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 rows (CSE with 3, ECE with 2).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the query as \"count the students in departments with more than one student\", which would give 5. The outer query counts nothing — it returns the view's rows, so the answer is the number of qualifying groups.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A view over an aggregate is a table of groups; filtering it filters groups.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q25", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about views, triggers and constraints are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["With <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> on a view, an insert or update performed through the view is rejected if the new row would not satisfy the view's defining condition.", "Dropping a base table that a view references is always rejected by the DBMS.", "A trigger declared <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH ROW</code> fires once for every row that the triggering statement affects.", "A <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> constraint may be declared directly on a view in standard SQL."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three different mechanisms are being separated. <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> makes the view's predicate a write-time constraint. Triggers are declared at row granularity or statement granularity. <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> is a table constraint; the standard mechanism for a cross-table rule is an assertion, which no mainstream product implements.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition of <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code>: a row that would vanish from the view cannot be written through it. True.</li><li>(B) false: the standard permits the drop and leaves the view invalid (\"inoperative\") until it is re-created or the table returns; some systems require <code class=\"mm\">CASCADE</code>, others simply fail at the next use of the view. It is not universally rejected.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH ROW</code> means one firing per affected row; <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH STATEMENT</code> fires once for the whole statement. True.</li><li>(D) false: <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> is declared on a table or column, not on a view.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C) are true; (B) and (D) are false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. First, assuming <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> only affects <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> — it affects writes. Second, assuming a trigger fires once per statement by default; the row granularity has to be read from the declaration, and <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH ROW</code> is the more common form.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CHECK OPTION guards writes through a view; FOR EACH ROW sets trigger granularity; CHECK belongs to tables.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q26", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Table <code class=\"mm\">orders</code> is declared as <code class=\"mm\">orders(oid INT PRIMARY KEY, qty INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 5, note VARCHAR(20))</code>. The statement <code class=\"mm\">INSERT INTO orders (oid, note) VALUES (7, NULL);</code> is then executed. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["It is rejected, because <code class=\"mm\">qty</code> is <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> and no value was supplied for it.", "It is accepted: <code class=\"mm\">qty</code> receives the declared default 5, and <code class=\"mm\">note</code> is stored as NULL.", "It is accepted, and <code class=\"mm\">qty</code> is stored as NULL because the column was left out of the column list.", "It is rejected, because <code class=\"mm\">note</code> may not be given a NULL."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A column that is omitted from an <code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code> column list takes its declared <code class=\"mm\">DEFAULT</code>, and a declared default satisfies <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code>. <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> forbids only an explicit NULL or an omitted column that has no default; it never forbids a default value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The statement names two columns, <code class=\"mm\">oid</code> and <code class=\"mm\">note</code>; <code class=\"mm\">qty</code> is not named.</li><li>An unnamed column is filled from its <code class=\"mm\">DEFAULT</code> clause, here 5, so <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> is satisfied.</li><li>NULL is written explicitly into <code class=\"mm\">note</code>, which is nullable because no constraint forbids it.</li><li>The row (7, 5, NULL) is inserted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — the row is accepted with qty = 5 and note = NULL.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) confuses an omitted column with a missing value; the default supplies one.</li><li>(C) would hold only if <code class=\"mm\">qty</code> had no <code class=\"mm\">DEFAULT</code>, in which case the insert would be rejected rather than storing NULL.</li><li>(D) invents a constraint on <code class=\"mm\">note</code>; the declaration makes it nullable.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> as \"every insert must name the column\". It only says the stored value may not be NULL, and the default guarantees that.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Omitted column with a DEFAULT → default value, never NULL and never an error.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q27", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, the statement <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE enroll SET marks = marks + 5 WHERE cid = 'C3';</code> is executed. Which statement is TRUE?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span>", "options": ["Both rows of course C3 change: 68 becomes 73 and the NULL becomes 5.", "The statement is rejected, because one row of C3 has a NULL mark.", "The graded row becomes 73, and the row whose mark is NULL stays NULL.", "The row whose mark is NULL is deleted, because NULL + 5 cannot be computed."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Any arithmetic with NULL yields NULL, so <code class=\"mm\">NULL + 5</code> is NULL. An <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code> writes that NULL back, which is a legal value in a nullable column. The <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause tests only <code class=\"mm\">cid</code>, so both C3 rows are still selected.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Rows selected by <code class=\"mm\">WHERE cid = 'C3'</code>: (12,C3,68) and (16,C3,NULL).</li><li>(12,C3,68) → 68 + 5 = 73.</li><li>(16,C3,NULL) → NULL + 5 = NULL, so the column is set to NULL again.</li><li>Two rows are updated; one value changes, the other is unchanged.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 73 and NULL.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) treats NULL as 0, which SQL never does for arithmetic.</li><li>(B) is wrong: a NULL operand is not an error, only an UNKNOWN value.</li><li>(D) is wrong: no statement deletes rows unless it is a <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating NULL as zero. <code class=\"mm\">marks + 5</code> on a NULL mark is NULL, not 5 — a value is only turned into 0 by an explicit <code class=\"mm\">COALESCE(marks,0)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NULL propagates through arithmetic; the row is still updated, to NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q28", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<code class=\"mm\">u1</code> holds <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> on <code class=\"mm\">student</code> <code class=\"mm\">WITH GRANT OPTION</code> and has granted <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> on it to <code class=\"mm\">u2</code>. The DBA now runs <code class=\"mm\">REVOKE SELECT ON student FROM u1 RESTRICT;</code>. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The statement succeeds, and <code class=\"mm\">u2</code> keeps the privilege.", "The statement succeeds, and <code class=\"mm\">u2</code> loses the privilege as well.", "The statement fails, but <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> still loses the privilege.", "The statement fails, because <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> has passed the privilege on; <code class=\"mm\">REVOKE ... CASCADE</code> would succeed and take it away from both <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">u2</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A privilege that has been granted onward creates a dependency. <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> refuses to revoke a privilege that others still hold through it; <code class=\"mm\">CASCADE</code> revokes the privilege from the grantee and, transitively, from everyone who received it from that grantee.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">u1</code> was granted <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> <code class=\"mm\">WITH GRANT OPTION</code>, so its grant to <code class=\"mm\">u2</code> is a dependent grant.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> is the default: it fails rather than silently changing <code class=\"mm\">u2</code>'s rights.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">CASCADE</code> removes the privilege from <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> and follows the dependency to <code class=\"mm\">u2</code>.</li><li>Nothing is revoked when the <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> form fails, so <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> keeps the privilege.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — RESTRICT fails; CASCADE removes it from both users.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) both assume <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> succeeds, which it does not when a dependent grant exists.</li><li>(C) invents a partial effect; a failed <code class=\"mm\">REVOKE</code> changes nothing at all.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> to revoke <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> and leave <code class=\"mm\">u2</code> dangling. The statement is atomic: it either succeeds completely or not at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> RESTRICT protects dependent grants by failing; CASCADE is the deliberate recursive revoke.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q29", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A schema declares both foreign keys with <code class=\"mm\">ON DELETE CASCADE</code>:<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">dept(dno)          -- primary key\nemp(eno, dno)      -- dno REFERENCES dept(dno) ON DELETE CASCADE\ndependent(did, eno) -- eno REFERENCES emp(eno) ON DELETE CASCADE</span>and holds the data<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">dept:      1, 2, 3\nemp:       (101,1)  (102,1)  (103,2)  (104,3)\ndependent: (1,101)  (2,101)  (3,103)</span>The statement <code class=\"mm\">DELETE FROM dept WHERE dno = 1;</code> is executed. How many rows are removed from <code class=\"mm\">emp</code> and <code class=\"mm\">dependent</code> together? (Count only those two tables.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A cascade chain is followed transitively. Deleting a <code class=\"mm\">dept</code> row cascades to its <code class=\"mm\">emp</code> rows, and deleting an <code class=\"mm\">emp</code> row in turn cascades to its <code class=\"mm\">dependent</code> rows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Delete dept 1 → cascade to emp rows with dno = 1: 101 and 102. That is 2 rows of <code class=\"mm\">emp</code>.</li><li>Each deleted emp row cascades to its dependents: emp 101 has dependents 1 and 2; emp 102 has none. That is 2 rows of <code class=\"mm\">dependent</code>.</li><li>Dept 2 and dept 3 are untouched, so emp 103, 104 and dependent 3 survive.</li><li>2 + 2 = 4 rows removed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the direct children (2 emp rows) and forgetting that the cascade continues to the grandchildren.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CASCADE is transitive; follow the chain until no referencing row remains.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q30", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about DML, DDL and privileges are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["If an <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code> would violate a <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> constraint for even one of the rows it touches, none of those rows is updated — the statement is atomic.", "An <code class=\"mm\">INSERT INTO t SELECT ...</code> that fails on the last of five rows leaves the first four rows inserted.", "<code class=\"mm\">DELETE FROM t;</code> with no <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> removes every row but keeps the table definition and the privileges granted on it.", "<code class=\"mm\">DROP TABLE t;</code> also removes the privileges that were granted on <code class=\"mm\">t</code>."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two separate principles are at work. A single SQL statement is atomic: it either has its full effect or none. And privileges are attached to the object, so destroying the object destroys the privileges that depended on it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Constraint checking is part of the statement, so a violation anywhere rolls the whole statement back.</li><li>(B) False. Statement atomicity means the four earlier rows are undone with the fifth.</li><li>(C) True. <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> is DML: the rows go, the object and its grants stay.</li><li>(D) True. A grant is a privilege on a named object; once the object no longer exists the grant has nothing to attach to.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D) are true; (B) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the classic partial-effect trap: only an explicit loop of separate statements can leave earlier rows behind.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a multi-row DML statement commits row by row. It does not; the whole statement succeeds or the whole statement is undone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One statement = all or nothing; dropping the object drops the grants on it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q31", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, what does the following query produce for roll 14, the student with no row in <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code>?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT s.roll,\n       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = s.roll) AS n\nFROM student s;</span>", "options": ["Roll 14 is left out of the result entirely.", "Roll 14 appears with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> set to NULL.", "Roll 14 appears with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> = 0, because <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> over no rows is 0.", "The query is rejected, because a correlated subquery may not appear in the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An aggregate without <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> always returns exactly one row, even when it sees no input rows. <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> over an empty set is 0, so the correlated subquery yields 0 rather than NULL or no row. A scalar subquery in the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list is legal as long as it returns at most one row.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For roll 14 the inner query is <code class=\"mm\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = 14</code>, which matches nothing.</li><li>An aggregate query over zero rows still produces one row: the count of an empty set is 0.</li><li>The outer row for roll 14 therefore survives, with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> = 0.</li><li>Rolls 11, 13 and 16 get 2, 2 and 2; rolls 12 and 15 get 2 each as well — every enrolled student has exactly two rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — roll 14 appears with n = 0.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a join or an <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> filter, not a scalar subquery in the select list.</li><li>(B) would be right for <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code> or <code class=\"mm\">MAX</code>, which return NULL over no rows; <code class=\"mm\">COUNT</code> does not.</li><li>(D) is false: correlation in the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list is standard and is how per-row aggregates are written.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every aggregate over an empty input returns NULL. Only <code class=\"mm\">COUNT</code> returns 0; <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code>, <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code>, <code class=\"mm\">MIN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">MAX</code> return NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> COUNT over nothing is 0; every other aggregate over nothing is NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q32", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "On the data below, the subquery in<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT s.roll FROM student s\nWHERE s.cgpa = (SELECT e.marks FROM enroll e WHERE e.cid = 'C1');</span>returns five rows. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The statement fails at run time, because a scalar subquery used with <code class=\"mm\">=</code> must return at most one row.", "The statement returns every student whose <code class=\"mm\">cgpa</code> equals any of those five marks.", "The statement returns no rows, because a comparison against a multi-row subquery is always UNKNOWN.", "The statement is a syntax error, because a subquery may not stand on the right of <code class=\"mm\">=</code> in a <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A subquery in a position that expects one value — the right operand of <code class=\"mm\">=</code>, or a <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list item — is a scalar subquery and must produce at most one row. Producing more is a run-time cardinality error, not a silent set comparison.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The right side of <code class=\"mm\">=</code> expects a single value, so the subquery is scalar by position.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">WHERE e.cid = 'C1'</code> matches five rows, so the subquery returns five values.</li><li>A scalar subquery that returns more than one row raises an error when it is evaluated.</li><li>To compare against a set, the query must say so: <code class=\"mm\">IN</code> or <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — the statement fails at run time with a cardinality error.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code> (equivalently <code class=\"mm\">IN</code>), which is a different operator.</li><li>(C) confuses a cardinality error with three-valued logic; no comparison is even attempted.</li><li>(D) is wrong: the position is legal, only the number of rows is not.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting <code class=\"mm\">=</code> followed by a subquery to behave like <code class=\"mm\">IN</code>. It does not; <code class=\"mm\">=</code> demands exactly one value, and an empty subquery would give NULL rather than an error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Use IN or = ANY for a set; = is for one value and fails loudly on many.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q33", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, which students does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT s.roll FROM student s\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enroll e\n                  WHERE e.roll = s.roll AND e.marks > 80);</span>", "options": ["Only roll 14, the student with no row in <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> at all.", "Rolls 12, 14 and 15 — every student with no enrollment above 80, including the student who has no enrollment at all.", "Rolls 12 and 15 only, because roll 14 has no <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> row for the inner query to test.", "Rolls 12, 15 and 16."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The extra condition belongs <code class=\"mm\">inside</code> the <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> subquery, so the inner query asks \"does this student have an enrollment above 80?\" and <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> keeps the students for whom the answer is no. A student with no enrollment trivially has none above 80, so that student is kept too.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Enrollments above 80: 82 and 91 (roll 11), 95 and 88 (roll 13), 88 (roll 16).</li><li>Rolls with such an enrollment: 11, 13, 16 — these are dropped.</li><li>Roll 12 has 74 and 68; roll 15 has 74 and 60; roll 14 has no row at all. None of them has an enrollment above 80.</li><li>The result is rolls 12, 14 and 15.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — rolls 12, 14 and 15.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) reads the query as \"has no enrollment\", which is what you get if the <code class=\"mm\">marks > 80</code> condition is dropped.</li><li>(C) wrongly assumes an empty inner result is not a valid answer; it is exactly what <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is built to detect.</li><li>(D) keeps roll 16, whose 88 is above 80, so <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> is true and the row is removed.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Moving <code class=\"mm\">marks > 80</code> out of the subquery into the outer <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code>. That changes the question from \"no enrollment above 80\" to \"has no enrollment, and separately scored above 80\", and it also silently drops every student with no enrollment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Conditions that define the thing being searched for go inside the EXISTS subquery.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q34", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about subqueries are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In the conceptual model, a correlated subquery in the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list is evaluated once for each row the outer query produces.", "<code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is TRUE for an outer row whenever the inner query produces no rows, even when the inner column is nullable.", "<code class=\"mm\">IN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> always return the same rows, so one may be substituted for the other without care.", "A subquery in the <code class=\"mm\">FROM</code> clause may refer to a column of another table in the same <code class=\"mm\">FROM</code> clause."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Correlation means the inner query depends on the current outer row, so conceptually it is re-evaluated per row. <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> tests only whether a row was produced, which makes it immune to NULLs — unlike <code class=\"mm\">IN</code>, whose comparison against a NULL yields UNKNOWN. Tables in one <code class=\"mm\">FROM</code> list are combined as a set, so one cannot see the other's columns without a lateral construct, which standard SQL does not have.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True by the definition of correlation.</li><li>(B) True: <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> never compares values, only counts rows, so a NULL in the inner column cannot break it.</li><li>(C) False: they diverge as soon as the inner column can be NULL (<code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> collapses to the empty set) and when duplicate rows matter.</li><li>(D) False: standard SQL has no <code class=\"mm\">LATERAL</code>; a derived table sees only its own tables and the enclosing query.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B) are true; (C) and (D) are false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) fails on NULLs: <code class=\"mm\">x NOT IN (…, NULL)</code> is never TRUE, while <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is unaffected.</li><li>(D) describes a lateral join, a vendor extension rather than standard SQL.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">IN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> are interchangeable. They agree only when the inner column is declared <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> and the outer query needs no duplicate elimination.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> EXISTS counts rows, IN compares values; only one of the two survives NULLs.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q35", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, how many students does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s\nWHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enroll e\n              WHERE e.roll = s.roll\n                AND e.marks > (SELECT AVG(e2.marks) FROM enroll e2\n                               WHERE e2.cid = e.cid));</span>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is a doubly nested correlated query. For a candidate student the middle query walks that student's enrollments; for each of them the innermost query recomputes the average marks of <code class=\"mm\">that course</code> over its non-NULL marks. A NULL mark never satisfies <code class=\"mm\">&gt;</code>, so ungraded rows contribute nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Course averages over non-NULL marks: C1 = (82+74+95+74+88)/5 = 413/5 = 82.6; C2 = (91+88+60)/3 = 239/3 ≈ 79.67; C3 = 68/1 = 68.</li><li>Roll 11: 82 &gt; 82.6 is false, 91 &gt; 79.67 is true → the student qualifies.</li><li>Roll 12: 74 &gt; 82.6 false, 68 &gt; 68 false → does not qualify.</li><li>Roll 13: 95 &gt; 82.6 true → qualifies.</li><li>Roll 14: no enrollment rows, so the middle query produces nothing → does not qualify.</li><li>Roll 15: 74 &gt; 82.6 false, 60 &gt; 79.67 false → does not qualify.</li><li>Roll 16: 88 &gt; 82.6 true, and the C3 NULL is never above 68 → qualifies.</li><li>Qualifying students: rolls 11, 13 and 16, so the count is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. Computing one global average instead of a per-course average, and treating the NULL mark as 0 — which would make the C3 average 34 and let roll 12's 68 clear it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Resolve the innermost aggregate per group first, then compare each row against its own group's value.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q36", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, what does <code class=\"mm\">SELECT COUNT(cid), COUNT(DISTINCT cid), COUNT(*) FROM enroll;</code> return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span>", "options": ["10, 3, 10", "3, 3, 10", "10, 10, 10", "10, 3, 7"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(col)</code> counts the rows where <code class=\"mm\">col</code> is not NULL, <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(DISTINCT col)</code> counts the distinct non-NULL values, and <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts rows regardless of any value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All ten <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> rows have a non-NULL <code class=\"mm\">cid</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(cid)</code> = 10.</li><li>The distinct <code class=\"mm\">cid</code> values are C1, C2 and C3, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(DISTINCT cid)</code> = 3.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts rows, so it is 10.</li><li>The answer is (10, 3, 10).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 10, 3, 10.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) applies <code class=\"mm\">DISTINCT</code> to the first count as well.</li><li>(C) drops <code class=\"mm\">DISTINCT</code> from the second count.</li><li>(D) reports 7, which is the number of rows for the courses with at least two enrollments — irrelevant here.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(DISTINCT cid)</code> equals <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(cid)</code> because there are only three courses. Distinctness and row count are different questions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> COUNT(col) counts values, COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts distinct values, COUNT(*) counts rows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q37", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the data below, what happens to <code class=\"mm\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enroll HAVING COUNT(*) > 5;</code>?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span>", "options": ["It is rejected, because <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> requires a <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> clause.", "It is legal: the whole table is one implicit group, so it returns the single value 10.", "It is legal, and it returns all ten rows.", "It is legal, and it returns one row per course that has more than five enrollments."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A query with aggregates and no <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> has exactly one group — the whole table. <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> is a filter on groups, so it is legal without <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> and is applied to that single group. The result is one row when the condition holds and zero rows when it does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>No <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> → one group containing all ten rows.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> for that group is 10.</li><li>10 &gt; 5 is true, so the group survives and one row is produced.</li><li>The result is the single value 10 — not ten rows, because the row count is fixed by the number of groups.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — one row containing 10.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is a common belief but not the standard: <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> without <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> is well defined.</li><li>(C) confuses filtering groups with filtering rows; <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> filters rows.</li><li>(D) would need a <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY cid</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> is only meaningful with <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code>. It filters whatever groups exist, and without <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> exactly one group exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> No GROUP BY + aggregate = one implicit group; HAVING tests that one group.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q38", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Table <code class=\"mm\">t(g, v)</code> holds <code class=\"mm\">(A,1), (A,2), (NULL,3), (NULL,4), (B,5)</code>. What does <code class=\"mm\">SELECT g, COUNT(*) FROM t GROUP BY g;</code> return?", "options": ["4 rows, because each NULL is its own group.", "2 rows, because groups whose key is NULL are discarded.", "3 rows: <code class=\"mm\">A</code> with 2, <code class=\"mm\">B</code> with 1, and one NULL group with 2.", "3 rows: <code class=\"mm\">A</code> with 2, <code class=\"mm\">B</code> with 1, and one NULL group with 1."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> treats NULLs as equal to each other for the purpose of forming groups. All rows with a NULL grouping key fall into a single group, which is then reported with a NULL key. This is a special case of grouping, not of <code class=\"mm\">=</code>, under which NULL = NULL is UNKNOWN.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Partition by <code class=\"mm\">g</code>: {A,A}, {NULL,NULL}, {B}.</li><li>Counts: A → 2, NULL → 2, B → 1.</li><li>Three groups, hence three rows; the NULL group is reported, not discarded.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 3 rows, the NULL group holding 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) applies the <code class=\"mm\">=</code> semantics of NULL to grouping, which does not use it.</li><li>(B) confuses <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> with an equality predicate that could fail.</li><li>(D) counts only one of the two NULL rows.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing NULL groups vanish, or that each NULL forms its own group. Grouping collapses all NULL keys into one group.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> All NULL keys form one group; COUNT(*) then reports every row in it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q39", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, what single value does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (\n  SELECT dept FROM student\n  GROUP BY dept\n  HAVING AVG(cgpa) &gt; 8.0\n) x;</span>", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The derived table groups students by department and keeps only the departments whose average <code class=\"mm\">cgpa</code> exceeds 8.0. The outer <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> then counts the surviving groups — one row per department.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>CSE: (8.6 + 9.2 + 7.9)/3 = 25.7/3 ≈ 8.567, which is above 8.0 → kept.</li><li>ECE: (7.4 + 8.0)/2 = 7.7, which is not above 8.0 → dropped.</li><li>MECH: 6.8/1 = 6.8, not above 8.0 → dropped.</li><li>One group survives, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> = 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting students rather than departments, or rounding CSE's 8.567 down to 8.0 and then applying a strict <code class=\"mm\">&gt;</code>. The comparison is on the exact average, and 25.7/3 is strictly greater than 8.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> HAVING filters groups, so the outer count is a count of groups.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q40", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about grouping and aggregate functions are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">SELECT dept, COUNT(*) FROM student;</code> is rejected in standard SQL, because <code class=\"mm\">dept</code> is neither grouped nor aggregated.", "<code class=\"mm\">SELECT cid, COUNT(*) FROM enroll GROUP BY cid HAVING marks > 80;</code> is legal, because <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> may reference any column of the table.", "<code class=\"mm\">AVG(marks)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">SUM(marks) / COUNT(*)</code> agree when no mark is NULL, and differ when some mark is NULL.", "<code class=\"mm\">MIN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">MAX</code> ignore NULLs, so a group in which every mark is NULL yields NULL for <code class=\"mm\">MAX(marks)</code>."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> After <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code>, each output row stands for a whole group, so the select list may contain only grouped columns, aggregates and expressions built from those two. <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> is itself a condition on the group and follows the same rule. Every aggregate except <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> ignores NULL, so an all-NULL group aggregates to NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: <code class=\"mm\">dept</code> varies inside the single implicit group, so it has no single value per group.</li><li>(B) False: <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> is neither grouped nor aggregated, so it has no value per group either — the engine rejects it.</li><li>(C) True: without NULLs the two expressions are identical; with k NULLs out of n rows, <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code> divides by n − k while <code class=\"mm\">SUM/COUNT(*)</code> divides by n, so they differ.</li><li>(D) True: <code class=\"mm\">MAX</code> over an empty set of non-NULL values is NULL.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D) are true; (B) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the trap: HAVING may use aggregates and grouped columns, not arbitrary columns.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking HAVING relaxes the grouping rule because it runs after grouping. It filters groups; it does not let an ungrouped column through.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Grouped SELECT and HAVING may only use grouped columns and aggregates.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q41", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, what does <code class=\"mm\">SELECT * FROM student NATURAL JOIN enroll;</code> return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span>", "options": ["Six columns and 60 rows, because the two <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> columns are both kept.", "One <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> column (not two) and 10 rows, because <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> is the only column name the two tables share.", "One <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> column and 6 rows, one per student.", "It is rejected, because a natural join needs a declared foreign key."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A natural join is an equi-join on every column name the two tables share, with the duplicate columns merged into one. The row count is the number of matching pairs, exactly as for any inner join.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Shared column name: <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> only. So the join condition is <code class=\"mm\">s.roll = e.roll</code>.</li><li>The merged result has 4 + 3 − 1 = 6 columns.</li><li>Every one of the 10 <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> rows has a matching student, so there are 10 matching pairs.</li><li>Roll 14 has no enrollment, so it does not appear — this is an inner join.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — one roll column and 10 rows.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a cross join that keeps both copies of <code class=\"mm\">roll</code>.</li><li>(C) counts students rather than matching pairs, and would silently drop the multi-enrollment students.</li><li>(D) is false: a natural join is driven by column names, not by declared keys.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that NATURAL JOIN merges the shared column, and that it is an inner join, so the unenrolled student disappears.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NATURAL JOIN = equi-join on shared names + one merged copy of each shared column.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q42", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which statement about <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> and <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> in an inner join is TRUE?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">ON</code> carries only equality conditions; an inequality belongs in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">ON</code> is evaluated after <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code>.", "They give different results whenever a joined column contains a NULL.", "For an inner join, moving a predicate from <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> to <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> gives the same result."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For an inner join the two clauses are interchangeable, because both simply discard rows that fail the predicate. The distinction only becomes visible with an outer join, where <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> decides which pairs match but <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> runs after the NULL-extension and can remove the preserved rows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>An inner join keeps exactly the pairs for which the <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> predicate is TRUE.</li><li>A <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> predicate is also applied to those same pairs, before the result is produced.</li><li>Conjunction is commutative, so applying either predicate first leaves the same set of pairs.</li><li>Hence the two placements are equivalent for an inner join — and only for an inner join.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is false: <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> accepts any boolean expression, including inequalities and conjunctions.</li><li>(B) reverses the order; <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> determines the pairs, <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> filters afterwards.</li><li>(C) is false for inner joins; NULL behaviour is the same under either placement.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Generalising the outer-join rule to inner joins. The placement matters only when there are NULL-extended rows to lose.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Inner join: ON and WHERE are equivalent. Outer join: ON matches, WHERE prunes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q43", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the data below, what does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student a JOIN student b\n  ON a.dept = b.dept AND a.roll < b.roll;</span>", "options": ["4", "6", "9", "15"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is a self-join that pairs students within a department. The strict inequality <code class=\"mm\">a.roll &lt; b.roll</code> keeps each unordered pair once, so the count is the sum over departments of <code class=\"mm\">C(k,2)</code> for a department of k students.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>CSE: rolls 11, 13, 16 → C(3,2) = 3 pairs.</li><li>ECE: rolls 12, 15 → C(2,2) = 1 pair.</li><li>MECH: roll 14 alone → 0 pairs.</li><li>3 + 1 + 0 = 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) 6 counts the CSE pairs twice, i.e. it uses <code class=\"mm\">a.roll &lt;&gt; b.roll</code> instead of a strict order.</li><li>(C) 9 is 3 × 3, the CSE rows of the unrestricted product.</li><li>(D) 15 is C(6,2), the number of pairs ignoring the department.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">&lt;&gt;</code> instead of <code class=\"mm\">&lt;</code>, which returns each unordered pair twice.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Self-join for unordered pairs: use a strict inequality so each pair appears once.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q44", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about joins are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">NATURAL JOIN</code> joins on every column name the two tables share and keeps one copy of each shared column.", "<code class=\"mm\">CROSS JOIN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">INNER JOIN ... ON TRUE</code> produce the same row multiset.", "A <code class=\"mm\">LEFT JOIN</code> whose <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> condition matches nothing still returns every row of the left table, NULL-extended.", "A <code class=\"mm\">FULL OUTER JOIN</code> never returns more rows than the sum of the two tables' row counts."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A join with no effective condition is a Cartesian product, whether it is written <code class=\"mm\">CROSS JOIN</code> or <code class=\"mm\">INNER JOIN ... ON TRUE</code>. An outer join guarantees the preserved side is represented: an unmatched preserved row still appears, padded with NULLs. The number of rows can therefore far exceed the sum of the inputs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True — that is the definition of a natural join.</li><li>(B) True: a condition that is always TRUE restricts nothing, so the inner join degenerates to the product.</li><li>(C) True — that is exactly what the preserved side means.</li><li>(D) False: if every left row matches every right row the result has m × n rows, which exceeds m + n as soon as both sides have at least two rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C) are true; (D) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) fails on a complete many-to-many match: 3 × 3 = 9 rows from tables of 3 and 3.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming an outer join bounds the output by the input sizes. Only the unmatched rows are added; matched pairs are duplicated exactly as an inner join would duplicate them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> No condition = product; outer join guarantees the preserved side, and nothing else.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q45", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, how many rows does the following query return?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s\nLEFT JOIN enroll e ON s.roll = e.roll AND e.marks &gt; 80;</span>", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a <code class=\"mm\">LEFT JOIN</code> the <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> condition decides which pairs match; the left table is preserved regardless. The row count is (matching pairs) + (left rows with no match). Placing the right-table predicate in <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> is what keeps the unmatched students in the result.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Enrollments above 80: (11,C1,82), (11,C2,91), (13,C1,95), (13,C2,88), (16,C1,88) — 5 matching pairs.</li><li>Students with at least one such enrollment: 11, 13, 16. They contribute their 5 pairs and nothing extra.</li><li>Students 12, 14 and 15 have no enrollment above 80, so each contributes one NULL-extended row: 3 rows.</li><li>5 + 3 = 8 rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. Putting <code class=\"mm\">e.marks &gt; 80</code> in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> instead of <code class=\"mm\">ON</code>, which deletes the three NULL-extended rows and leaves 5; or counting students (6) instead of joined rows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> LEFT JOIN row count = matching pairs + unmatched left rows, with the right-side filter kept in ON.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q46", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<code class=\"mm\">CREATE VIEW high AS SELECT roll, cid, marks FROM enroll WHERE marks > 80 WITH CHECK OPTION;</code> is created, and then <code class=\"mm\">INSERT INTO high VALUES (17, 'C1', 50);</code> is executed. What happens?", "options": ["The row is inserted into <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> and is simply invisible through <code class=\"mm\">high</code>.", "The row is inserted into <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> with <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> raised to 81 by the check option.", "The insert is rejected, because the new row would not satisfy the view's <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> predicate.", "The insert is rejected, because <code class=\"mm\">high</code> is not updatable."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> turns the view's predicate into a condition on writes made through the view: a row that would fall outside the view is refused rather than inserted invisibly. Without the clause the insert would be accepted and the row would simply not appear through the view.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The view is updatable: it is a selection and projection of a single base table.</li><li>The check option requires every inserted or updated row to satisfy <code class=\"mm\">marks &gt; 80</code>.</li><li>50 does not satisfy it, so the insert fails with an error.</li><li>Nothing is written to <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — the insert is rejected.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is what happens <code class=\"mm\">without</code> the check option.</li><li>(B) invents a coercion; SQL never adjusts data to make a constraint pass.</li><li>(D) is false: this view is updatable, which is why the check option is meaningful.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> with <code class=\"mm\">WITH READ ONLY</code>. The first permits the write if the predicate holds and rejects it otherwise; the second forbids writes altogether.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> WITH CHECK OPTION makes the view's predicate a write-time constraint.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q47", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which statement about an <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> trigger on a view is TRUE?", "options": ["It fires after the row has been written to the base table.", "It runs in place of the triggering statement, which is how a non-updatable view is made writable.", "It may be declared only for <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> on a view.", "It can be attached to a base table as well as to a view."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> trigger replaces the triggering DML statement on the view. Because the view has no storage of its own, the trigger body performs the real writes on the base tables — which is exactly what makes an otherwise non-updatable view writable. It is the one trigger form that is defined on views rather than on tables.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The statement targets the view, and the view cannot store rows.</li><li>The trigger intercepts the statement and supplies its own actions on the base tables.</li><li>No automatic write happens, which is why the trigger body must perform it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a normal <code class=\"mm\">AFTER</code> trigger, which assumes the write already happened.</li><li>(C) is false: <code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code> and <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> are all allowed.</li><li>(D) is false: <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> is defined on views; base tables use <code class=\"mm\">BEFORE</code> and <code class=\"mm\">AFTER</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming an INSTEAD OF trigger also performs the original write. It replaces it, so the base-table write must be written by hand.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> INSTEAD OF = on views, replaces the statement, body does the real work.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q48", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In standard SQL, a <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> constraint whose condition contains a subquery over another table …", "options": ["is allowed, and is re-evaluated whenever the other table changes.", "is allowed, but evaluated only on <code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code>, never on <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code>.", "is not allowed: a <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> condition may reference only columns of the row being tested.", "is allowed, but silently ignored."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> constraint is a row-level predicate: it is tested against each candidate row and may mention only columns of that row. A subquery would make the constraint depend on other rows, which the standard excludes; cross-row conditions belong in a foreign key, an assertion or a trigger.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The constraint is evaluated per row, at insert and update time, with no wider context.</li><li>A subquery would need the rest of the database, including rows being changed concurrently.</li><li>The standard therefore forbids subqueries in <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code>; most engines reject the DDL outright.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — not permitted.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) would require re-checking every row of the table whenever the other table changes — not part of the model.</li><li>(B) invents a partial enforcement.</li><li>(D) is false: a rejected statement is not a silent ignore.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a CHECK can compare against another table because the syntax accepts a general condition. The permitted scope is the current row only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CHECK sees one row; anything cross-row needs a trigger, a key or an assertion.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q49", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<code class=\"mm\">CREATE VIEW cs AS SELECT roll, cgpa FROM student WHERE dept = 'CSE';</code> is created on the schema and data below, and then <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE cs SET cgpa = cgpa + 0.5;</code> is executed. How many rows of <code class=\"mm\">student</code> change?<span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded</span><span style=\"display:block;font-family:'Consolas','DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:12.9px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px;margin:9px 0;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto\">student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)</span>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This view is updatable: it selects a subset of rows and a subset of columns of one base table, so each view row maps to exactly one base row. An <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code> through the view therefore reaches the underlying rows, and the <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> in the view definition limits it to the CSE students.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The view exposes only rows with <code class=\"mm\">dept = 'CSE'</code>: rolls 11, 13 and 16.</li><li>Selection and projection of a single table preserve updatability, so the update is propagated.</li><li>The statement names no rows, so every row in the view is updated.</li><li>3 rows of <code class=\"mm\">student</code> change.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding up all six students, or assuming a view is never updatable. Only the CSE rows are visible through the view, so only they can be reached.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Updatable view = one view row ↔ one base row; an update through it hits exactly the rows the view exposes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q50", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about views, triggers and constraints are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A view whose definition uses <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> is not updatable in standard SQL.", "<code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> turns the view's predicate into a condition on every write made through the view.", "An <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> trigger fires only after the row has been written to the base table.", "A view may rename base columns, so the same base column can appear under different names in different views."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Updatability requires a one-to-one map from view rows to base rows. Grouping destroys that map because one view row summarises many base rows, so such a view is read-only. <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> adds a write-time predicate. A view is a named query, so it may rename and reorder columns freely — the name in the view is what users see.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: a grouped row is an aggregate over many base rows, so an update has no single target row.</li><li>(B) True — that is its definition.</li><li>(C) False: <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> replaces the statement; nothing has been written when it runs.</li><li>(D) True: <code class=\"mm\">SELECT roll AS id, cgpa AS score FROM student</code> is a legal view definition.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D) are true; (C) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) inverts the firing semantics: an INSTEAD OF trigger runs in place of the write, so no base-table row exists yet.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any view can be written through. Grouping, DISTINCT and unions all break the one-to-one mapping and make the view read-only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Grouped view = read-only; WITH CHECK OPTION = write-time predicate; views may rename columns.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q01", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) is subject to no constraint other than its schema. Which one of the following functional dependencies is trivial?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">B</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">AB</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">C</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">AB</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">ABC</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">A</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A functional dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> is <i>trivial</i> when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X</code>: the right-hand side is already part of the left-hand side, so the dependency holds in <i>every</i> relation with that schema, whatever the data. Triviality is a syntactic test on the two attribute sets, not a claim about what the data happens to satisfy.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>: <code class=\"mm\">B &sube; A</code> is false (B is a different attribute), so this dependency constrains the data and is non-trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C &sube; AB</code> is false, so it is non-trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; AB</code>: the right-hand side contains B, which is not in <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, so it is non-trivial. It happens to be true whenever <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> holds, but being implied is not the same as being trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">ABC &rarr; A</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &sube; ABC</code> is true, so every tuple satisfies it automatically. This one is trivial.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">ABC &rarr; A</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> forbids two tuples that agree on A and differ on B &mdash; a real constraint.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> is the ordinary kind of dependency a schema is built on.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; AB</code> is valid only when <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> is, so it is not automatic.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; AB</code> trivial because it looks obviously true. It is true only under an extra assumption; a trivial dependency needs no assumption at all. The other frequent slip is testing the left-hand side against the right-hand side instead of the other way round.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask one question only: is every attribute on the right already on the left? If yes, the dependency is trivial and tells you nothing about the data.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q02", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following is the exact condition for a relation to be in first normal form (1NF)?", "options": ["Every attribute of every tuple holds a single atomic value from the attribute's domain.", "No non-prime attribute is partially dependent on a candidate key.", "No non-prime attribute is transitively dependent on a candidate key.", "Every determinant of a non-trivial functional dependency is a superkey."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 1NF is the weakest of the normal forms and the only one that talks about the <i>shape</i> of a value rather than about dependencies. It requires the relational model's basic assumption: every cell holds one atomic value from the column's domain, so there are no repeating groups, no sets, and no nested relations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1NF = atomic values only. A cell such as <code class=\"mm\">{98111, 98122}</code> in a <code class=\"mm\">phone</code> column breaks it; the fix is one row per phone number.</li><li>Option (B) is the definition of 2NF, which presupposes 1NF and adds a constraint on partial dependencies.</li><li>Option (C) is the definition of 3NF, one step further up.</li><li>Option (D) is the definition of BCNF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is 2NF, which is a statement about non-prime attributes and keys, not about atomic values.</li><li>(C) is 3NF.</li><li>(D) is BCNF.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating 1NF as a trivial form that \"every table satisfies anyway\". A table with a comma-separated list, a repeating group, or an array column is not in 1NF, and every higher form is then undefined for it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Learn the ladder in one line each: 1NF atomic values, 2NF no partial dependency, 3NF no transitive dependency, BCNF every determinant is a superkey.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q03", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A -> B, B -> C, C -> D}. Which one of the following sets is the only candidate key of R?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A candidate key is a <i>minimal</i> attribute set whose closure is the whole relation. Compute the closure by repeatedly applying the dependencies, then check minimality: no proper subset may close to <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>. Apply <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> to get B; then <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> gives C; then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> gives D.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>: the whole relation is determined by A alone.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> is therefore a superkey, and it has no non-empty proper subset, so it is minimal.</li><li>Every set containing A, such as <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> or <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code>, is a superkey but not minimal. No set without A closes: B, C and D each determine only attributes further down the chain.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (D) contain A, so they are superkeys; they are not minimal, and a candidate key must be minimal.</li><li>(B) contains A as well, and C adds nothing: <code class=\"mm\">A</code> alone already reaches every attribute.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> because the schema \"looks like\" it needs two attributes, or missing the chain because only one dependency is applied per pass. Keep applying the rules until nothing new appears.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In a chain <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B &rarr; C &rarr; D</code> the head of the chain is the only key. Chains always collapse to their first attribute.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q04", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(X, Y, Z) satisfies the multivalued dependency X ->> Y. Which one of the following best states what this means?", "options": ["Each value of <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is associated with exactly one value of <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>.", "For each value of <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, the set of <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> values that occurs with it does not depend on which <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> values occur with it.", "Within each group of equal <code class=\"mm\">X</code> values, <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> functionally determines <code class=\"mm\">Z</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">X</code>, <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> must together form the primary key."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> in <code class=\"mm\">R(X, Y, Z)</code> says that Y and Z are <i>independent</i> given X. Formally, whenever <code class=\"mm\">(x, y&#8321;, z&#8321;)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(x, y&#8322;, z&#8322;)</code> are both in the relation, so are <code class=\"mm\">(x, y&#8321;, z&#8322;)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(x, y&#8322;, z&#8321;)</code>. The Y values repeat for every Z value and vice versa.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take one X value, say a student. The set of courses that student takes and the set of hobbies that student has are two independent facts about that student.</li><li>If the two facts are stored in one relation, every course must be paired with every hobby, or the table would wrongly suggest that a particular course goes with a particular hobby.</li><li>That forced cross-product is exactly the independence statement: given X, the Y set is the same whichever Z you look at.</li><li>So the meaning is (B); nothing here says a single Y per X, which would be the functional dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a functional dependency, a strictly stronger statement.</li><li>(C) invents a dependency inside the group that the MVD does not assert.</li><li>(D) confuses the MVD with a key constraint; keys are about uniqueness, not about independence.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">&rarr;&rarr;</code> as \"many values, therefore many-to-many\". An MVD is about two <i>independent</i> multi-valued facts sharing one determinant, which is why it needs a decomposition into two relations and not a junction table of pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> FD = one value per determinant. MVD = a set of values per determinant, independent of the remaining attributes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q05", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) is decomposed into R1(A, B) and R2(B, C). The decomposition is lossless if and only if", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> or <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; B</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> or <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; A</code> or <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "no dependency need hold: a binary decomposition is always lossless."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a decomposition into two relations, the lossless-join test is: the common attributes must functionally determine all of at least one part, i.e. the common set must be a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> or of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>. Here the common attributes are <code class=\"mm\">{B}</code>, so the condition is <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; A</code> (B is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>) or <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> (B is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Compute the intersection: <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {B}</code>.</li><li>Ask whether the intersection determines the whole of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; A</code>.</li><li>Ask whether the intersection determines the whole of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>.</li><li>If either holds, every tuple of the join is a tuple of the original relation, so the join loses nothing. If neither holds, the join can manufacture spurious tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) puts the dependencies on the wrong side: A and C are not the common attributes, so they cannot reconstruct a tuple across the join.</li><li>(B) names attributes that do not even appear together in one part.</li><li>(D) is false: <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code> with no dependencies at all can join into extra tuples.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Testing the common attribute in the wrong direction, for example accepting <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>. That dependency is about A, but A is in only one part; the join is stitched together on B.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two-way rule: intersect the two schemas, then ask whether that intersection is a key of either side. One side is enough.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q06", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Let F = {A -> B, B -> C, C -> D} be the set of dependencies of R(A, B, C, D). Which one of the following dependencies is NOT in F+?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; D</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> is the closure of F: every dependency implied by F. A dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> is implied exactly when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X&#8314;</code>, so the whole question reduces to computing attribute closures.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D}</code>: no dependency has D on its left-hand side, so nothing can be derived. <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> is therefore not implied.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; D</code> are both implied.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C, D}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code> is implied.</li><li>Only the first candidate fails, so (A) is the answer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> is not implied.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) follows by transitivity through B.</li><li>(C) follows by transitivity through C.</li><li>(D) is the transitive chain of all three dependencies.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming dependencies run backwards as well as forwards. Transitivity is one-directional: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; D</code> says nothing about <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code>. Reversing an arrow is the single most common error in closure questions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To test any dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code>, compute <code class=\"mm\">X&#8314;</code> and look for Y inside it. Never reason from the right-hand side.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q07", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) has the single candidate key {A, B} and satisfies A -> C. Which one of the following correctly explains why R is not in 2NF?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">C</code> is a prime attribute, and prime attributes may not be functionally determined by anything.", "The dependency <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> is transitive, and 2NF forbids transitive dependencies.", "<code class=\"mm\">C</code> is not determined by <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> at all, so the key cannot identify a tuple.", "<code class=\"mm\">C</code> is a non-prime attribute that is determined by <code class=\"mm\">A</code> alone, a proper subset of the candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 2NF forbids <i>partial</i> dependencies: a non-prime attribute must not depend on a proper subset of a candidate key. Here the key is <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>, so both A and B are prime and both C and D are non-prime.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Prime attributes: A and B, because they belong to the candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>.</li><li>Non-prime attributes: C and D.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> has a left-hand side <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> that is a proper subset of the key, and its right-hand side C is non-prime.</li><li>That is precisely a partial dependency, so the relation violates 2NF. The fix is to split off <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C)</code> and keep <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, B, D)</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is false: a prime attribute may perfectly well be determined by something, including by a proper subset of another key.</li><li>(B) confuses 2NF with 3NF; transitivity is the 3NF concern, and it is measured against non-prime attributes determining other non-prime attributes.</li><li>(C) is false: C <i>is</i> determined by <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>, since <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> implies <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> by augmentation.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking whether \"a part of the key determines something\" without checking that the something is non-prime. If the right-hand side is prime, the dependency is not a 2NF violation at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two conditions must both hold for a 2NF violation: the left-hand side is a proper subset of a candidate key, and the right-hand side is non-prime.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q08", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {AB -> C, C -> D, D -> A}. How many candidate keys does R have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An attribute that never appears on the right-hand side of any dependency can never be derived, so it must be present in <i>every</i> candidate key. Here only B has that property, which narrows the search to sets containing B.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Right-hand sides are C, D and A, so B never appears there: B is in every key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so B alone is not a key; test the pairs that contain B.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>. <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code>, and then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> is already satisfied &mdash; the closure is <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>. <code class=\"mm\">BC</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BD)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> gives A, and now <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> gives C, so the closure is <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>. <code class=\"mm\">BD</code> is a candidate key.</li><li>No larger set is minimal, and the three keys found are not supersets of one another. The count is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 &mdash; the candidate keys are <code class=\"mm\">AB</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BC</code> and <code class=\"mm\">BD</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first key found, or missing <code class=\"mm\">BD</code> because it needs a two-step derivation: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> first, and only then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>. Closure computation must be run to exhaustion.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Find the attributes that appear on no right-hand side &mdash; they are in every key &mdash; then grow each of them with one more attribute at a time until the closure is the full relation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q09", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about multivalued dependencies are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every functional dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> is also a multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code>.", "Every multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> is also a functional dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> is trivial exactly when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X</code> or <code class=\"mm\">X &cup; Y = R</code>.", "A relation is in 4NF exactly when, for every non-trivial multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> that holds in it, <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is a superkey."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Functional dependencies are a special case of multivalued dependencies, so the MVD class is strictly larger. Triviality and the 4NF condition are the two definitions the form is built on.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) If every X value pairs with exactly one Y value, then certainly the Y values do not vary with Z. So <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> implies <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> &mdash; true.</li><li>(B) The converse fails: a student may take several courses and have several hobbies, which is an MVD but certainly not an FD &mdash; false.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> is trivial when Y is inside X (then it says nothing) or when X and Y together exhaust the relation (then the leftover part is empty and again nothing is constrained) &mdash; true.</li><li>(D) That is exactly the definition of 4NF: every non-trivial MVD must have a superkey on its left &mdash; true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses the containment: MVDs are the weaker, more general form.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">&rarr;&rarr;</code> as a notational variant of <code class=\"mm\">&rarr;</code>. The arrow direction of the implication matters: FD implies MVD, never the other way.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 4NF is to MVDs what BCNF is to FDs: every non-trivial dependency of the relevant kind must have a superkey on the left.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q10", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Armstrong's axiom system for functional dependencies consists of three inference rules. Which of the following are among those three axioms? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Reflexivity: if <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X</code> then <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code>.", "Augmentation: if <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> then <code class=\"mm\">XZ &rarr; YZ</code> for every attribute set <code class=\"mm\">Z</code>.", "Pseudotransitivity: if <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">YW &rarr; Z</code> then <code class=\"mm\">XW &rarr; Z</code>.", "Transitivity: if <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y &rarr; Z</code> then <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Z</code>."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Armstrong's three axioms are reflexivity, augmentation and transitivity. They are <i>sound</i> (they never derive a dependency that fails in some legal instance) and <i>complete</i> (every implied dependency can be derived from them). Everything else in the literature is a derived rule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Reflexivity: a set determines its own subsets &mdash; an axiom.</li><li>Augmentation: adding the same attributes to both sides preserves the dependency &mdash; an axiom.</li><li>Transitivity: chaining two dependencies &mdash; the third axiom.</li><li>Pseudotransitivity is sound, and it can be proved from augmentation and transitivity, but it is not one of the three axioms. So (C) is not an axiom, while (A), (B) and (D) are.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is a derived rule: it follows from (A), (B) and (D) rather than being assumed.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Listing pseudotransitivity, union, decomposition or additivity as axioms. They are all valid rules, but a valid rule is not necessarily an axiom &mdash; the system needs only three assumptions to generate all the rest.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three axioms, three words: reflexivity, augmentation, transitivity. Any other named rule is derived.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q11", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {AB -> C, C -> D}. Which one of the following is the highest normal form that R satisfies?", "options": ["1NF only", "2NF", "3NF", "BCNF"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Test the forms bottom-up. Each one adds a condition, so the highest form satisfied is the last test that passes before the first that fails.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Find the keys. <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, D}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D}</code>; <code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> is the only candidate key, A and B are prime, C and D are non-prime.</li><li>1NF: every value is atomic &mdash; satisfied.</li><li>2NF: is any non-prime attribute determined by a proper subset of <code class=\"mm\">AB</code>? The only dependency with a smaller left-hand side is <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, and C is not a subset of the key. So there is no partial dependency &mdash; 2NF holds.</li><li>3NF: for <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, C is not a superkey and D is not prime, so the 3NF condition fails. The relation is not in 3NF.</li><li>BCNF fails for the same dependency, since C is not a superkey. The highest form is therefore 2NF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 2NF, and not 3NF.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is too low: 2NF is satisfied, so the relation is better than 1NF only.</li><li>(C) fails because <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> is a transitive dependency on a non-prime attribute.</li><li>(D) fails for the same reason, and BCNF is stricter than 3NF anyway.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Declaring the relation to be in 3NF because \"the key determines everything\". A relation can be 2NF and still have a non-prime attribute determining another non-prime attribute, which is exactly the 3NF violation here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Compute the keys first, then label every attribute prime or non-prime, then walk the ladder one form at a time. Skip no rung.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q12", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The tableau (chase) test is applied to a decomposition of R into R1, ..., Rn. The test declares the decomposition lossless when", "options": ["some row of the final tableau consists entirely of distinguished (a) symbols.", "every row of the final tableau contains at least one distinguished symbol.", "no row of the tableau can be changed by any further application of the dependencies.", "the union of the parts is <code class=\"mm\">R</code> and the intersection of all the parts is empty."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The chase builds a tableau with one row per decomposed relation. A cell holds the distinguished symbol <code class=\"mm\">a&#7522;</code> when the attribute <code class=\"mm\">A&#7522;</code> belongs to that relation, and a subscripted <code class=\"mm\">b</code> symbol otherwise. Dependencies are then applied until nothing changes. The decomposition is lossless exactly when one row becomes all-distinguished, because that row represents a tuple of the original relation that the join reproduces.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write one row per <code class=\"mm\">R&#7522;</code>, marking <code class=\"mm\">a&#7522;</code> in the columns that <code class=\"mm\">R&#7522;</code> contains.</li><li>Apply each dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code>: if two rows agree on all of X, make them agree on all of Y, preferring an <code class=\"mm\">a</code> symbol over a <code class=\"mm\">b</code> symbol.</li><li>Repeat to a fixed point.</li><li>If some row is all <code class=\"mm\">a</code> symbols, the join is lossless; otherwise it is lossy.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; one row must become entirely distinguished.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is too weak: scattered <code class=\"mm\">a</code> symbols across different rows prove nothing.</li><li>(C) merely says the chase has finished, which is the stopping rule, not the verdict.</li><li>(D) describes a union and an empty intersection, which is a property of the decomposition, not a lossless guarantee; it is in fact the classic lossy shape.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that a decomposition into relations whose union is <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is automatically lossless. That condition is necessary for the decomposition to make sense at all; it says nothing about whether the join reconstructs the original tuples.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Chase verdict in one line: one all-<code class=\"mm\">a</code> row means lossless, no such row means lossy.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q13", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A -> B, B -> C, C -> D}. How many attributes are there in the closure A+?", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">X&#8314;</code> is the set of all attributes determined by X. It is computed by starting with X and repeatedly adding the right-hand side of every dependency whose left-hand side is already inside the set.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start with <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> applies, so add B: <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> applies, so add C: <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> applies, so add D: <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>.</li><li>No further dependency applies, so the closure holds all 4 attributes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the attributes reached in one pass, or forgetting that the starting attribute itself belongs to its closure. <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314;</code> always contains A.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A chain <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B &rarr; C &rarr; D</code> sweeps up every attribute downstream, so the closure of the head is the whole relation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q14", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Let F = {A -> B, B -> C, A -> C} be a set of dependencies on R(A, B, C). How many functional dependencies are there in a minimal cover of F?", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A minimal (canonical) cover is an equivalent set of dependencies in which every right-hand side is a single attribute, no dependency is redundant, and no left-hand side can be shrunk. Here every right-hand side is already single, and every left-hand side is a single attribute that cannot be shrunk further.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Right-hand sides are already singletons, so there is nothing to split.</li><li>Is <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> redundant? Remove it and compute <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314;</code> from <code class=\"mm\">{B &rarr; C, A &rarr; C}</code>: the closure is <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>, which does not contain B. So it is needed.</li><li>Is <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> redundant? Remove it and the remaining set gives <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so it is needed.</li><li>Is <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> redundant? Remove it: from <code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, B &rarr; C}</code> we get <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>, which contains C, so it is redundant and can be dropped.</li><li>The minimal cover is <code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, B &rarr; C}</code>: two dependencies.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting three because the original set had three. A minimal cover is not a subset of the original in general &mdash; it is an equivalent set with no redundancy &mdash; and here the transitive dependency must go.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To minimise: make right-hand sides single, then remove each dependency in turn and test whether the rest still derives it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q15", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies the two multivalued dependencies A ->> B and A ->> C, and no functional dependency. Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in BCNF, and therefore also in 4NF.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in 4NF because <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> are its only dependencies.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is not in 4NF, because <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>; the lossless 4NF decomposition is <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, C)</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> cannot be decomposed into 4NF without loss of information."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 4NF asks that every non-trivial MVD have a superkey on its left. With no functional dependency at all, the only candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> is the full set <code class=\"mm\">ABC</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey and both MVDs violate 4NF.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Keys: no FD holds, so no proper subset determines the relation; the candidate key is <code class=\"mm\">ABC</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is non-trivial (B is not inside A, and <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B = AB &ne; ABC</code>) and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey, so 4NF fails.</li><li>The standard 4NF decomposition of <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> in <code class=\"mm\">R(X, Y, Z)</code> is <code class=\"mm\">(X, Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X, Z)</code>. Applying it gives <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, C)</code>, and the second MVD is then trivially satisfied inside those parts.</li><li>The decomposition is lossless: the common attribute A is a key of both parts (each part is a two-attribute relation in which A determines the other attribute by the MVD), so the join restores exactly the original tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is false: BCNF speaks only about FDs, so a relation with no FDs is trivially in BCNF &mdash; and that is exactly why BCNF alone cannot detect this redundancy. It is 4NF that fails.</li><li>(B) inverts the 4NF condition: having MVDs is precisely what must be checked, not a reason to pass.</li><li>(D) is false: the MVD-based decomposition above is lossless.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding \"in BCNF, therefore in 4NF\". BCNF is defined on functional dependencies only; a relation can be in BCNF and still be in 3NF-violating shape for MVDs. Every 4NF relation is in BCNF, never the reverse.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> An MVD with no FD on the same determinant is invisible to BCNF and fatal to 4NF. Split <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> into <code class=\"mm\">(X, Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X, Z)</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q16", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A relation R is in Boyce-Codd normal form (BCNF) exactly when", "options": ["every attribute is atomic and no attribute holds a set of values.", "no non-prime attribute is transitively dependent on a candidate key.", "every candidate key is a superkey.", "for every non-trivial functional dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> that holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>, <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> BCNF strengthens 3NF by dropping 3NF's escape clause. 3NF allows a dependency whose left-hand side is not a superkey provided the right-hand side is a prime attribute; BCNF allows no such exception. Every non-trivial dependency must have a superkey on its left.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take every non-trivial FD <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> that holds in the relation.</li><li>Ask whether <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is a superkey &mdash; that is, whether <code class=\"mm\">X&#8314;</code> is the whole relation.</li><li>If the answer is yes for all of them, the relation is in BCNF; a single no means it is not.</li><li>Option (D) states exactly this. (A) is 1NF, (B) is 3NF stated for non-prime attributes, and (C) is a consequence of the definition rather than the definition itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes 1NF.</li><li>(B) describes 3NF, which is weaker than BCNF.</li><li>(C) is true of every relation by definition &mdash; a candidate key is a minimal superkey &mdash; so it cannot be the BCNF test.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Quoting BCNF as \"no transitive dependency\". That is 3NF. The difference shows up precisely when a non-superkey determinant has a prime attribute on its right-hand side: 3NF permits it, BCNF does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> BCNF = every determinant of a non-trivial FD is a superkey. No exceptions, no prime-attribute escape.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q17", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A -> B, B -> C, C -> D}. Which of the following decompositions of R are lossless? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, D)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C, D)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, C, D)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code>"], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a two-way decomposition the test is short: intersect the two schemas and check whether the intersection determines one of the parts. The chase test gives the same verdict.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C) &cap; R&#8322;(B, D) = &empty;</code>. With no common attribute the join is a cartesian product, so the decomposition is lossy. (A) is out.</li><li>(B) common attribute B. <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C, D}</code>, so B is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C, D)</code>. Lossless.</li><li>(C) common attribute A. <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>, so A is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>. Lossless.</li><li>(D) common attribute C. <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, D}</code>, so C is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code>. Lossless.</li><li>Three of the four pass: (B), (C) and (D).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) shares no attribute between the two parts, which is the textbook lossy case.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Judging losslessness by \"does the decomposition look natural\" or by counting attributes. Only the intersection test decides, and a decomposition can look perfectly reasonable and still be lossy.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Intersect, then ask \"is the intersection a key of either part?\" For a chain <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B &rarr; C &rarr; D</code>, any split whose two parts share a point of the chain is lossless.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q18", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D, E) satisfies F = {AB -> C, C -> D, D -> E, E -> A}. How many candidate keys does R have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> B appears on no right-hand side, so B is in every candidate key. Every key is therefore B together with the smallest extra attribute or attributes needed to close the cycle <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C &rarr; D &rarr; E &rarr; A</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so B alone is not a key; B needs a partner.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code> &mdash; the closure is all five. <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> is already satisfied &mdash; all five. <code class=\"mm\">BC</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BD)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> &mdash; all five. <code class=\"mm\">BD</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BE)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> &mdash; all five. <code class=\"mm\">BE</code> is a key.</li><li>None of the four contains another, so all four are minimal. The count is 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 &mdash; the candidate keys are <code class=\"mm\">AB</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BC</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BD</code> and <code class=\"mm\">BE</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the cycle <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C &rarr; D &rarr; E &rarr; A</code> produces one key. A cycle lets you enter it at any point, and each entry point gives a different minimal key: that is exactly why there are four here and not one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Locate the attributes that appear on no right-hand side, pair them with each possible entry into the cycle, and keep only the pairs whose closure is the whole relation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q19", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies F = {A -> B, B -> C}. Which of the following functional dependencies are in F+? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code>"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> belongs to <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> exactly when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X&#8314;</code>. Compute the closure of each left-hand side and test containment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> then <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> holds, and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code> holds too.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> holds. It also follows directly by augmentation from <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>: C is on the right-hand side only, so nothing can be derived from it and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> fails.</li><li>Hence (A), (B) and (C) are in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>; (D) is not.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) reverses the direction of the chain; a dependency never implies its own converse.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Missing <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> because \"AB is bigger than the left-hand side of the original dependency\". Augmentation makes any left-hand side bigger and keeps the dependency valid.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Closure test, every time: grow the left-hand side until it stops growing, then check that the right-hand side is inside.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q20", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies A ->> B and A ->> C, with candidate key ABC. Which one of the following is a correct 4NF decomposition of R?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B, C)</code> &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is already in 4NF, so no decomposition is needed.", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, C)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R(X, Y, Z)</code>, the 4NF decomposition is <code class=\"mm\">(X, Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X, Z)</code>. The determinant X is kept in both parts, which is what makes the join lossless.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">ABC</code>, so the relation is not in 4NF and must be decomposed.</li><li>Applying the rule to <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> with the leftover attribute C gives <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code>.</li><li>Check the parts: in <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code> the MVD <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is trivial because <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B</code> is the whole relation; the same holds for <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> in <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code>. Both parts are in 4NF.</li><li>Check losslessness: the common attribute is A, and each part is determined by A, so the join restores exactly the original tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) leaves a non-trivial MVD with a non-superkey determinant in place, so 4NF is still violated.</li><li>(C) and (D) both drop A from one part. Joining on B or on C cannot reconstruct the pairing of Y values with Z values, so the decomposition loses information.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Decomposing like a binary many-to-many relationship, into <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(B, C)</code>. An MVD split is not a relationship split: the determinant must appear in both parts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> MVD split: keep X on both sides, put Y in one part and the remaining attributes in the other.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q21", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about 3NF and BCNF decompositions are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every relation has a decomposition into 3NF that is both lossless and dependency preserving.", "Every relation has a decomposition into BCNF that is lossless.", "Every relation has a decomposition into BCNF that is both lossless and dependency preserving.", "Every relation that is in BCNF is also in 3NF."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two guarantees are not symmetric. 3NF can always deliver both properties at once, by the synthesis algorithm. BCNF can always deliver losslessness, but may be forced to give up dependency preservation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) 3NF synthesis from a minimal cover produces relations that are lossless and preserve every dependency, adding a relation over a candidate key if none of the synthesised relations contains one &mdash; true.</li><li>(B) The BCNF decomposition algorithm splits on any violating dependency and always yields a lossless join &mdash; true.</li><li>(C) is false. The classic counterexample is <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">{AB &rarr; C, C &rarr; B}</code>: the BCNF decomposition into <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(B, C)</code> loses <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>.</li><li>(D) BCNF is strictly stronger than 3NF: its condition implies the 3NF condition, so every BCNF relation is in 3NF &mdash; true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) overstates BCNF. When the two goals conflict, BCNF wins on redundancy and loses on dependency preservation, which is why 3NF is often the practical stopping point.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing BCNF is always preferable because it is \"higher\". A BCNF decomposition that drops a dependency forces every update to be checked by an expensive join, which is often worse than the redundancy 3NF leaves behind.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 3NF = lossless + dependency preserving, always. BCNF = lossless, always; dependency preserving, only sometimes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q22", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A -> B, BC -> D} and is decomposed into R1(A, B) and R2(A, C, D). Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["The decomposition is lossless and dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is dependency preserving but lossy.", "The decomposition is lossless but not dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is both lossy and not dependency preserving."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Losslessness and dependency preservation are independent properties and must be tested separately. Losslessness is about the join reconstructing the original tuples; preservation is about being able to check every original dependency inside a single decomposed relation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Losslessness: <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {A}</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> holds, so A is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>. The join is lossless.</li><li>Dependency preservation: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> is checkable inside <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code> needs B and C together, but B appears only in <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> and C only in <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>. Neither part contains both, and no part implies it: the projection of F on <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code> is empty, and on <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> it is just <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code> is lost. The decomposition is lossless but not dependency preserving.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) fails on preservation, as shown above.</li><li>(B) fails on losslessness: the common attribute A is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>, so no spurious tuples can appear.</li><li>(D) is wrong on both counts.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a lossless decomposition automatically preserves dependencies. It does not, and this example is the smallest one that shows it: the two properties pull in different directions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Run two tests, not one. Lossless: does the intersection determine a part? Preserving: does every dependency live inside some single part?</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q23", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A -> B, B -> C, C -> D}. How many superkeys does R have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey is any attribute set that contains a candidate key. Once the candidate keys are known, counting superkeys is a counting problem on the remaining attributes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Keys: <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>, and no attribute alone or in a pair without A closes, so the only candidate key is <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code>.</li><li>Every superkey must contain A; the other three attributes B, C, D are free to be in or out.</li><li>The number of superkeys is therefore <code class=\"mm\">2&#179; = 8</code>.</li><li>Check by listing: <code class=\"mm\">{A}, {AB}, {AC}, {AD}, {ABC}, {ABD}, {ACD}, {ABCD}</code> &mdash; eight.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <code class=\"mm\">2&#8308; = 16</code> by letting A vary as well, or counting only the four subsets that are not supersets. A superkey must contain the whole candidate key, so A is not free.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With a single candidate key of size k in an n-attribute relation the superkey count is <code class=\"mm\">2^(n &minus; k)</code>; here <code class=\"mm\">2&#179; = 8</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q24", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In R(A, B, C), which one of the following multivalued dependencies is trivial?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; BC</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">B &rarr;&rarr; C</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">C &rarr;&rarr; A</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> is trivial when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X</code> or when <code class=\"mm\">X &cup; Y</code> is the whole relation. In either case nothing is left for Y to be independent of, so the dependency imposes no condition on the data.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code>: <code class=\"mm\">B &sube; A</code> is false and <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B = AB &ne; ABC</code>, so it is non-trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; BC</code>: <code class=\"mm\">BC &sube; A</code> is false, but the second clause holds, because <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; BC = ABC = R</code>. There is no leftover attribute, so the dependency is trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B &rarr;&rarr; C</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C &sube; B</code> is false and <code class=\"mm\">B &cup; C = BC &ne; ABC</code>, so it is non-trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C &rarr;&rarr; A</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &sube; C</code> is false and <code class=\"mm\">C &cup; A = AC &ne; ABC</code>, so it is non-trivial.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; BC</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A), (C) and (D) all leave a genuinely undetermined remainder, so each one constrains the data.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using only the functional-dependency rule <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X</code> and forgetting the second clause <code class=\"mm\">X &cup; Y = R</code>. That second clause is what makes <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; BC</code> trivial, and it is also why a dependency can be trivial without looking obviously so.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two triviality clauses for MVDs, one for FDs. If nothing remains outside <code class=\"mm\">X &cup; Y</code>, the dependency cannot bite.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q25", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) is decomposed into R1(A, B, C) and R2(C, D). The decomposition is lossless if", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; C</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The common attribute here is C. The decomposition is lossless when C is a superkey of at least one of the two parts: of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code>, which needs <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, or of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B, C)</code>, which would need C to determine A and B.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {C}</code>.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> holds, C is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>, so every tuple of the join is a tuple of the original relation &mdash; lossless.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> constrains A and C, both of which live inside <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>; it never relates the two parts, so it cannot make the join lossless.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; C</code> makes D a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>, not C. The common attribute must itself be the key, so this does not help.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> likewise lives inside <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> and does not involve D at all.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) and (D) are dependencies entirely inside one part; they say nothing about how the parts join.</li><li>(C) points the arrow the wrong way: it makes D determine C, so C is not the key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Accepting any dependency that mentions the common attribute. The dependency must have the common attribute on the <i>left</i> and must reach across the split.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Common attribute C, split into <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>: lossless if <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; (R&#8321; &minus; C)</code> or <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; (R&#8322; &minus; C)</code>. Here <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> is the second form.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q26", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following is NOT a valid inference rule for functional dependencies?", "options": ["Union: if <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Z</code> then <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; YZ</code>.", "Decomposition: if <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; YZ</code> then <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code>.", "Converse: if <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> then <code class=\"mm\">Y &rarr; X</code>.", "Pseudotransitivity: if <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">YW &rarr; Z</code> then <code class=\"mm\">XW &rarr; Z</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A functional dependency is a one-way statement about values: equal values on the left force equal values on the right. It never licenses the reverse. Reversing the arrow is the single most common invalid inference in this unit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Union and decomposition are derived rules that follow from Armstrong's augmentation and transitivity; both are sound.</li><li>Pseudotransitivity follows from augmentation and transitivity; it is also sound.</li><li>The converse claims that <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> implies <code class=\"mm\">Y &rarr; X</code>. Take <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B)</code> with the single tuple <code class=\"mm\">(1, 1)</code> against <code class=\"mm\">(2, 1)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> holds but <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; A</code> fails, because <code class=\"mm\">B = 1</code> maps to two different <code class=\"mm\">A</code> values.</li><li>So the converse is the invalid rule.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Union is valid: from <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Z</code>, augment and combine to get <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; YZ</code>.</li><li>(B) Decomposition is valid because a set of attributes determines each of its members.</li><li>(D) Pseudotransitivity is valid: augment <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> to <code class=\"mm\">XW &rarr; YW</code>, then transitively compose with <code class=\"mm\">YW &rarr; Z</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">&rarr;</code> as if it were an equation. Augmentation adds attributes on both sides, which looks like reversibility, but it is a completely different operation from swapping the two sides.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> You may add attributes to both sides, or chain arrows, but you may never reverse an arrow.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q27", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following statements about Armstrong's three axioms is TRUE?", "options": ["The axioms are sound but not complete, so some dependencies in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> cannot be derived from them.", "The axioms are both sound and complete: every dependency they derive holds in every relation satisfying <code class=\"mm\">F</code>, and every dependency that holds can be derived.", "The axioms generate exactly the dependencies listed in <code class=\"mm\">F</code> and never anything new.", "Completeness means the axioms never derive a dependency that does not actually hold."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two separate properties are at stake. <b>Soundness</b> means every derived dependency is genuinely implied by <code class=\"mm\">F</code>. <b>Completeness</b> means every dependency implied by <code class=\"mm\">F</code> can be derived. Armstrong's reflexivity, augmentation and transitivity are sound and complete.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Soundness: each of the three axioms is easily checked against the definition of a functional dependency.</li><li>Completeness is the harder half; it is what guarantees that closure computation by repeated application of the axioms reaches <i>all</i> of <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>.</li><li>Together they mean the closure <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> is exactly the set of derivable dependencies.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Denies completeness, which Armstrong's system has.</li><li>(C) Denies that anything new is derived, which would make transitivity useless.</li><li>(D) Describes <i>soundness</i>, not completeness — the two words are swapped.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the two words interchangeably. Soundness is \"nothing wrong gets derived\"; completeness is \"nothing right is left underived\".</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sound + complete is what lets a closure computation stand in for reasoning about <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> directly.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q28", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies F = {AB → C, C → A}. Which one of the following functional dependencies is in F⁺?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; B</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; A</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> is in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> exactly when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X&#8314;</code>. So the whole question is four small closure computations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code> — no dependency has <code class=\"mm\">B</code> alone on its left, so <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> is out.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code> — <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> needs <code class=\"mm\">B</code> as well, so <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> is out.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, A}</code> — <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> applies, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> needs <code class=\"mm\">B</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; B</code> is out.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314; = {B, C, A}</code> — <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, and now <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> is already satisfied. Hence <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; A</code> holds.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) have left-hand sides that determine nothing beyond themselves.</li><li>(C) would need <code class=\"mm\">B</code> in <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314;</code>; the only dependency producing <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, which produces <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> backwards as \"<code class=\"mm\">C</code> determines <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B</code>\". The given <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> supplies only <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To test membership, compute the closure of the left-hand side only — never reason about the arrow by analogy.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q29", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies F = {A → B, B → C}. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">F</code> is a minimal cover of itself.", "<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> is in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> is in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>.", "The dependency <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> is redundant in <code class=\"mm\">F</code>."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A minimal cover is a set of dependencies with singleton right-hand sides, no extraneous attribute on any left-hand side, and no dependency that is implied by the rest. A dependency is redundant when it survives in the closure of the others.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Both right-hand sides are already singletons, and both left-hand sides are single attributes, so no attribute can be extraneous.</li><li>Drop <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>: with only <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> left, <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> is not recoverable — it is needed.</li><li>Drop <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>: with only <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> left, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> is needed too.</li><li>Hence <code class=\"mm\">F</code> is already minimal, and by transitivity <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> holds.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> does not hold.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B) are true; (C) and (D) are false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) inverts the chain: the arrows run <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B &rarr; C</code>, never back.</li><li>(D) removing <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> leaves nothing that produces <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, so the dependency is essential.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a minimal cover must be unique. Several minimal covers can exist for the same <code class=\"mm\">F</code>; here there happens to be only one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Minimal means no redundancy, not minimum cardinality over all possible equivalent sets in every case.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q30", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A → BC, B → D, C → D}. How many functional dependencies are there in a minimal cover of F?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Building a minimal cover has three passes: split every right-hand side into singletons, remove extraneous left-hand attributes, then remove redundant dependencies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Split: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>. The set is now <code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, A &rarr; C, B &rarr; D, C &rarr; D}</code>.</li><li>No left-hand side has more than one attribute, so there is no extraneous attribute to remove.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>: without it, <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, C, D}</code> (via <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>), which does not contain <code class=\"mm\">B</code>. Needed.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>: without it, <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, D}</code> (via <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>), which does not contain <code class=\"mm\">C</code>. Needed.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>: without it, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>. Needed.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>: without it, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>. Needed.</li><li>Nothing is redundant, so the minimal cover has <b>4</b> dependencies.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3 by counting the three original dependencies. Splitting <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code> increases the count before any redundancy removal can reduce it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Split first, then prune — the two passes change the count in opposite directions, so do them in order and never skip one.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q31", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) has exactly two candidate keys, {A} and {B, C}. Which attributes of R are prime?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A</code> only", "<code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An attribute is <b>prime</b> when it belongs to at least one candidate key. It need not belong to every key, and it need not be a key on its own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> is in the candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is prime.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B</code> is in the candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is prime.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C</code> is in the same candidate key, so <code class=\"mm\">C</code> is prime.</li><li>All three attributes are prime.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) counts only the single-attribute key and forgets the composite one.</li><li>(B) and (C) each drop one member of <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code>, but membership of a key is enough on its own.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a prime attribute must be able to identify a tuple by itself. Prime means \"part of some key\", nothing more; <code class=\"mm\">B</code> alone determines nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Collect every candidate key first, then take their union — that union is exactly the set of prime attributes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q32", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {AB → C, C → D, D → B}. Which one of the following is a candidate key of R?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{B, D}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{C, D}</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A candidate key is a <i>minimal</i> superkey. First find the attributes that can never be produced by any dependency — they must appear in every key — then close each smallest candidate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> appears on no right-hand side, so every key must contain <code class=\"mm\">A</code>. <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A</code> alone is not a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AC)&#8314; = {A, C, D, B}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; B</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AD)&#8314; = {A, D, B, C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; B</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code> is a key.</li><li>The candidate keys are therefore <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code>; <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> is the only option among them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code> is a superkey but not minimal — <code class=\"mm\">C</code> is unnecessary because <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> already determines everything.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">(BD)&#8314; = {B, D}</code>: nothing determines <code class=\"mm\">A</code> or <code class=\"mm\">C</code> from <code class=\"mm\">B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D</code>.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">(CD)&#8314; = {C, D, B}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is still missing.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first superkey found. <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code> closes to everything but fails minimality, which is the whole difference between a superkey and a key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Start from the attributes that appear in no right-hand side; they are forced into every key and cut the search down immediately.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q33", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A → B, B → C, C → D, D → A}. How many superkeys does R have?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "15", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey is any attribute set whose closure is the whole relation. Count keys by finding the minimal ones first, then count every set that contains at least one of them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The dependencies form a single cycle <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B &rarr; C &rarr; D &rarr; A</code>, so from any one attribute the closure grows to all four.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C, D, A}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, D, A, B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D, A, B, C}</code>.</li><li>Every single attribute is therefore a candidate key, and no proper subset of a singleton exists.</li><li>A superkey is any non-empty set containing at least one attribute — which is every non-empty subset of a four-attribute relation.</li><li>Count: <code class=\"mm\">2&#8308; &minus; 1 = 15</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 15</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by counting only the candidate keys. The question asks for superkeys, and every set that contains a key is one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> When every attribute is a key, every non-empty subset is a superkey — the count is <code class=\"mm\">2&#8319; &minus; 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q34", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {AB → C, C → D, D → A}. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> is a candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> is a candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> is a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">D</code> is a prime attribute of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Candidate keys here are all pairs containing <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, because <code class=\"mm\">B</code> appears on no right-hand side and so must be in every key. Prime attributes are the union of all candidate keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">B</code> is never determined, so every key contains <code class=\"mm\">B</code>. <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{B}</code> is not a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code> — a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314; = {B, C, D, A}</code> — a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BD)&#8314; = {B, D, A, C}</code> — a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> is not even a superkey.</li><li>Prime attributes = <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> &cup; <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> &cup; <code class=\"mm\">{B, D}</code> = <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>; in particular <code class=\"mm\">D</code> is prime, through the key <code class=\"mm\">{B, D}</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D) are true; (C) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other option is wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">A</code> determines nothing at all: the only dependency with <code class=\"mm\">A</code> on the left is <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, which needs <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding that <code class=\"mm\">D</code> is non-prime because it sits on the right-hand side of <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. Being determined by something says nothing about prime status; only key membership does.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Enumerate candidate keys, take their union for the prime attributes, and answer superkey questions from the key list rather than from the arrows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q35", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D, E) satisfies F = {AB → C, BC → D, CD → E, DE → A}. How many superkeys does R have?", "options": ["10", "12", "13", "16"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Count candidate keys first, then count the attribute sets that contain at least one of them. Where keys overlap, inclusion&ndash;exclusion removes the double counts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>No single attribute determines all five: <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E&#8314; = {E}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D, E}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">CD &rarr; E</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314; = {B, C, D, E, A}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">CD &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">DE &rarr; A</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BDE)&#8314; = {B, D, E, A, C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">DE &rarr; A</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> is a third key — easy to miss.</li><li>No other set closes to all five: <code class=\"mm\">(CD)&#8314; = {C, D, E, A}</code> is missing <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">(AC)&#8314; = {A, C}</code>.</li><li>Count the supersets of each key: <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">2&#179; = 8</code>, <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> gives 8, <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">2&#178; = 4</code>.</li><li>Remove the double counts: sets containing both <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> number 4, both <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> number 2, both <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> number 2, and the set containing all three is counted back once.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">8 + 8 + 4 &minus; 4 &minus; 2 &minus; 2 + 1 = 13</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 13 superkeys.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 10 ignores the third key <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> altogether.</li><li>(B) 12 is what you get if the two overlapping keys are treated as disjoint and the triple overlap is dropped.</li><li>(D) 16 is every non-empty subset of the five attributes, which would need every attribute to be a key.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping after the first two keys found. <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> only closes to everything through the chain <code class=\"mm\">DE &rarr; A &rarr; C</code>, and missing it changes the answer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Superkey counts need the complete key list plus inclusion&ndash;exclusion; never count from the number of dependencies.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q36", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A relation R is in third normal form (3NF) exactly when, for every non-trivial functional dependency X → A that holds in R,", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">X</code> is a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">A</code> is a prime attribute of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">X</code> is a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>, or <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is a prime attribute of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">X</code> contains no prime attribute of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 3NF relaxes BCNF with one escape clause. BCNF demands that every non-trivial determinant be a superkey; 3NF allows a determinant that is not a superkey provided the determined attribute is prime.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The strict condition alone is the BCNF definition, so option (A) describes BCNF, not 3NF.</li><li>The prime-attribute condition alone is too weak on its own and does not state 3NF either.</li><li>The correct statement is the disjunction: for each non-trivial <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; A</code>, either <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is a superkey or <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is prime.</li><li>Because a superkey condition implies the prime condition is irrelevant once satisfied, 3NF is a strict weakening of BCNF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is exactly BCNF and is therefore too strong.</li><li>(B) ignores the superkey route entirely and would wrongly reject relations whose determinants are keys.</li><li>(D) is not part of any normal-form definition.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reciting 3NF as \"no transitive dependency\" and then applying it literally. The usable test is the disjunction above; the transitive-dependency phrasing is only an informal description.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 3NF = BCNF + the prime-attribute escape. Remember the escape, because every 3NF-but-not-BCNF question turns on it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q37", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A relation R is in 2NF but not in 3NF. Which one of the following must be TRUE?", "options": ["Some non-prime attribute is transitively dependent on a candidate key.", "Some non-prime attribute is partially dependent on a candidate key.", "Some determinant of a non-trivial dependency is not a superkey, and its right-hand side is a prime attribute.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> has at least two candidate keys."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 2NF removes partial dependencies of non-prime attributes on part of a key. 3NF additionally removes transitive dependencies of non-prime attributes on a key. Being in 2NF but not 3NF therefore means the remaining defect is transitive, not partial.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Since <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in 2NF, no non-prime attribute depends on a proper subset of a candidate key.</li><li>Since <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is not in 3NF, some non-trivial <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; A</code> has <code class=\"mm\">X</code> not a superkey and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> non-prime.</li><li>Because <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is non-prime and determined by a non-superkey, it reaches <code class=\"mm\">A</code> only through another attribute set — that is precisely a transitive dependency on a candidate key.</li><li>Hence option (A) must hold.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes a violation of 2NF, which contradicts the premise.</li><li>(C) describes 3NF-but-not-BCNF: there the right-hand side is prime, so 3NF still holds.</li><li>(D) is not forced; a relation with a single candidate key can be in 2NF and not 3NF.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading \"not in 3NF\" as \"not in 2NF\". The forms are nested — every 3NF relation is in 2NF — so a 3NF failure leaves the 2NF guarantee intact.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read a normal-form gap as a diagnosis: 2NF fixes partial dependencies, 3NF fixes transitive ones, BCNF fixes everything else.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q38", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D, E) satisfies F = {A → BC, CD → E, B → D, E → A}. How many of the five attributes of R are non-prime?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "0", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An attribute is prime when it belongs to at least one candidate key. So the only way to count non-prime attributes is to enumerate candidate keys and take the complement of their union.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D, E}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">CD &rarr; E</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">E&#8314; = {E, A, B, C, D}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{E}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314; = {B, C, D, E, A}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">CD &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(CD)&#8314; = {C, D, E, A, B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{C, D}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(CE)&#8314; = {C, E, A, B, D}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{C, E}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BE)&#8314; = {B, E, A, C, D}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{B, E}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li>Union of all candidate keys = <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D, E}</code>. Every attribute is prime, so <b>0</b> attributes are non-prime.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 0</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every relation has some non-prime attribute, so answering 1 or more without checking. Here the dependency structure is so tightly connected that each attribute sits inside some key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> When every attribute is prime, the 3NF prime-attribute escape applies to every dependency, so the relation is automatically in 3NF — a useful shortcut worth checking before a long normal-form analysis.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q39", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about second normal form are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["2NF forbids a non-prime attribute from depending on a proper subset of any candidate key.", "A relation in 1NF whose only candidate key is a single attribute is automatically in 2NF.", "Every relation in BCNF is also in 2NF.", "Every relation in 2NF is also in 3NF."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 2NF is about <i>partial</i> dependencies: a non-prime attribute must depend on a whole candidate key, never on part of one. Partial dependency can only arise when a candidate key has at least two attributes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition of 2NF, stated for every candidate key rather than just the primary key.</li><li>(B) follows because a single-attribute key has no non-empty proper subset, so no partial dependency is possible.</li><li>(C) follows because BCNF forbids every non-superkey determinant, which includes the partial ones; BCNF is the strongest of the three forms and implies the weaker two.</li><li>(D) is false: a relation can be in 2NF and still carry a transitive dependency, which is exactly what 3NF removes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C) are true; (D) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other option is wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) reverses the containment: 3NF &sub; 2NF &sub; 1NF, so 3NF implies 2NF and never the other way round.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking partial dependency against the primary key only. 2NF is defined against <i>every</i> candidate key, and a relation can be clean on one key and dirty on another.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Nested order 1NF &sub; 2NF &sub; 3NF &sub; BCNF; each step removes one specific defect.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q40", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D, E) satisfies F = {A → B, BC → D, D → E, E → C}. Which one of the following is the highest normal form that R satisfies?", "options": ["1NF only", "2NF", "3NF", "BCNF"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Test the forms from the strongest down, but you need the candidate keys first: whether a dependency is partial depends on which attributes form a key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> appears on no right-hand side, so <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is in every key. <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> is not a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AC)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D, E}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AD)&#8314; = {A, B, D, E, C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code> is a key. Likewise <code class=\"mm\">{A, E}</code> is a key.</li><li>Candidate keys are <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{A, E}</code>; the non-prime attribute is <code class=\"mm\">B</code> alone.</li><li>2NF: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> determines the non-prime <code class=\"mm\">B</code> from <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, a proper subset of the key <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>. That is a partial dependency, so 2NF fails.</li><li>Since 2NF fails, 3NF and BCNF fail too, and the highest form satisfied is 1NF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 1NF only.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) would require <code class=\"mm\">B</code> to depend on the whole of every key, but <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> gives it from <code class=\"mm\">A</code> alone.</li><li>(C) and (D) cannot hold once 2NF has failed, because the forms are nested.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Spotting that all three keys contain <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and concluding the relation is fine. A shared attribute in every key makes partial dependencies <i>more</i> likely, not less.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Find the candidate keys first; without them \"partial dependency\" is not even a well-defined test.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q41", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A decomposition of R into R₁, …, Rₙ is dependency preserving when", "options": ["the union of the projections of <code class=\"mm\">F</code> onto the parts is equivalent to <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.", "every pair of parts shares at least one attribute.", "the union of the parts is <code class=\"mm\">R</code>, so no attribute is lost.", "every part is in BCNF."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Dependency preservation is about whether the constraints can still be enforced without joining the parts back together. Each part <code class=\"mm\">R&#7522;</code> can only enforce the dependencies whose attributes all lie inside it — its projection <code class=\"mm\">F&#7522;</code> of <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Project <code class=\"mm\">F</code> onto each part: <code class=\"mm\">F&#7522;</code> contains every dependency of <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> whose attributes are all in <code class=\"mm\">R&#7522;</code>.</li><li>Take the union <code class=\"mm\">G = F&#8321; &cup; &hellip; &cup; F&#8345;</code>.</li><li>The decomposition preserves dependencies exactly when <code class=\"mm\">G&#8314; = F&#8314;</code>, i.e. when <code class=\"mm\">G</code> is equivalent to <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes a condition sometimes used for losslessness, not for dependency preservation; two parts can share an attribute and still lose a dependency.</li><li>(C) is the basic sanity condition for any decomposition at all, and is satisfied by decompositions that lose dependencies.</li><li>(D) is a normal-form property and is unrelated to whether dependencies survive.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing dependency preservation with losslessness. They are independent: a decomposition can be lossless and not preserve dependencies, or preserve them and be lossy.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Lossless = no spurious tuples on join; dependency preserving = constraints still enforceable part by part.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q42", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A → B, C → D} and is decomposed into R₁(A, B) and R₂(C, D). Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["The decomposition is lossless and dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is lossless but not dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is lossy but dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is both lossy and not dependency preserving."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two independent tests. Losslessness for a binary decomposition: the common attributes must be a superkey of at least one part. Dependency preservation: the union of the projected dependencies must cover <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = &empty;</code>. An empty intersection is never a superkey of either part, so the join test fails and the decomposition is <b>lossy</b>.</li><li>Concretely, the join of the two parts produces every combination of the <code class=\"mm\">A, B</code> pairs with the <code class=\"mm\">C, D</code> pairs, including tuples that were never in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</li><li>Projection onto <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>; projection onto <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>.</li><li>Their union is exactly <code class=\"mm\">F</code>, so both dependencies survive: the decomposition is <b>dependency preserving</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) both claim losslessness, which an empty intersection rules out.</li><li>(D) claims the dependencies are lost, but each dependency lies entirely inside one part.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a decomposition that keeps every attribute and every dependency must be lossless. Losing no attribute is not the same as losing no information.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Check the intersection first — if <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322;</code> is empty, the binary decomposition is lossy no matter how the dependencies fall.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q43", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A → B, B → C, C → D}. Which of the following decompositions of R are dependency preserving? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8323;(C, D)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, D)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, C, D)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B, C)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code>"], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For each dependency of <code class=\"mm\">F</code>, compute the closure of its left-hand side under the union of the projected dependencies. The decomposition preserves dependencies only if every dependency passes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Projections give <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> — exactly <code class=\"mm\">F</code>. Preserved.</li><li>(B) Projections give <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code> (valid since <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C, D}</code>). Under these, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, D}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> is lost.</li><li>(C) Projections give <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and, on <code class=\"mm\">A, C, D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. Under these, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> is lost.</li><li>(D) Projections give <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. All three dependencies of <code class=\"mm\">F</code> are recovered. Preserved.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (D) are dependency preserving; (B) and (C) are not.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) and (C) both split <code class=\"mm\">B</code> away from <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, so nothing inside either part can express <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Judging preservation by whether the original dependencies \"appear\" in the parts. A dependency can be implied by the projections even when it is not written down, as <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> in part (D) shows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Preservation is a closure test on the union of projections, not a visual check for identical arrows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q44", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D, E) satisfies F = {AB → C, C → D, D → E} and is decomposed into R₁(A, B, C), R₂(A, B, D) and R₃(D, E). How many of the three dependencies of F are preserved by this decomposition?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Project <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> onto each part, union the projections into <code class=\"mm\">G</code>, then test each dependency of <code class=\"mm\">F</code> by computing the left-hand closure under <code class=\"mm\">G</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Projection onto <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B, C)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> (attributes <code class=\"mm\">A, B, C</code> all present).</li><li>Projection onto <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, B, D)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; D</code>, which is valid because <code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D, E}</code> in <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</li><li>Projection onto <code class=\"mm\">R&#8323;(D, E)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">G = {AB &rarr; C, AB &rarr; D, D &rarr; E}</code>.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>: <code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314;</code> under <code class=\"mm\">G</code> = <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D, E}</code>. Preserved.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314;</code> under <code class=\"mm\">G</code> = <code class=\"mm\">{C}</code>, because no dependency in <code class=\"mm\">G</code> has <code class=\"mm\">C</code> on its left. Lost.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D&#8314;</code> under <code class=\"mm\">G</code> = <code class=\"mm\">{D, E}</code>. Preserved.</li><li>Two of the three are preserved.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> as preserved because <code class=\"mm\">C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D</code> both survive somewhere in the schema. They never appear together in a single part, so no part can enforce that dependency.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A dependency survives only if some single part contains all its attributes and the union of projections still implies it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q45", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A → B, C → D}. Which one of the following decompositions of R is lossless?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, D)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, C, D)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a binary decomposition into <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>, the decomposition is lossless exactly when <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322;</code> determines every attribute of at least one of the two parts — that is, the intersection is a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> or of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = &empty;</code>, which is never a superkey. Lossy.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = &empty;</code> again. Lossy.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {A}</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> holds, so <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> determines all of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>. <b>Lossless.</b></li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {C}</code>. Neither <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> (for <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C)</code>) nor <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; B</code> (for <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code>) holds. Lossy.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) have empty intersections, so the join reconstructs the full cross product of the parts.</li><li>(D) shares <code class=\"mm\">C</code> but <code class=\"mm\">C</code> determines only <code class=\"mm\">D</code>, which is in neither part, so nothing is pinned down.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any two parts that share an attribute join losslessly. The shared attributes must actually be a key of one of the parts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Binary losslessness = \"the intersection is a superkey of one side\". Test it with the closure of the intersection.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q46", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies the multivalued dependency A →→ B. Which one of the following multivalued dependencies is guaranteed to hold in R as well?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">B &rarr;&rarr; A</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">C &rarr;&rarr; B</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Multivalued dependencies come with a <b>complementation</b> rule: if <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; (R &minus; X &minus; Y)</code> holds too. The two right-hand sides partition what is left of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> once <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is removed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Here <code class=\"mm\">X = A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y = B</code> in <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">R &minus; X &minus; Y = {A, B, C} &minus; {A} &minus; {B} = {C}</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> follows immediately.</li><li>Intuitively, \"the set of <code class=\"mm\">B</code> values under an <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is independent of the <code class=\"mm\">C</code> values\" is exactly the same statement as \"the set of <code class=\"mm\">C</code> values is independent of the <code class=\"mm\">B</code> values\".</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the converse of the implication <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y &rArr; X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code>; an MVD does not give a functional dependency.</li><li>(C) reverses the left and right sides, which no rule permits.</li><li>(D) moves the determinant to <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, which nothing licenses.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating an MVD as a weaker functional dependency. It is a different statement — about independent sets of values — and the only guaranteed consequences are complementation, augmentation, transitivity and the trivial cases.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For every <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> there is a matching <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; (R &minus; X &minus; Y)</code>; always check the complement before concluding anything.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q47", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(X, Y, Z) is decomposed into R₁(X, Y) and R₂(X, Z). This decomposition is lossless whenever", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">Y &rarr;&rarr; Z</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "no condition is needed: every binary decomposition is lossless."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> in <code class=\"mm\">R(X, Y, Z)</code> says the <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> values and the <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> values under an <code class=\"mm\">X</code> vary independently. Storing them in separate relations <code class=\"mm\">(X, Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X, Z)</code> therefore loses nothing: the cross product on join regenerates exactly the original tuples.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> holds, then for each <code class=\"mm\">X</code> value the relation contains every combination of its <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> values with its <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> values.</li><li>Joining <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(X, Y)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(X, Z)</code> on <code class=\"mm\">X</code> regenerates exactly those combinations.</li><li>No tuple that was absent can appear, so the join is exactly <code class=\"mm\">R</code> — lossless.</li><li>This is the standard MVD decomposition used by 4NF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> is stronger than needed and would also work, but it is not the condition; the MVD is what justifies the split.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">Y &rarr;&rarr; Z</code> would justify a different split, on <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, not this one.</li><li>(D) is false: <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(X, Y)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(Z)</code> is a binary decomposition and is lossy.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing losslessness requires a functional dependency. An MVD is enough, and the 4NF decomposition depends on exactly that fact.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Lossless binary rule: the intersection is a superkey of one part <i>or</i> an MVD holds across the split.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q48", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about fourth normal form are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every relation in 4NF is also in BCNF.", "Every relation in BCNF is also in 4NF.", "Decomposing <code class=\"mm\">R</code> on a multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> into <code class=\"mm\">(X &cup; Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X &cup; (R &minus; X &minus; Y))</code> is lossless.", "4NF is defined using functional dependencies alone."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 4NF strengthens BCNF: for every non-trivial multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code>, <code class=\"mm\">X</code> must be a superkey. Since every functional dependency is also a multivalued dependency, the 4NF condition subsumes the BCNF condition.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: if every non-trivial MVD has a superkey determinant, then in particular every non-trivial FD does, because <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y &rArr; X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code>. So 4NF implies BCNF.</li><li>(B) False: a relation can have a non-trivial MVD with a non-superkey determinant while having no non-trivial FD at all.</li><li>(C) True: that split is exactly the MVD decomposition, and joining the two parts on <code class=\"mm\">X</code> regenerates <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</li><li>(D) False: 4NF is defined in terms of multivalued dependencies; using only FDs would just restate BCNF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C) are true; (B) and (D) are false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses the containment: 4NF &sub; BCNF, not the other way round.</li><li>(D) removes the very concept that distinguishes 4NF from BCNF.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking 4NF and BCNF are incomparable. They are nested, and the nesting direction matters: a relation can be in BCNF and fail 4NF, never the reverse.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Order: 1NF &sub; 2NF &sub; 3NF &sub; BCNF &sub; 4NF, each defined by a strictly stronger condition.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q49", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies the multivalued dependencies A →→ B and A →→ C. Applying the 4NF decomposition repeatedly until no non-trivial multivalued dependency with a non-superkey left-hand side remains, how many relations result?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The 4NF decomposition on <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> replaces <code class=\"mm\">R</code> with <code class=\"mm\">(X &cup; Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X &cup; (R &minus; X &minus; Y))</code>, and the process repeats on each part until every remaining MVD is trivial or has a superkey left-hand side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is non-trivial, so split on it: <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(A, C, D)</code>.</li><li>In <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code>, the restriction of <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is trivial because <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B</code> is the whole part. Done.</li><li>In <code class=\"mm\">(A, C, D)</code>, the restriction of <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> is non-trivial and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey. Split: <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(A, D)</code>.</li><li>Both remaining parts have two attributes, so every MVD in them is trivial. The process stops.</li><li>Resulting relations: <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(A, D)</code> — three relations.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2 by splitting only once on <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> and stopping. The second MVD survives inside <code class=\"mm\">(A, C, D)</code> and must be applied again.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Keep decomposing until every part is either two attributes wide or has only trivial MVDs — the count comes from the recursion, not from the number of given dependencies.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q50", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following relations is in BCNF but not in 4NF?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> and no non-trivial functional dependency.", "<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; B</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> BCNF constrains functional dependencies; 4NF constrains multivalued dependencies. A relation escapes BCNF only through a non-trivial FD with a non-superkey determinant, and escapes 4NF through a non-trivial MVD with a non-superkey determinant. To be in BCNF but not 4NF, the relation must have no offending FD but at least one offending MVD.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) With no non-trivial FD, BCNF is satisfied vacuously. But <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is non-trivial (<code class=\"mm\">B &not&sube; A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B &ne; R</code>) and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey. So 4NF fails. <b>This is the answer.</b></li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> has <code class=\"mm\">B</code> as a non-superkey determinant, so BCNF already fails.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; B</code> has <code class=\"mm\">C</code> as a non-superkey determinant (the keys are <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> and <code class=\"mm\">AC</code>), so BCNF fails.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> has <code class=\"mm\">A</code> as a non-superkey determinant, so BCNF fails.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B), (C) and (D) each contain a non-trivial FD whose determinant is not a superkey, so none of them is even in BCNF.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that a relation with no functional dependencies must be fully normalized. With no FDs it is in BCNF automatically, but an MVD alone can still leave it outside 4NF — which is precisely why 4NF exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> BCNF looks only at FDs; 4NF looks at MVDs. A relation with MVDs and no FDs is the classic BCNF-but-not-4NF case.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q01", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A transaction debits &#8377;5,000 from account <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and credits &#8377;5,000 to account <code class=\"mm\">B</code>. The debit is written to disk, then the system crashes before the credit is written. On restart the debit is undone. Which ACID property is being enforced?", "options": ["Atomicity: either all of the transaction's updates are reflected in the database or none of them is.", "Consistency: the sum of the two balances is unchanged.", "Isolation: no other transaction observed the intermediate state.", "Durability: the committed debit survives the crash."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Atomicity requires that a transaction is an indivisible unit of work: its effects are either all applied or all removed. A partially applied transaction that is then rolled back is exactly what atomicity forbids, and undoing the debit restores the all-or-nothing guarantee.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The transaction consists of two updates that must travel together: debit <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and credit <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</li><li>The debit reached the database, the credit did not, so the database held a state in which money had disappeared.</li><li>Recovery used the before-image of <code class=\"mm\">A</code> to undo the debit, leaving the database as if the transaction had never run.</li><li>Removing the partial effect is the definition of atomicity; consistency and durability are properties of the end states and of committed data respectively, and neither is what the undo enforces.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Consistency is about the integrity constraints holding before and after a complete transaction; the undo is not what preserves the sum, the completed pair of updates would be.</li><li>(C) Isolation concerns interference between concurrent transactions; no second transaction appears here.</li><li>(D) Durability concerns committed data surviving a crash. This transaction never committed, so durability has nothing to protect.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 'the system crashed and recovery fixed it' and answering durability. Durability protects work that has committed; atomicity removes work that has not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Undo of a partial transaction is the atomicity mechanism; redo of a committed transaction is the durability mechanism.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q02", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which ACID property guarantees that, once a transaction has committed, its updates survive a subsequent system crash?", "options": ["Isolation", "Consistency", "Atomicity", "Durability"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Durability is the guarantee that the effects of a committed transaction are permanent: once the commit is acknowledged, a later failure cannot erase them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The moment of interest is the commit point: before it, the transaction may be undone; after it, it must never be undone.</li><li>Durability is implemented by forcing the log records of the transaction to stable (non-volatile) storage before the commit is acknowledged, and by redoing committed work during recovery.</li><li>Hence the property that speaks about survival after a crash is durability.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Isolation is about the visibility of intermediate states to concurrent transactions.</li><li>(B) Consistency is about integrity constraints being preserved by a complete transaction.</li><li>(C) Atomicity is about all-or-nothing execution; it undoes aborted work but says nothing about committed work surviving a crash.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing atomicity with durability because both involve recovery. Recovery performs undo for atomicity and redo for durability; they are opposite halves of the same log.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Undo = atomicity, redo = durability, locks/timestamps = isolation, constraints = consistency.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q03", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following statements about the consistency property (the C of ACID) is TRUE?", "options": ["The DBMS can enforce consistency entirely on its own, without being told the application's integrity constraints.", "Consistency and isolation are the same requirement, because both concern concurrent execution.", "A transaction that starts from a state satisfying the declared integrity constraints must leave the database in a state that again satisfies them.", "A schedule is consistent only when no two transactions execute concurrently."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Consistency is a statement about states, not about execution: if a transaction runs to completion on a consistent database, the database is consistent again afterwards. The constraints themselves come from the application; the DBMS can only enforce the ones it is told about (keys, foreign keys, CHECK assertions).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Consistency is preserved by the transaction's own logic together with the declared constraints: every intermediate state may be inconsistent, but the final state must satisfy all constraints.</li><li>The other three ACID properties are mechanisms the DBMS supplies; consistency is the property the application is responsible for stating.</li><li>Therefore (C) is the accurate statement.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The DBMS cannot enforce constraints it does not know; a transfer of funds between accounts keeps the total constant only because the application says so.</li><li>(B) Isolation is about concurrent execution being equivalent to a serial order; consistency is about the end state satisfying constraints. A serializable schedule can still break a constraint if the transaction's logic is wrong.</li><li>(D) Serial execution is a scheduling notion; consistency does not require it.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating consistency as something the concurrency-control protocol delivers. A perfectly serializable schedule can still violate a business rule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Isolation, atomicity and durability are DBMS mechanisms; consistency is the application's contract, checked with the DBMS's constraint machinery.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q04", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about the ACID properties and the way a DBMS implements them are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Atomicity is implemented by undoing, from the log, the updates of a transaction that aborts.", "Durability is obtained by forcing the log records of a committed transaction to stable storage before the commit is acknowledged.", "Isolation is obtained by a concurrency-control protocol whose effect is that concurrent execution is equivalent to some serial order.", "Consistency is guaranteed by the log manager, because replaying the log restores every integrity constraint."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each of the first three letters of ACID has a standard mechanism: undo logging for atomicity, force-log-at-commit plus redo for durability, and a serializability-enforcing protocol (locking, timestamps, validation) for isolation. Consistency has no such mechanism of its own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. An aborted transaction is rolled back with the before-images in the undo part of the log.</li><li>(B) True. A commit is acknowledged only after its log records are on stable storage; otherwise a crash immediately after commit could lose the transaction.</li><li>(C) True. Isolation is defined as equivalence to a serial order, and that is precisely what 2PL or timestamp ordering enforces.</li><li>(D) False. Replaying the log restores states, not constraints: if a transaction's logic broke a constraint, replaying it reproduces the violation. Consistency needs the constraints to be declared and checked.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) is the odd one out: the log manager has no knowledge of integrity constraints, and redo/undo are value-level operations.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that recovery 'repairs' an inconsistent database. Recovery restores the last committed state; if that state was inconsistent, recovery faithfully restores the inconsistency.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Map each letter to its mechanism: A = undo, D = force + redo, I = serializability protocol, C = declared constraints plus correct transaction logic.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q05", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A DBMS uses the deferred-update technique, in which a transaction's updates reach the database only after its commit record has been written. Transaction <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> updates 6 data items and commits; its log records are on stable storage, but none of its pages has been written to the database yet. Transaction <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> updates 3 data items and aborts before commit, and none of its updates ever reached the database. After a crash and restart, how many data items must recovery process in total (redo plus undo)?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deferred update is a NO-UNDO/REDO scheme: updates are buffered and applied only after commit, so an aborted or uncommitted transaction leaves nothing in the database and needs no undo. A committed transaction whose pages were not yet written does need redo.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>T1 committed, so its effects are permanent, but its 6 pages are not yet in the database: recovery must redo all 6.</li><li>T2 aborted before commit under deferred update, so none of its 3 updates ever reached the database: there is nothing to undo, so undo contributes 0.</li><li>Total items processed = redo 6 + undo 0 = 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding 3 for T2 out of habit. Under deferred update an abort costs nothing at recovery because the update never touched the database; undo would only be needed under a steal policy or immediate update.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NO-UNDO/REDO (deferred update) means aborts are free and only committed work is redone; UNDO/NO-REDO (immediate update, no-force) means the reverse.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q06", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Two operations conflict when they belong to different transactions, access the same data item, and at least one of them is a write. Which one of the following pairs of operations does <b>NOT</b> conflict?", "options": ["r1(X) and w2(X)", "w1(X) and r2(X)", "w1(X) and w2(X)", "r1(X) and r2(X)"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Conflict is defined on three conditions together: different transactions, same data item, and at least one write. Two reads satisfy the first two conditions but fail the third, so their order can never be observed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) read-write on X from different transactions: conflicts.</li><li>(B) write-read on X: conflicts.</li><li>(C) write-write on X: conflicts.</li><li>(D) read-read on X: both operations see the same value whatever their order, so swapping them changes nothing. No conflict.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A), (B) and (C) each contain a write on the same item from two transactions, so all three are conflicting pairs.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any two operations on the same item conflict. Read-read pairs are the single exception, and that exception is what lets the precedence graph be sparse.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three conflict types: read-write, write-read, write-write. Read-read never conflicts.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q07", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following statements about conflict serializability and view serializability is TRUE?", "options": ["Every view-serializable schedule is conflict-serializable.", "Every conflict-serializable schedule is view-serializable.", "The two notions coincide for every schedule.", "A schedule whose precedence graph contains a cycle can still be conflict-serializable."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Conflict equivalence implies view equivalence, because if two schedules agree on the order of every conflicting pair they necessarily agree on who reads which version and on the final writer. The reverse implication fails, and blind writes are the standard counterexample.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Conflict equivalence compares every conflicting pair; view equivalence compares only the reads-from relation and the final write of each item.</li><li>Agreeing on more (conflicts) forces agreement on less (views), so conflict-serializable implies view-serializable.</li><li>A concrete counterexample is <code class=\"mm\">S = r1(X); w2(X); w1(X); w3(X)</code>. The conflict graph has <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code> (from <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>) and <code class=\"mm\">T2 &rarr; T1</code> (from <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code>), a cycle, so S is not conflict-serializable. Yet S is view-equivalent to the serial order <code class=\"mm\">T1, T2, T3</code>: there too <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> reads the initial value of <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> performs the final write.</li><li>Hence (B) is true and (A) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) reverses the implication; view serializability is the weaker, more permissive notion.</li><li>(C) fails because of blind-write schedules.</li><li>(D) is false by definition: an acyclic precedence graph is exactly the test for conflict serializability.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the two definitions as interchangeable because both are 'serializable'. Conflict serializability is the practical notion (testable in polynomial time); view serializability is the theoretical superset (NP-complete to test).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Conflict-serializable ⊂ view-serializable, strictly. Precedence graph acyclic ⇔ conflict-serializable.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q08", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Consider the schedule <code class=\"mm\">S = r1(A); r2(A); w1(A); w2(A)</code> of two transactions on a single data item <code class=\"mm\">A</code>. Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["S is conflict-serializable.", "S is view-serializable but not conflict-serializable.", "S is neither conflict-serializable nor view-serializable.", "S is both conflict-serializable and view-serializable."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A schedule is conflict-serializable exactly when its precedence graph is acyclic. View serializability needs the same reads-from relation and the same final writer as some serial schedule; with no blind writes involved, a cycle in the conflict graph is fatal to both.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Conflicts: <code class=\"mm\">r2(A)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">w1(A)</code> gives the edge <code class=\"mm\">T2 &rarr; T1</code>; <code class=\"mm\">r1(A)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">w2(A)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>; <code class=\"mm\">w1(A)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">w2(A)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>. The graph has a two-cycle, so S is not conflict-serializable.</li><li>Try the serial order <code class=\"mm\">T1, T2</code>: there <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads the value written by <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>, whereas in S both transactions read the initial value. Different reads-from relation.</li><li>Try the serial order <code class=\"mm\">T2, T1</code>: there <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> reads the value written by <code class=\"mm\">T2</code>, again different from S.</li><li>The final writer in S is <code class=\"mm\">T2</code>, which both serial orders also give, but the reads-from relation already rules out both, so S is not view-serializable either.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (D) fail on the two-cycle in the precedence graph.</li><li>(B) fails because no serial schedule reproduces the reads-from relation; view serializability is not rescued by the final write agreeing.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only the final writer and declaring the schedule view-serializable. View equivalence requires the reads-from relation as well.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With no blind writes, view serializability coincides with conflict serializability, so a cycle settles both at once.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q09", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about serializability are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A schedule is conflict-serializable if and only if its precedence graph is acyclic.", "Every conflict-serializable schedule is view-serializable, and every view-serializable schedule is conflict-serializable.", "Blind writes can make a schedule view-serializable even though it is not conflict-serializable.", "Every serial schedule is conflict-serializable."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The precedence (serialization) graph is the decision procedure for conflict serializability: nodes are transactions, an edge <code class=\"mm\">Ti &rarr; Tj</code> means Ti must precede Tj, and the schedule is conflict-serializable exactly when the graph is acyclic. View serializability is strictly weaker because blind writes may be reordered harmlessly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. This is the serialization-graph theorem; a topological order of an acyclic graph is the equivalent serial schedule.</li><li>(B) False. The forward implication holds, the reverse does not; view serializability is the strictly larger class.</li><li>(C) True. Example: <code class=\"mm\">w1(X) w2(X) w3(X)</code> where T1 and T2 both write blindly. The conflict graph has a cycle, yet the schedule is view-equivalent to the serial order T3, T1, T2 once the overwritten blind write of T2 is disregarded.</li><li>(D) True. A serial schedule has no conflicting operations in an interleaved order at all, so its graph has no edges and is trivially acyclic.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the only false statement: it asserts the two notions coincide, which blind-write schedules disprove.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the two definitions are equivalent because every textbook example is conflict-serializable. View serializability is a strictly larger, NP-complete-to-test class.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Acyclic precedence graph is the examinable test; blind writes are the reason view serializability is larger.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q10", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Consider the schedule <code class=\"mm\">S = r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); w2(X); r3(Y); w3(Y)</code> of three transactions over data items <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>. How many of the 3! = 6 serial schedules of T1, T2 and T3 are conflict-equivalent to S?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two schedules are conflict-equivalent when they order every conflicting pair of operations the same way. Equivalently, the set of serial schedules equivalent to S is exactly the set of topological orders of S's precedence graph.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Conflicts on <code class=\"mm\">X</code>: <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>. All three give the edge <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>.</li><li>Conflicts on <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>: none, because <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> touches only <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>.</li><li>The precedence graph is the single edge <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>; T3 is isolated, so it may be placed anywhere relative to the pair.</li><li>Topological orders: <code class=\"mm\">T1 T2 T3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T1 T3 T2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T3 T1 T2</code>. That is 3 of the 6 serial schedules.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting 2 by keeping T3 in its original position, or counting 6 by forgetting that T1 must precede T2. The count is the number of linear extensions of the partial order, which is 3!/(the constraints imposed) = 3 here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Number of conflict-equivalent serial schedules = number of topological orders of the precedence graph. An isolated node multiplies the count by its number of insertion slots.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q11", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A schedule is recoverable if, for every pair of transactions <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code>, whenever <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> reads a data item previously written by <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code>, the commit of <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> appears before the commit of <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code>. Which one of the following is TRUE about a schedule in which <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> writes <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> then reads <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> commits before <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> does?", "options": ["The schedule is recoverable, because T2 read a value that T1 had already written.", "The schedule is not recoverable, because if T1 aborts, T2 has already committed on the strength of a value that never existed and cannot be undone.", "The schedule is strict but not cascadeless.", "The schedule is cascadeless but not recoverable."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Recoverability exists so that a commit is never based on data that later turns out never to have existed. If a reader commits before its writer, the writer's abort forces an undo of committed work, which is impossible; such a schedule is therefore not recoverable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>T2 read <code class=\"mm\">X</code> written by the uncommitted T1: this is a dirty read.</li><li>T2 then committed. T1 has not committed.</li><li>If T1 now aborts, T2's committed read is invalidated, and no rollback can repair a committed transaction.</li><li>The commit order required by recoverability (writer before reader) is violated, so the schedule is not recoverable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) misreads the definition: reading a written value is not enough, the writer must commit first.</li><li>(C) A strict schedule forbids even reading or writing an uncommitted item, so this schedule is not strict.</li><li>(D) Cascadelessness is stronger than recoverability, so 'cascadeless but not recoverable' is impossible.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only that the read happened after the write. Recoverability is about the order of the two commits, not the order of the operations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Recoverable ⇔ no reader commits before the transaction it read from. Violating it means committed data may have to be undone, which no recovery manager can do.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q12", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following best describes a cascadeless (avoids-cascading-rollback) schedule?", "options": ["No transaction reads a data item written by a transaction that has not yet committed.", "No transaction overwrites a data item written by a transaction that has not yet committed.", "Every transaction acquires all of its locks before releasing any lock.", "Transactions read uncommitted data only when the writer commits immediately afterwards."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A cascadeless schedule forbids dirty reads: a read is allowed only on committed data. That single restriction means an abort can never force another transaction to roll back, because nobody depends on uncommitted values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cascading rollback happens when a reader of uncommitted data must itself be undone because the writer aborted.</li><li>Removing dirty reads removes the dependency, hence the cascade.</li><li>So cascadeless means: reads only of committed values, which is statement (A).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes a strict schedule, which forbids both reading and writing uncommitted data. Strict implies cascadeless, not the reverse.</li><li>(C) describes two-phase locking, a protocol rather than the schedule property.</li><li>(D) is not a property of schedules at all: 'commits immediately afterwards' does not remove the window in which the reader depends on an uncommitted writer.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping (A) and (B). Cascadeless restricts reads only; strict restricts reads and writes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Recoverable ⊂ cascadeless ⊂ strict. Each step adds one restriction: commit order, then no dirty reads, then no dirty reads or writes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q13", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following implications is TRUE for all schedules?", "options": ["Every recoverable schedule is cascadeless.", "Every conflict-serializable schedule is recoverable.", "Every recoverable schedule is serializable.", "Every cascadeless schedule is recoverable."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three recovery classes form a chain of strict inclusions: strict ⊂ cascadeless ⊂ recoverable. Serializability is an orthogonal notion: it constrains the order of conflicting operations, not the order of commits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cascadeless forbids dirty reads. If nobody reads uncommitted data, then whenever Tj reads from Ti, Ti has already committed, which satisfies the recoverability condition. So cascadeless ⇒ recoverable.</li><li>The converse fails: a schedule can allow a dirty read and still order the commits correctly, so recoverable does not imply cascadeless.</li><li>Serializability says nothing about commit order, so neither implies the other; a conflict-serializable schedule with T2 committing before T1 can be non-recoverable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) reverses the implication; recoverable is the weaker class.</li><li>(B) is false: conflict serializability constrains conflicting operations, not commits. Take T1 writing X, T2 reading X, T2 committing, then T1 aborting: the conflict order is T1 before T2, serializable, yet not recoverable.</li><li>(C) is false: a serializable interleaving may still be non-recoverable, and a recoverable schedule need not be serializable.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming serializable schedules are automatically recoverable. They are not; serializability and recoverability are independent axes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Memorise the chain strict ⊂ cascadeless ⊂ recoverable, and keep serializability on a separate axis.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q14", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every strict schedule is cascadeless.", "Every cascadeless schedule is strict.", "A schedule in which no transaction performs a dirty read cannot suffer cascading rollbacks.", "In a recoverable schedule, no committed transaction has read a value written by a transaction that later aborts."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Strict forbids both reading and writing uncommitted data; cascadeless forbids only reading it. The extra prohibition can only shrink the class, so strict is a subset of cascadeless. The purpose of both is to prevent one abort from forcing other transactions to roll back.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Strict forbids more, so it is contained in cascadeless.</li><li>(B) False. A schedule that allows overwriting an uncommitted value but never reads one is cascadeless and not strict.</li><li>(C) True. Cascading rollback is caused exactly by dirty reads; remove them and no abort can propagate.</li><li>(D) True. That is the definition of recoverability read contrapositively: if Tj read from Ti and committed, then Ti committed first, so Ti never aborts after Tj's commit.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) asserts the reverse containment, which the overwrite-without-read example refutes.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the direction of the inclusion. Strict is the strongest condition, so it is the smallest class.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> strict ⊂ cascadeless ⊂ recoverable; the extra power of strict comes from also banning writes over uncommitted values, which is what strict 2PL provides.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q15", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Consider the schedule: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> writes <code class=\"mm\">X</code>; <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and writes <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>; <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>; <code class=\"mm\">T4</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">Z</code>. No transaction has committed when <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> aborts. How many transactions in total must be rolled back, counting <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> itself?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Cascading rollback follows the reads-from relation transitively: every transaction that read a value produced by an aborted transaction must be undone, and so must every transaction that read a value produced by those, and so on.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code> wrote <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and aborts: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> must be rolled back.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code> read <code class=\"mm\">X</code> from the uncommitted <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> must be rolled back too (first-level cascade).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T3</code> read <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, which <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> wrote, and <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> is now being undone, so <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> must be rolled back as well (second-level cascade).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T4</code> read only <code class=\"mm\">Z</code>, which no undone transaction wrote, so <code class=\"mm\">T4</code> is unaffected.</li><li>Total rolled back: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> = 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first level and answering 2, or including T4 because it ran concurrently. Only the transitive closure of the reads-from relation from the aborted transaction is rolled back.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Cascading rollback = transitive closure over reads-from edges. A schedule with no dirty reads (cascadeless) has no such closure beyond the aborting transaction itself.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q16", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "In the two-phase locking protocol, which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["A transaction may release a lock and later acquire a new lock, provided it still holds at least one lock.", "Two-phase locking prevents deadlock.", "A transaction has a growing phase in which it only acquires locks and a shrinking phase in which it only releases locks.", "A transaction may acquire a shared lock during its shrinking phase."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two-phase locking splits each transaction's life into a growing (lock-acquisition) phase followed by a shrinking (lock-release) phase; the boundary is the lock point. Once the first unlock happens, no further lock may be granted to that transaction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Phase 1, growing: the transaction acquires locks and holds all of them; the number of locks held is non-decreasing.</li><li>Phase 2, shrinking: the transaction releases locks and acquires none; the number of locks held is non-increasing.</li><li>The instant at which the transaction holds its maximum number of locks is the lock point, and it separates the two phases.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) contradicts the protocol: any acquisition after the first release destroys the two-phase structure and the serializability guarantee.</li><li>(B) is false; 2PL can deadlock because two transactions may each hold a lock the other needs.</li><li>(D) is false for the same reason as (A): no lock of any kind may be acquired in the shrinking phase.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing 2PL removes deadlock. It guarantees conflict serializability and nothing about deadlock; deadlock avoidance needs ordering, wait-die or wound-wait.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 2PL gives serializability, not deadlock-freedom; the lock point is the moment of maximum held locks.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q17", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following is guaranteed by the <b>basic</b> two-phase locking protocol?", "options": ["Freedom from deadlock.", "Recoverability of the schedule.", "Freedom from starvation.", "Conflict serializability of the schedule."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Basic 2PL guarantees exactly one thing: every schedule it produces is conflict-serializable. It does not order commits, so it does not give recoverability; it does not order lock requests, so it can deadlock; it does not prevent repeated restarts, so it can starve a transaction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Under 2PL the lock point of each transaction defines a total order that every conflicting pair respects.</li><li>Conflicting operations are therefore ordered as they would be in the serial order of the lock points.</li><li>That is precisely conflict serializability, and it is the whole of what basic 2PL promises.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 2PL can deadlock; classic example: T1 locks X then requests Y while T2 locks Y then requests X.</li><li>(B) basic 2PL allows a transaction to release a lock before commit, so another transaction may read uncommitted data and commit first: not recoverable.</li><li>(C) a transaction may be repeatedly chosen as victim and never finish.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Crediting 2PL with more than it delivers. Strict 2PL adds cascadelessness and recoverability; rigorous 2PL adds more; neither adds deadlock-freedom.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Basic 2PL = conflict serializability only. Strict 2PL = + cascadeless/recoverable. Rigorous 2PL = + serial order by commit time.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q18", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A transaction performs the operations <code class=\"mm\">LS(X), LX(Y), U(X), LS(Z), U(Y), U(Z)</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">LS</code> is a shared lock, <code class=\"mm\">LX</code> an exclusive lock and <code class=\"mm\">U</code> an unlock. Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["The sequence obeys basic two-phase locking.", "The sequence violates two-phase locking, because a lock is acquired after a lock has been released.", "The sequence obeys basic two-phase locking but not strict two-phase locking.", "The sequence violates two-phase locking only because it holds two locks at the same time."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The test for 2PL is purely structural: scan the operation sequence and check that no lock acquisition follows any release. Holding several locks simultaneously is not a violation; it is what the growing phase looks like.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code> acquires <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, then releases <code class=\"mm\">X</code> at the third operation: the shrinking phase has begun.</li><li>The fourth operation acquires <code class=\"mm\">Z</code>, a lock taken after a release, which is exactly what 2PL forbids.</li><li>Hence the sequence is not two-phase, and therefore is neither strict nor rigorous two-phase.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (C) claim the sequence obeys basic 2PL, which the lock-after-unlock step contradicts.</li><li>(D) Holding several locks at once is legal and expected during the growing phase; the violation is the re-acquisition.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Looking only at how many locks are held. 2PL is about the shape of the sequence (all acquisitions, then all releases), not the number held at any instant.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To test 2PL, find the first unlock and check that no lock request follows it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q19", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about two-phase locking are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Basic two-phase locking produces only conflict-serializable schedules.", "Strict two-phase locking holds every exclusive lock until the transaction commits or aborts.", "Rigorous two-phase locking holds every lock, shared as well as exclusive, until the transaction commits or aborts.", "Two-phase locking guarantees that no deadlock can occur."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three variants differ only in when locks are released. Basic 2PL releases anywhere after the lock point; strict 2PL keeps all exclusive locks to the end; rigorous 2PL keeps all locks to the end. The later the release, the stronger the recovery guarantee, and none of them addresses deadlock.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. That is the serialization theorem for 2PL.</li><li>(B) True. Holding exclusive locks to commit is what makes strict 2PL cascadeless: no other transaction can read or overwrite uncommitted data.</li><li>(C) True. Rigorous 2PL extends the rule to shared locks, which makes the serial order agree with the commit order.</li><li>(D) False. 2PL is deadlock-prone by construction, since locks are held while further locks are requested.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) is the only false statement: deadlock is prevented by lock ordering, wait-die, wound-wait or timestamp ordering, not by 2PL.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the strongest variant of 2PL also removes deadlock. Stronger release rules strengthen recoverability only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> basic ⊂ strict ⊂ rigorous in terms of what they guarantee, and deadlock-freedom is in none of them.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q20", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Three transactions are shown with the full sequence of lock and unlock operations each performs on data items <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">LS</code> is a shared lock, <code class=\"mm\">LX</code> an exclusive lock and <code class=\"mm\">U</code> an unlock.<br/><code class=\"mm\">T1: LS(X), U(X), LX(X), U(X)</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">T2: LX(X), U(X)</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">T3: LX(X), LX(Y), U(X), U(Y)</code><br/>How many of the three transactions obey two-phase locking?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A transaction obeys 2PL when, scanning its operation sequence from left to right, no lock acquisition occurs after the first release. A transaction that locks once and unlocks once, or that locks everything and then releases everything, is in two-phase form.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code>: acquires <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, releases <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, then acquires <code class=\"mm\">X</code> again. A lock is requested after a release, so the growing phase is restarted: violates 2PL.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code>: acquires <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and releases it. There is a growing phase (one lock) and a shrinking phase (one unlock) with no re-acquisition: obeys 2PL.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T3</code>: acquires <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, then releases <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>. All acquisitions precede all releases: obeys 2PL.</li><li>Two of the three transactions are in two-phase form, so the answer is 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Judging T3 by how many locks it holds, or judging T1 by its last operation only. The test is positional: the first release closes the growing phase for good.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 2PL is checked by the position of the first unlock relative to every later lock request.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q21", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "In basic timestamp ordering, the read operation <code class=\"mm\">r(X)</code> of a transaction <code class=\"mm\">T</code> is rejected when", "options": ["TS(T) < RTS(X).", "TS(T) > WTS(X).", "TS(T) < WTS(X).", "T holds no lock on X."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Timestamp ordering keeps, for every data item, the largest timestamp of any transaction that has read it (RTS) and of any that has written it (WTS). A read by T must behave as though it happened at time TS(T): if a younger transaction has already written X, the read cannot be placed before that write, so T is aborted and restarted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For a read <code class=\"mm\">r(X)</code>, the rule compares <code class=\"mm\">TS(T)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">WTS(X)</code>.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>, some younger transaction has already written <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, so a read at time <code class=\"mm\">TS(T)</code> would have to see that write; the schedule cannot be rearranged to allow it, so T is rejected.</li><li>Otherwise the read proceeds and <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X)</code> is updated to the maximum of its old value and <code class=\"mm\">TS(T)</code>.</li><li>Hence the rejecting condition is <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; RTS(X)</code> is not a rejection condition: a reader older than the last reader is always allowed.</li><li>(B) a read older than nothing in particular is irrelevant; the comparison that matters is against the last write.</li><li>(D) timestamp ordering uses no locks at all; that is precisely why it cannot deadlock.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Mixing up the read rule with the write rule. Read compares against WTS; write compares against both WTS and RTS (and Thomas's rule modifies the write case only).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read rule: reject if TS(T) < WTS(X). Write rule: reject if TS(T) < WTS(X) or TS(T) < RTS(X), with Thomas's rule replacing the first rejection by ignoring the write.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q22", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Under basic timestamp ordering, a write <code class=\"mm\">w(X)</code> by <code class=\"mm\">T</code> with <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code> is rejected. Under <b>Thomas's write rule</b> the same operation is", "options": ["always rejected.", "ignored when RTS(X) ≤ TS(T), because the value it would write is already obsolete.", "executed after waiting for the younger writer to commit.", "ignored whenever TS(T) < WTS(X), whatever RTS(X) is."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Thomas's write rule observes that a write which is older than the current value of the item and which nobody has read in the meantime can never be observed: the value it produces is overwritten by the younger write before any read can see it. Discarding such a write keeps the schedule view-equivalent to a serial one and avoids an unnecessary abort.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Basic rule: if <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>, a younger transaction has already written <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, so T is aborted.</li><li>Thomas's refinement adds a second condition: if additionally <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) &le; TS(T)</code>, no transaction has read the younger version, so T's write would be invisible anyway.</li><li>In that case the write is simply skipped (an obsolete write) and T continues; only when <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) &gt; TS(T)</code> must T be aborted, because a reader has already seen a version that T's write would have to precede.</li><li>Hence Thomas's rule ignores the write when <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) &le; TS(T)</code>, which is option (B).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is the basic rule, which Thomas's rule relaxes.</li><li>(C) timestamp ordering never waits, so no operation is ever delayed for a commit.</li><li>(D) drops the RTS condition, which would discard a write that a reader has already observed.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying Thomas's rule to reads as well. It refines the write rule only; reads are still rejected whenever TS(T) < WTS(X).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Thomas's write rule = ignore obsolete writes. It makes timestamp ordering tolerate some out-of-order writes without aborting, and it is the reason the resulting schedules are view-serializable rather than conflict-serializable.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q23", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following statements about deadlock detection is TRUE?", "options": ["A cycle in the wait-for graph means that a deadlock exists, and the cycle may involve any number of transactions.", "A cycle in the wait-for graph is necessary but not sufficient for a deadlock.", "Timestamp ordering can deadlock, so it needs a wait-for graph.", "Wait-die and wound-wait can deadlock."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The wait-for graph has one node per active transaction and an edge <code class=\"mm\">Ti &rarr; Tj</code> when Ti is waiting for a lock held by Tj. A cycle is both necessary and sufficient for deadlock, because each transaction in the cycle waits for the next and none can proceed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A transaction blocked on a lock is waiting for the holder to release it.</li><li>If the wait-for edges form a cycle, every member of the cycle waits for another member, so none can ever proceed: deadlock.</li><li>If there is no cycle, the waits form a partial order and the transaction at the end of the longest chain will eventually finish.</li><li>The cycle may be of length 2 (the common case) or longer; nothing limits it to two transactions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses necessity and sufficiency: a cycle is exactly the criterion, not merely a symptom.</li><li>(C) timestamp ordering never makes a transaction wait, so it cannot deadlock and needs no wait-for graph.</li><li>(D) wait-die and wound-wait are deadlock-prevention schemes based on timestamps; one of the two transactions is always aborted rather than allowed to wait cyclically.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking only two-transaction deadlocks matter. Cycles of any length occur, and a cycle of length 3 is common with three-way lock orders.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Cycle in wait-for graph ⇔ deadlock. Detection means finding a cycle; resolution means aborting a victim in it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q24", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Basic timestamp ordering is free of deadlock because no transaction ever waits for another.", "Thomas's write rule ignores an obsolete write instead of aborting the writing transaction.", "In wait-die, an older transaction waits for a younger one, while a younger transaction that requests an item held by an older one is aborted.", "In wound-wait, an older transaction that requests an item held by a younger one is aborted."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Timestamp-based protocols avoid deadlock by never blocking: a transaction either proceeds, has its operation skipped, or is aborted and restarted. The two prevention schemes, wait-die and wound-wait, decide by age who waits and who dies, always in the same direction, which is what rules out cycles.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Timestamp ordering decides every operation immediately against RTS and WTS; a transaction is aborted rather than queued, so no wait-for edge ever exists.</li><li>(B) True. That is the definition of Thomas's write rule.</li><li>(C) True. Wait-die: the older transaction is allowed to wait for the younger; the younger is aborted (dies) if it asks for an item held by an older one.</li><li>(D) False. Wound-wait is the mirror image: the older transaction wounds (aborts) the younger holder, and the younger waits for the older.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) reverses wound-wait: in wound-wait the older preempts the younger, so an older requester is never the one aborted.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the two schemes. Mnemonic: wait-die is 'old waits, young dies'; wound-wait is 'old wounds, young waits'.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Both schemes are deadlock-free because waits always go in one direction of age; both can starve, which restarts with the original timestamp are meant to bound.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q25", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Transactions <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> start with timestamps 10, 20 and 30 respectively. Basic timestamp ordering (not Thomas's write rule) is used, and the operations below are executed in the order shown: <code class=\"mm\">1. r1(X) 2. w3(X) 3. r2(X) 4. w2(Y) 5. r3(Y) 6. w1(Y)</code>. How many transactions are aborted during this execution?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every operation is checked against the read and write timestamps of its item. A read is rejected when TS(T) < WTS(X); a write is rejected when TS(T) < WTS(X) or TS(T) < RTS(X). A rejected operation aborts its transaction, whose later operations are then not executed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">1. r1(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">WTS(X)</code> is empty, so the read is granted and <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) = 10</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">2. w3(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS(T3) = 30 &ge; WTS(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">TS(T3) = 30 &ge; RTS(X) = 10</code>, so the write is granted and <code class=\"mm\">WTS(X) = 30</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">3. r2(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS(T2) = 20 &lt; WTS(X) = 30</code>, so the read is rejected and <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> is aborted. That is 1 abort.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">4. w2(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> has already been aborted, so this operation is not executed.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">5. r3(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS(T3) = 30 &ge; WTS(Y) = 0</code>, so the read is granted and <code class=\"mm\">RTS(Y) = 30</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">6. w1(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS(T1) = 10 &ge; WTS(Y) = 0</code> but <code class=\"mm\">TS(T1) = 10 &lt; RTS(Y) = 30</code>, so the write is rejected and <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> is aborted. That is 2 aborts.</li><li>Total aborts = 2, one at step 3 and one at step 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only WTS and forgetting the read timestamp, which is what rejects the write at step 6; or executing step 4 for an already aborted T2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Simulate timestamp ordering with a table of RTS and WTS per item and check every operation against both timestamps; a rejected operation removes the transaction from the rest of the schedule.</p>"}];
